      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MeDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOU R PREVENTS INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>MeDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOU R PREVENTS INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>IN examining the faults of our neighbours nearly all of us use a microscope. The air castles of most girls are covered with orange blossoms. "Procrastination is the thief of time," arid the plunder can never be recovered. It's astonishing how much patience some people have with themselves. Happiness is getting something that you want, but didn't expect. SHE: "And what would you be now if it weren't for my money?" He : "A bachelor." WHEN a man considers himself as "one in a , thousand," he naturally regards the others as ciphers. " THAT man Smithers is a clever fellow. He can write with either hand." "Is that so? How does ho do it?" "On a typewriter!' "HAVE you seen my last song George?" asked the would-be distinguished composer. "I hope so, Billy !" was the reply. Hs (consulting tea-cup) : "Ah ! you are to be married soon." " Mercy me ! To whom?" "To use; I came to-day on purpose to tell you." Its.xmoußN "My dear feller, it is always better to begin at the bottom of the ladder." Chessney "Nonsense ! How about when you are escaping from a fire?" MISTRESS "Mary, how was it I saw you treat- ing your friends to my cake and fruit?" Mary: "1 can't tell, ma'am, for the loife of me, for I'm sure I covered the keyhole." BOBBY: "I say, Miss Silverspoons, my sister Maud's going to marry your brother Dick; but don't say anything about it, because he doesn't know it himself, yet." • MRS. STYLE: "I want a hat, but it must be in the latest style." Shopman : "Kindly take a chair, madam, and wait a, few minutes; the fashion is just changing.', DOWN TO FACTS.—He: How many voices are there in that choir at your church?" She: "About • seven." He: "Why, I had an impression that it had forty or fifty members." She: "So it has, but you asked me how many voices!" " WEBB those cough-drops beneficial :" "They worked like a charm. They have such a horrible i• taste that the children have all stopped coughing."</p>
        <p>Da. GLADE: "Do you know anybody who has a horse for sale?" Drover: "I reckon Hank</p>
        <p>Bitters has. I sold him one yesterday." t- THE BRIGHT SIDE. —"There's one consolation about issomnia ?" remarked the sufferer. "What is that ? " "While I lie awake I don't have night- mare." "I DON'T mean to reflect on you," said a wit to a man whom he had insulted. "No," was the reply, "you are not polished enough to reflect on arybody.' Fur "They say it was a perfect love match between Miss Goldand gilt and the duke." Quip: "No doubt of it, my boy. She loved rank, and he loved money." WHAT DID HE MEAN.—" YOU will be sorry for the way you have neglected me when I am silent in the tomb," said Mrs. Peck. "Think of that." " My dear," said Mr. Peck, as innocently as he could, "I cannot imagine such a thing." HE: There's a bigger nuisance than the wife who keeps her husband waiting. She: What is it He: It is the wife who gets ready first and site down in the hall calling for her husband to hurry up. HIS CONCIZSIVE WAY.—Teller: " Grimshaw is the only man of my acquaintance who invari- ably wins in an argument with a woman." Askins "How, in the name of wonder, does he do it?" Teller: "Oh, he states his side of the oasts and walks off." His HANTS.—In a Western school, not so very long ago, a little fellow was called up to read for the county superintendent, who was paying the school a visit. The boy was a good reader in all respects but one ; he gave absolutely no heed to punctuation marks. When he had finished, the superintendent asked: " Willie, where are your pauses?" Willie dropped his book and held up both hands. "Here they are, sir," he said. A MisaraDED MAN.—" Miss Scadds broke her engagement with Mr. Spudds because he sent her for a birthday present a book entitled How to he Beautiful.'" "it serves him right. He should have sent her a book called "How to Stay Beautiful.'" AN INCONSISTENT COMPLAINER. "It's tire- some," he remarked, cynically, "to see people getting so excited over a circus. It's the same old thing over and over again." "Maybe you haven't observed closely," ventured his wife. 4' Humph ! I guess I ought to know what I'm talk- ing about. I've seen every one that ever came here since I was six years old." - MISTRESS (to nurse-girl) : "Sarah, I hear that</p>
        <p>, you are seen quite often speaking to the policemen in the park. I cannot allow that." Artful • " I can't help it, ma'am. All the policemen in the , park admire the baby so much—they will stop the pera.mbulator to speak to him. They all say that he's the handsomest baby that comes into the park." GOING ONE BETTER. —ln a certain manufacturing town it was a common thing, not many years ago, for skilled workmen to save sufficient money wherewith to build houses for themselves. A great deal of rivalry existed among these men as to who should have the best house, with sometimes curious architectural results. A and B were two rivals. A having built a house, B, whose turn soon afterwards came, determined to outdo him. So he called in a well-known architect to prepare .- plans. Asked what aspect he would like to his house, B, scratching his head, inquired : " Aspect ! What's that? Has A got one?" "Why, of course," said the architect; "he couldn't pos. "Then put me on two of 'em." "Do you see that thing on the wall there? Above the clock? That thing with ribbons on it?" " Yes; what is it?" "That's a match receiver. It is intended to receive burned matches. I extinguish the match and put it on the table. Then I go across the street and burrow a step- ladder. I bring the step-ladder into this room, move the sofa away from the wall, plant my step- ladder and carry that piece of burned match up the ladder, and drop it into the receiver. Then I come down from the ladder, put the sofa back in its place, take the step-ladder home, and there you are. I tell you it's a great thing to have these handy little articles around the house. A certain fat lady resolved to consult a physician about her corpulence. She had had no previous experience with " banting " of any sort. The doctor drew up a careful dietary for her. She must eat dry toast, plain boiled beef, and a few other things of the same lean sort, and in a month return and report the result to the doctor. At the end of the time the lady came, and was so stout that she could hardly get through the door. Toe doctor was aghast. "Did you eat what I told you?" he asked. " Religiously, ' she answered. His brow wrinkled in perplexity. Suddenly he had a flash of inspiration. "Did you eat any- thing else?" he asked. " Why, I ate my ordinary meals," said the lady. NOT - a SURGICAL INSTRUMENT. —There had been a surgical operation in the house, which was happily over. Some time after the doctor had gone the mistress of the establishment discovered on the table what she took to he one of the surgical instruments, inadvertently left behind. She firat carefully cleansed the polished steel with a solution of carbolic acid and water, then rubbed it dry with a flannel cloth, wrapped it up, and sent a servant with it to the doctor. With it she sent this note : _"Dear Doctor,—When you were at the house yesterday, you were so unfortunate as to mislay one of your surgical instruments. I have taken good care of it, and return it by the messenger. I am, very truly,". etc. Before long the messenger came back with the same parcel, a little rumpled by opening, and with it brought this note from the doctor:—" Dear Mrs. X.,—l thank you for your thoughtfulness, but there is evidently a mistake somewhere. The instrument does not belong to me. I would suggest that you ask your son about Very truly yours," etc. Full of astonishment, the lady went to her sixteen.year-old son. "Do 7ou know what this is?" she asked. "Why, yes That's the pump that I fill my pneumatic tyres with I" he said.</p>
        <p>FEMALE</p>
        <p>AILMENTS</p>
        <p>Irregularities and Obstructions however obstinate quickly and Surely relieved and removed in a few pours, after all else fails, this remedy acts as magic. Full particulars, testimonials and proofs will be sent</p>
        <p>on receipt of stamped envelope.</p>
        <p>Madam MARI YN, 20, EishopagateiWitnout, London. Retabllidied 30 Years.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT." Princess Ena of Battenbeig was the recipient of many handsome presents on the anniversary of her birthday, including one trom the Empress Frederick who is much attached to her little niece. The Princess, who has now completed her eleventh year,grows a very charming girl, and is quite a little companion CO the Queen and her mother. She is usually dressed very simply, in white, cream, grey, or pale heliotrope, not having yet discarded mourning for her father. • The Queen's children, as everyone knows, were brought up in a very simple, homely fashion, and Princess Henry of Battenberg hallowed in her Royal mother's footsteps, as far as ;dose ble. To keep to the " rules "however, has not always b_en an easy matter. The Queen is much given to petting her grandchildren, and takes the keenest delight in devising" treats," un- dreamt of in their parents nursery days. Of the two young Buileiaa Princesses, destined to make their debut in society early next year, little is known in this country. The Grand Duchess Valdi- mirovna, daughter of the elde3t uncle of the Czer, was born in January 1882, and enjoys the reputation of be- ing remarkably clever. The Grand Duchess Olga Alexandroyna, is the younger sister of the Czar, and, of course, a niece of the Princess of Wales. She will be eighteen in June next, and is probably the wealtiest marriageable Princess in the world. She is said to be the only member of the Imperial Family who resembles the Empress Marie in appearance. The Czarina whowas so long known to tho world as the Empress Marie, was a Princess of Heese Darnstadr, and therefore a great aunt of the present Empress. During the later years of her life, her relations with Great Britain became closer, owing to the marriage of her only daughter to the Duke of Edinburgh, now the Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha. The Duchess of Edinburgh was devoted to both her parents, and as a young married woman, stayed a portion of each year in Ruesia where she attended her mother in her last illness. Nothing has been heard of the Dowager Duchess of Sutherland's jewels, and it seems scercely probable now that they will ever be recovered. The Duchess is a woman of striking appearance, an decisive tempera- , ment, and possesses the most execrable taste in dress. I She is an American by birth, and married when quite young, a Mr. Blair, who died of a gunshot wound. She married next, the late Duke of Sutherland in 1889—a little more than three months after the death of his first wife—and at his death three years later, there ensued the costly and sensational litigation, between the pre- sent Duke and his stepmother. Sir Albert Rollit was the friend and adviser of the Duchess during these troubloue times, and two years ago her grace made tier third matrimonial venture, and she and Sir Albert were married in London at St. George's, Hanorer-square. The Duke and Duchess of Sutherland are now at 'Tren- tham, the family seat in Staffordshire, which is famous for its fine gardens and terraces, and i's beautiful lakes and woods. Since her husband's succession to the Duke- dom the youthful Duchess has identified herself with his interest!, his province, and his people, and has brought her own cultivated modern views of life to bear on the surroundings of her situation. The Duchess is devoted to her family, and both at Dunrobin and Trentham, tte circle of guests often takes the form of a house paity, the " outsiders " being geeerally intimate friends or some brilliant lights in statesmanship and literaeure. Lady Garvagh, who is now quite recovered from her recent somewhat severe indisposition, is a Dane,thetigh French by extraction. She is very tall, with a queenly dignity and natural grace that is as rare as it is delight- ful. Quite independent of her excessively handsome features, there is a fascination in voice and gesture that captivates este at once. Her hair is a rich brown that the sun turns into subtle shades of golden bronze,which tone off into auburn. Her eyes, almond in shape and dark in colour are most expressive. Mr. Otto Goldschmidt has presented Methane Albani with an exquisite engraving of his late wife, Jenny Lind, as a souvenir of the Leeds Festival whereat the prima donna sang the chief music in his new Ode. Many were the bonds of sympathy between Madame Albani and the great Swedish artiste, the foremost being their special devotion to Her Majesty woo extended to both artistes the honour of her personal friendship. Mr. Otto Goldsehmidt is a most genial and kind hearted old gentleman. He does not play much nowadays, but when be does it is delightful to hear him, for the beauty and delicacy of his touch on the piano are as remarkab'e SS ever. - The recent exhibition of Welsh industries in Lon- don, opened by the Duchess of Wellington, was a great success. The display of tweeds was exceptionally fine, the embroideries toe were delightful, and so far as the less decorative, but equally important item of apparel— viz flannel—was concerned, this left abselutely nothing to be desired. The Duchess—a very sweet looking woman—was quietly dressed in a claret coloured co:- tume. with grey felt hat, adorned with long ostrich feathers. Fur hats trimmed with lace, and feathers, will be quite features of dress a little later on. Fur is beiog made up into all the new shapes for this purpose, bit the best are costly, and quits beyond a purse of moder- ate dimensions. It is possible though, with a little care and perseverence, to build a hat ot this description at about one third the price asked by fashionable milliners Chinchilla skins, at certain shops, can be had for abeus three or four shillings each, minis for even less, and given a becoming shape, and a careful study of some well put together headgear, the task of maniputating an up-to-date hat or togue, should not be a difficult one. Evening gowns are being built high in the neck, but with abort sleeves. Last winter this order of things was reversed. We kept our arms encased in long sleeves, while our necks were cut low. Those who object to the high neck, are having their bodices cut square or V shaped, tolerably low, and then filled in with lace, folded chiffon, net, or sequined tulle. Where dressmaking and millinery is done at home, it is a great boon when a room can be set apart for this particular pursuit. The constant " clearing up" pro- cess, unavoidable when a family sitting-room is taken possession of for dressmaking purposes, is not conducive to a happy frame of mind, or success in one's work An attic is often avallable,and this with a little contrivance, makss a capital sewing room. It is well out of the way, and there is therefore less likelihood of things b.ing dis- turbed than would be the case, if pearr the apartments frequented by memhers of the household. Ales the sewing machine, among the necessary arti- cles such a room should contain is a large carting table. One of those old-fashioned dining tables with the side leaves that let down answers capitally, and may often be picked up at olefurniture shops, or sales, for a mere song. A chest of drawers that, in the course of years. has worked its way upwards,is an invaluable receptacle, In which to stow away the thousand and one things, needed by the home dressmaker, so is an old ottoman. and a wardrobe. Then, there should be a comfortable chair or two, some sharp scissors, and, last hut by no means least, a goodly supply of dust sheets that ean be thrown over the work when leaving it. Is there anywhere I wonder an enlightened people which walks as badly as we do ? One maiden stride., another minces, a third waddles ! The art of walking needs to tee taught, and teachere would do well to more carefully instil into the children a to-di y,how to scsom- plieh the feat gracefully. A long corridor with a mirror at the end, would be a useful accessory surely, in a school. Here is the recipe for a delicious sweet e—Reat up two eggs, add two ounces of castor sugar and half a pint of milk. Pour into a jug and stand in a saucepan of bell- ing water, stirring well, until it thicheni. Dissolve a quarter of an ounce- of gelatine, which has been pr e- vinusly soaked in cold water for two hours, in it, fievou with vanilla, pour into a wetted mould, and turn ou when set. •</p>
        <p>We Guarantee Satisfaction! Sufferers from Gravel. Lumbago. Piles. Pains in the Back Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint. Diseases of Kidneys Bladder, Stone. Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout will find a Positive Cure in HoldtOyd's Gravel Pills., Try a small box and if not satisfied ynar money will .he returned. Price Is ltd.. of all Chemists, or post free for 15 stamps. from HOLDROYD'S. Medical Hall, Cleckheaton, Yorks. Sold at 3d, Bridge-street, Warrington. Don't be put off. If You can- not get them write to the Proprietor and a box will be sent next post.—Adyt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY Dzen</p>
        <p>Richly embroidered crowns are to continue to decorate hats and toques, and some of these sur- pass all previous efforts of elaboration. Gold and silver sequins are thickly sewn on satin in white or cream colour, yellow, orange, or some pale tone of green, blue, mauve, and pink. Or perhaps the foundation material may be velvet in any of the above-named colours, or in black or dark blue. Both hats and toques are often raised extremely high at the left side, a style that has its advan- tages on the score of picturesqueness. But the Trelawny hats is the reigning favourite in head- gear. It is found much too becoming to part with.</p>
        <p>A long feather is upon on either side, the stems being hidden at their point of part. fog by a large buckle in silver, Steel, pr jet, and the ends of the feathers fall over the hair. It is more often seen in black than in colours, though in white, with a mother-o'-pearl buckle, it is worn at weddings, and is invariably pronounced success: ful. As to toques, they are large—comparatively speaking—and are made for the most part of quilled velvet as to the brim, and the same material, embroidered and arranged in full loose folds, for the crown. Fur brims and velvet crowns are often seen, as are velvet brims and crowns composed of closelY.massed ostrich ferthere, The tricorn hat—it is also known as the tiappho—is only becoming to the few, as its sharp turn up. wards from the face is trying, but it is decidedly smart and looks its best in black or dark-coloured velvet, with a narrow fur or jet edging and full plumes of feathers.</p>
        <p>Many ...nter garments, and more especially those in the fashionable cloth of neutral tint and dull as to surface, are now adorned with grouped lines of narrow braid, or with many rows of waved or straight stitching, which not only form outlines for edging the garment, but also define the curves and seams. Backs are, indeed, just now most characteristic, and this is particularly noticeable in the short coats which accompany skirts. A necessary and fashionable cape is of black crepon, with a frill at the edge, which is continued up the front tojortu a watertall jabot. It is lined through with gaiit.fd silk, and nas a pinked-out frill of silk inside the edge, which also forms ruffle at the throat, taL!.sed collar.</p>
        <p>The appallingly tight aspect which every self- respecting skirt must wear is another feature of the moment. The flounced shirt has died the death, but in its place is the eel skin sheath of twenty years ago, fitting the hips without a wrinkle, sometimes plain at the back, with the placket hole in front, a little to the left, or with a row of buttons clown the centre seam, but guiltless of pleat or gather. From below the knee it widens out into voluminous clinging folds, necessitating as much attention to one's steps when walking as at a dancing class; so that the beholder is in ,doubt whether infirm old age has suddenly descended on the victim to fashion, or whether she is practising an improvement on the careless deportment of the present day. There are several styles of coat which promise to be equally in vogue this winter. First, there is the tight, swallow-tailed type, cut on the lines of a gentlemen's morning coat with rounded fronts, and a single row of buttons. At present this is' oftenest seen in tweed and cloth coat-and-skirt cos- tumes, but some of the newest sealskin and astra- chan jackets are of this shape, which is, it must be confessed, neither pretty nor becoming. The sleeves of jackets are quit' devoid of fulness at the shoulder, and the revers are " stepped" like the lapels of a masculine coat. Aother stylish shape has an extremely short basque and four rows of very small buttons down the front. Your loving JTSNIL • Tuesda:</p>
        <p>• At a coroner's inquest, held at Liverliool on _ Ay, it transpired that the deceased, a middle.aged man, named Barnes, committed suicide whilst insane. He suffered from religious mania, and, remarking that he would never be for-. given fot praying for himself and not also brothers, rushed out of the house and jumped into the canal.</p>
