      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>COUNTY WATER P-6-LO CHAMPIONSHIP</head>
        <p>COUNTY WATER P-6-LO CHAMPIONSHIP</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE v. LEICESTERSHIRE.—RE- PLAYED FINAL. This anal for the county championship honeurs replayed at Leicester on Tuesday night. The prarieue encounter ended in a draw, each side scoring a goal. Leleesterahire were strongly represented, but Lancashire were without Derbyshire. The game waned fast, both goals being visited in turn, but not till cloee 019 half- time was a point scored, when Jarvis. receiving a pass from Pratt, gave his side ths lead. In the second half Lancashire pressed severely, and Hodgson drew level. Ripley shortly after put hia side aheasl. Then Pratt scored for Leicester, but in the last flute Hodgson again heat Tabberer, the game ending—Lancashire, 3 goals; Leicestershire, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 4 1898</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 4 1898</p>
        <p>(All rights reserved.) THE</p>
        <p>glint</p>
        <p>Mar]</p>
        <p>torrents of rain far :Imre naturally Chau anything I have heard in the best theatres.</p>
        <p>We put our fingers in our ears while the rattles and drums were going and the women and girls screamed. A dozen men threw cornmeal on the more vicious snakes, and then fanned them till they coiled up. Those which darted out of bounds with the dreaded "zaz!" were seized by the back of their heads and placed under a buffalo skin. "This is uncanny," observed Mark in an under- tone to me, "especially as it is only the curtain- raiser to a tragedy." "Be quiet," I rebuked, "the old chief's can int. Holloa I Pecos an' Jip are missing." "Oh, they have gone hunting rabbits," answered ..ark somewhat evasively. "Look!" exclaimed Lucia, hardly able to govern her excitement; "they are seizing the vicious reptiles, and throwing them into the circle." "Now, watch," I added, as all eyes were strained on the hazardous rite, "the wunaen are covering the creatures wi' corn-meal. At the priest's signal men will grab the reptiles, rush wi' them to the ' nearest rock, an' scatter them aboot in a' direc- tions." The words had hardly escaped my lips when this was done, and shortly the snake-runners came back and swallowed a very bitter draught given by the medicine man. Within five minutes Ms patients resembled seasick passengers, and vomited with the most ludicrous contortions. They received further treatment to neutralise the poison from venomous bites, and the old chief afterwards explained to me that prior to the ceremony those who handled the reptiles took a strong narcotic. The strange proceedings over, the company gave themselves up to feasting and merriment, Mark and his immediate friends being toasted by the ancient warrior in the woods furthest from the captive's tent. But not a drop of their pulque passed our lips. The Senorita sang and danced, much to the Indians' delight, but my bagpipes and our band roused them to a great pitch of excite- ment. The high priest stood aloof, bent and tottery, watching us suspiciously. During a short lull Jip came bounding up, with a string of beads and eagles' feathers about his neck. " Jip's no brooden. D'ye ken if aught's amiss ? " But Mark was as one dumb, for the old chief eyed us with characteristic cunning. Within fifteen minutes Pecos came hopping and dancing in our midst, and Mark observed: "The , evil glances thrown at Pecos by the other redskins shew how they despise physical deformity. Another proof that the physique of s man has much to do with his success in life." "Deesposition counts a lot," I answered. "I hae ken' clever men, walking encyclopvedias, fail fer want o' tact; while I has repeatedly seen mere nonentities rise i' the world an' become weal-to- do, because they were genial an' kept their ain counsel." But my attention at that moment was riveted on the chief's pipe, and I made bold to ask whether 1 it was an ancestral one. The least observant 1 among us could sae thl effect of his reply upon Lucia Diaz, when Pecos translated it at my bidding, with an obvious purpose. "This pipe," quoth the chief, casting down his eyes with an air of apologetic honesty, "is a gift from a Castilian, Muro by name, a white brave ' of my acquaintance, whom I left in the Gila Valley on his way to Mexico." I noticed that Lucia's gaze was suspiciously riveted upon the high priest, who stood aloof. Mark looked dubious, and when he rose to go it was a signal for Pecos to shuffle off, for Jip to frisk like a pleased pup, and for the lot of us to bid our Indian friends a speedy good-bye. But the Senorita appeared disappointed, and lingered as though joining us with reluctance. Mark observed her keenly, then with wistful affection, for nothing sinister was betrayed by her face or eyes, but a sadness seldom witnessed made her supremely beautiful, even to an unsentimental judge like myself. After all, Mark was to be envied his choice, for wherever we journeyed the Senorita was the cynosure of all eyes. "Lucia, you look troubled," he said tenderly. "Have you a longing to remain with these red- skins 7" And as she shook her head he added, laughingly: "She that is not with me is against me. My darling, choose." She raised her eyebrows and pointed her fan cautiously at the sacrificial block, and with a puzzled face nodded in the direction of the prisoner's tent. "Why are you so callous, Mark, when a two- edged sword hangs above the girl's head?" Then one saw by Mark's solicitude as he put his arm through hers and persuasively led her away that he comprehended her anxiety. "Now is the merriment," she observed, in an undertone, to us, "but before another sundown the cruel sacrifice will be complete." "There's na coot that the captive maiden will be stripped," I explained, "tied face upwards to the block, an' her heart be torn oot by yonder high priest. In cases the like o' these, I sometimes think a white lie is forgi'en ; to deceive sic monsters is, in legal phraseology, • privileged,' far the end justifies the means." "Having given my word of honour that we will not molest this people," spake my chief, somewhat sternly, "I am not to be moved by covert innuendoes." I dug my hands deep in my pockets and mattered: "Humbug! " Then, finding Lucia's charms add loveliness exercising a beneficent influence on Mark's temper, I ventured another bid for the girl's release. "I have promised not to interfere," expostulated Mark, somewhat irritably, "and you have no right to encourage me to break faith even with a red- skin." I said no more, objecting to be snubbed, and the Senorita was as quiet as a wild rabbit in clover. "Wurnen are seldom conseestent," I murmured, walking aside; "ane hour they sob in tender pity an' the next they're 'frivolous. Noo the butterfly flits aboot, flutterin' her wings, her colours brilliant in the soonshine, drinkin' in the nectar, but ne'er restful except she is nestlin' on the breast o' her lover. At this moment the captive girl's danger Is nano the less imminent, then why ' Lucia's apathy? I hae it—Mark has silenced her." It was not my business to upbraid him, for we were not strong enough to ward off the hand of fate from this poor girl. The free-and-easy method of our departure puzzled me, and not till we had travelled to a safe distance did the reason for my colleague's sang-froid dawn upon me. Lucia's prescience must have discovered treachery, for had she not warned us of a deadly potion?</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII. TIIM COMING OF A HALT-CASTE.</p>
        <p>It so happened that I lingered and fell abreast I of the serving-women, and, much to my surprise, I discovered a stately girl among them, a perfect stranger to me. I spurred my mustang and was soon by Mark's side. "I say, Mark," said I familiarly, "who is yonder addition to our party? I mean the new female." "Some Indian hag, I suppose," be carelessly replied, "a camp follower. Tramps will always abound—worthless men and woman who have no aspirations beyond sponging ot their betters. They eiist in every class of society. Don't give her tobacco, Sandy, or she'll stick to our party like a leech." "Hoot, man!" I replied, incredulously gazing at his immovable countenance, "she's a young half- caste, as beautifully moulded as the Senorita. Lips like Cupid's bow, bewitching dark een, an' a divine face!" "You are joking, Sandy," observed Mark, laughing heartily. "How long have you constituted yourself a judge of beauty This is not April Fools' Day." "I'm positive a strange half-caste is there," I somewhat testily replied. "I ne'er saw a mair interesting lass. Her beauty may no' be enhanced by the tinge o' copper colour, but a mair intelligent face I ne'er clapped een on. If ye doot it, draw up an' see fer yourser." "No, no," smartly responded my leader, "we must press on faster. We are going at a danger ously slow pace. Hurry up the lads." And 13,: urged his steed forward. Now I noticed that occasionally Pecos tares and after short conversations with this yoe‘ girl he spoke to Mark, by the way. The retiwk , was that whenever we came to a stream the horses ' were made to walk in the shallow water, and all the common devices were resorted to for the deception of pursuers. The scouts and rearguards had strict instructions to keep a good look-out; and when a beautiful moon rose and the long shadows of the pines slowly crept about us Mark beckoned me aside. He spoke in a confidential tone. "Sandy, Pecos has had his wits about him to-day. He learnt that the captive girl was a half- caste of his tribe, and while we entertained the savages he managed to release her." I uttered a sound of surprise, but Mark silenced me. "Pecos," continued he, "confided his plan to me, and I agreed. He frolicked with Jio, threw sticks</p>
        <p>among the trees, and chased the dog round the tents till the sentinel seemed to enjoy the fun- Of course, the hunchback appeared to have Yas designs upon the prisoner—a despised fellow like that would be no match for a stalwart brava. At last, kya cunning gift of tobacco, Pecos managed to ge4 chummy with the Indian guard, and then the whiskey flask was brought out. At great risk Pecos at last managed to excite the other's appetite for a drink, but before handing him the flask he poured some in the cup, put his lips to it, and apparently drank a good drop. The other's suspicion was thrown off, and, as the sentinel was thirsty after his hunt for snakes, he relished a deep draught. Pecos took the flask, placed it in his pocket, and, with a few friendly words, was soon capering again with Jip. But the smart chap had one eye on the dog and the other on the sleepy Indian. Pecos saw the fellow gradually r7t4ecumb—in fact, he slept as he sat by the tent. ereupon Pecos sent Jip to the camp and crawled to the rescue, his knife between his teeth, ready for an emergency. On entering the tent he found the girl fastened to a stake driven deep into the ground. She was half dazed and exhausted; but Pecos had drawn the charm from his breast, and when he roused her and she saw the token of kinship Pecos had his work cut out to stop her crying aloud with surprise. A hushed word or two in her native tongue gave the girl hope and strength, and in a trice Pecos had cut the horse-hair cords which bound her.</p>
        <p>As Mark coughed I exclaimed in admiration: "The clever monkey!"</p>
        <p>"Well," continued my friend, "the next thing was for Pecos to get the girl into our camp with- out observation, and for this purpose he had wound a light frock and ribbons about his body beneath his jersey. These the trembling girl put on, and when your bagpipes were amusing the Indiano the two crawled among the brushwood and safely joined our women. After a hasty meal the girl a 0011 recovered sufficiently to tell the story of her capture and the whereabouts of her home. And who do you think she is ? "</p>
        <p>"I dinna ken."</p>
        <p>"Why, Olive, the only daughter of Queen Kana, begotten of a pale-face. She was captured by a cunning dmign, her royal birth probably marking lwr for destruction." "Begotten 0' a paleface? " I reiterated. "0' what name?"</p>
        <p>"dh, there I'm done. The girl apparently does not know herself." I tossed my head and smiled incredulously, as wiseacres are wont to do. Bah! what a simpleton my rigid bachelorhood often made me My theories were impotent, compared with even Lucia's know- ledge of human nature. I had condemned my chief's supine conduct, till the Senorita's silence gave me confidence in his generosity towards the captive. While I fumed to myself with vain con- ceit, a mere hunchback's subterfuge worked the oracle. This lesson taught me caution and prompt- ness. Really, a man begins to think and learn how to live when the period of sere and yellow leaf overtakes him. The only consolation is that this existence is but a probationary stage to the life which casts off the mortal, as a seed casts its shuck and springs up anew. Such were my musings while Mark gave a few commands, and when he rejoined me he said: "You see, Sandy, with such odds against, us my stratagem was better than bribery or bloodshed. An explorer may be humane, and much good may be done without bluster. It was wise of me to appear negligent. If an admiral were to open his secret despatches and pin them to the mast, he might lose his fleet, for excited shipmates are apt to blunder. If you get married, you will learn that a husband dare not tell all his business to his wife."</p>
        <p>"Weal, that's a nasty knock, Mark, but it cuts ,Doth ways. D'ye think the Senorita will e'er be absolutely confidential?" "You've scored one there, Sandy. I hope Lucia will make Olive at her ease." "She'll do that fast enough," I responded, "so long as ye do no' pay too great attention to the Princess.' "No fear of that," laughed Mark. "I'm not such a fool." "Ye hae yer hostage, noo bargain fer yer goods." "I've no quarrel with Olive, but she's a splendid prize, for through her I shall find immediate favour with Kana, and effect my purpose without a bother. I shall now get my own with interest" "I wondered why ye took sic a zig-zag course on leaving. After the licking we gave the redskins at San Leno those fellows will na risk a brush wi' us."</p>
        <p>—"I propose having a hasty supper where our fires can't be seen far afield, and then we must press on for a few hours towards Kana's country, and camp in the open." "'Why was Princess Olive picked oot for kid- nappin' ? " "So far as I gather, it was cunningly done at the instigation of some palefaces ; and Pecos says it was not for blackmailing purposes, but I think it was one of Muro's tricks to get a big ransom." I whistled with surprise. Then I remembered the jealous look which flashed across Lucia's glow- tag face as Mark repeated to her what Pecos said concerning this Indian Princess. But I held my tongue, and patiently awaited developments. We selected a diminutive and well-wooded gorge for our halting place, the patrols having strict injunctions to 'keep well on the alert and report the least suspicion of danger. The meal over, Pecos led Olive forth, and presented her to our leader. The interesting maiden was still weak and trembling, in consequence of her horrible dread of torture; yet she had tho dignity and grace of her tribe, combined, as I soon discovered, with highly civilised tastes. None of the licentious brutality once common had been practised on her by her captors. Her physique was of a graceful mould, and as she sat on a fallen tree before our blazing fire, between Lucia and Mark, I saw that her skin was singularly clear, though it could not vie in delicacy with the Senorita's, her dark eyes intensely eloquent, and her manner naturally amiable. My observation bore out all the good points referred to by her kinsman. Mark was extremely reticent both of speech and look, but Lucia's close interest manifested itself in rapt attention. She hung upon the maiden's soft intonation; she comforted her with extreme solicitude; she put an arm about her waist in a sisterly way and held her hand, looking with winsome regard into her wistful yet glorious eyes ; and whenever Olive's face became pensive Lucia's various graceful attitudes shewed how deeply absorbed was her warm heart in the 'welfare of this pastoral waif. Pecos interpreted in a somewhat husky voice as he sat at the feet of his Princess and gazed intently at her, the while she told how she was caught as a bird in a snare, and expressed her gratitude and Kana's obligation. The Senorita dangled a richly embroidered purse of money before Olive's eyes, and bid Pecos offer it for an account of her religious myths. Thereupon Olive shook her head seriously, and disdained pay- went. I saw the strong-featured Mark Pepys bend forward admiringly, his expression the reflex of his soul. "Olive is above sordid considerations, Lucia; treat her tenderly and you will win her affection. Already we have her confidence." Lucia gazed at her lover for a moment, a cynical smile playing about her pretty mouth. "I know a woman's duty to the stranger," she falteringly said. And I wondered whether, with this lovely Indian opal shewing its rich and variegated colours, the Castilian diamond would lose any of its lustre in Mark's eyes. The Princess feeling chilly, Lucia covered her shoulders with a shawl. She nestled Olive's head upon her bosom in a sisterly fashion. Shortly there came a scout breathless with haste and anxiety. To him Mark and I went, and these were his words: "Yonder, some two miles, a band of some laundreds of Indians hey' passed, bearin' s'uth- oast and in hot haste. From the cover of a copse could see thet many were armed with muskets, an' through my glass I clearly saw the leader's profile upon the sky. I guess he's Muro." "Had he a half-Mexican garb?" "Theta his cut, sir. An' ez I could make out," rejoined the scout, "he makes towards our destina- tion, Golden Vale, I reckon. Well, I stopped thar some time, fer fear of stragglers, so thet by now they must be five or six miles away. Per God's sake don't 'ee let thet pore gel hear, else she'll guess they're arter her, an' go stark mad wi' fright." "They've discovered Olive's escape, or they are another party bent on annihilating us," was Mark's rejoinder. "Strike camp immediately!" A look-out on the top of a crag shouted that bonfires were being lit on the tops of many mountains and hills away to the south-east. Pecos asked Olive the meaning of this, and, with agita- tion, she explained that Queen Kana's warriors were preparing to go on the war-path against invaders. This news determined our movements, and soon our camp was all bustle for a speedy departure. When we neared the Golden Vale, Olive put her hand on my arm and shuddered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SECRET OF MARK PEPYS.</head>
        <p>SECRET OF MARK PEPYS.</p>
        <p>BY FRED J. PROCTOR, Author of "Timothy Twill's Secret," "Jean Afarlou," "Dr. Dobbs," &amp;o.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: This story is related by Sandy Dees, a Californian trader before the days of the gold rush. His em- ployer, Mark Pepys, is in love with a Mexican girl, Lucia Diaz. Manuel Muro, a Mexican, also appears to love the girl, and Dees fears that he is plotting to take Pepys's life. He doubts the girl's good faith also. One night Pepys's store is ransacked, and a half-breed on guard is killed. There is reason to believe that Muro and a band of Indians have bad a hand in the affair. Pepys takes an oath to revenge the half-breed. Sandy Dees discovers that Pepys has a secret which prevents him ever returning to his native land, and the Scot tries to discover its nature. The settlement of San Leno, besieged by Indians, sends for help. Mark and his friends, wit:: Lucia,proceed thither. San Leno is besieged, and Mark and his men are almost defeated. The settle- ment is relieved, after Lucia has slain a traitor.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XI. PROPITIATING A GOD.</p>
        <p>Through the diplomacy of Pecos, Mark, Lucia, and I were introduced to the ugly old warrior—a shrivelled and genuine one this time! He was seated upon a log, and when we approached he removed his pipe from his mouth and cried with a loud voice: "Let the drum beating cease. 'Welcome, palefaces!" "Thank him, Pecos," answered Mark, "and ask permission for me to fill his pipe." The grizzly old man handed it without the least Compunction. I offered him whiskey or brandy, but he stolidly refused. In answer to the appeal of Pecos for our people to view the ceremony, the chief replied: ."Yes, if the palefaces promise neither to molest 'nor even to rebuke my people." Pecos consulted us, and then said :r"Noble chief, we are dumb—we see and hear, but our hands end • tongues move not save at the bidding of our host." "And before the sun rises miles shall part us? " was the chief's trite rejoinder. "Even so, lord of the wilds." Thereupon the wily old man rose, and gave instructions for our accommodation with seats at a spot diametrically opposite that part of the camp where the comely captive was a prisoner, tied to a stake within a tent. Pecos had bribed the underlings into giving him a description of the maid, and our curiosity was whetted by his glowing account of her good points, As for Lucia, she already worshipped the girl, for she had fairly bewildered Pecos with questions. "Did ye recognise that pipe?" I whispered to Mark, as we wended our way to the vantage ground. "Yes, it once hung in Puke's Delight. The name was carved on it. We are on the track of the robbers. Be cautious what you say, for we are watched. These fellows may be in league with Muro ; but we are too strong for them." "Did ye no' see boo Lucia stared, her bosom heaved, an' the veins stood oot on her forehead when the chief passed the pipe?" "I noticed the flush, and then the pallor of her face. The Senorita is an insoluble puzzle to me. Hush! she speaks." "I am sure the chief is an artful old coyote," she averred, with that sweet intonation of voice so universal among the Castilians. "He bids us adieu to-night, lest we should object to the girl's murder on the morrow. If she were of my own blood, I could not get more anxious about her. I hate these savage monsters, who gloat over her misery, and look with such longing for to-morrow's tortures. Oh, how the fiends will laugh at her pain, and jeer when she groans! Hateful are the men who look on women as beasts of burden, to be kicked and brutally Mused! Even now this girl is undergoing mental torture. Is Mark's good nature changing ?—he makes friends with the bar- barian 1" Lucia looked up unflinchingly into my eyes as I . meekly made pretence to screen him. "I'm afraid the ad fellow stipulated our departure before day- break, and Mark is a man of his word." The dreamy radiance of her lovely eyes gave place to a look ardent yet witch-like. For Mark's peace of mind I succumbed to the glamour of this feminine gimcrack; bowed deferentially, and moved a pace to the rear. She simply sighed, and, like an im- passive snowflower, reclined upon a hillock. One moment her passionate bearing and pride goaded me to retaliation, the next I was in a chaotic state of mind; for she looked beautifully sad, her eyes lit with dreamy radiance, and her manner gentle even to the melting of my stony heart. So I added, soothingly: you and the musicians should play before these barbarians, win their fay..ur, and so break the ice for us to negotiate for the girl's release." She laughed, and, seeing my discomfiture, softly hummed an operatic air. This was tantalising, and I impatiently patted the turf with my sole, a/nd whipped my leg—sure signs of an unbalanced haind, or temper, as you have cared to anticipate. On my honour, I declare I was destitute of faith in Lucia; and, save that shame prevented me acting the mean cur towards Mark, I had snapped my fingers at the Castilian, and again candidly given tny estimate of her. I "I comprehend your thoughts, Senor Dees," she h smilingly observed, "as plainly as I read a book. You class me as a Mestiza, outwardly showy, perhaps a little wilful, but possessing the soul of a harpy. Maybe you weep for Mark, as the victim of my prurient desires; you think I have the heart af a vulture, dressed in the plumage of a humming- bird, and enticing with the voice of melody. I , tell you, sir; there are greater sins among man than pandering to the love of Lucia Diaz. Hide it as you may, I have long perceived the contempt you bear for me. But there, I would rather have zour hate than the attentions of a moneyed milksop. You pose as a saint—" "I deny itl But if your fascinations are harm- ful to my friend, I claim the right to warn him." With a sarcastic smile she continued: "I despise a libertine; I loathe his company, and I take a delight in tearing the mask from the face of a hypocrite. Perhaps even a paragon of perfection, like yourself, may some day acknowledge that Lucia s love for your friend was both pure and compassionate. I have somewhat abruptly told you whit is my estimate of your distrust. What- ever the future may reveal—even if I were jealous of Mark„ or annoyed at your petty interference— your chief shall not suffer at my hands. As the air is clearer and cooler after a thunderstorm, so I hope my candour may clear that indefinable suspicion which lies so heavily about me." "Senorita," I averred, "I have no desire to quarrel with Mark's choice. If I have not the suavity which win', nor the pleasant humour which attracts women, that is my misfortune. You do not understand me, nor I you. Let us not mar others' happiness with our quarrels." She covered her face with her hands to hide the tearless anguish of her mind. Her character was inexplicable. In bravery and natural gifts she was vastly superior to most women, and yet I judged her to be brimful of deceit. • An incantation by the choristers interrupted raj thoughts, and as Mark approached Lucia's vivacity returned with surprising suddenness. Her manner was gracious towards me, and loving towards Mark. Of a strange and complicated nature is woman! "The arrangements for the ceremony are com- plete," observed my leader. 'Let us take up our positions for a rare sight." The noise made by singers and gourd rattlers was deafening, and the monotonous chanting resembled a funeral dirge. "Look!" said Mark, "the dancers are coming from between the tents stark naked. This is awkward for Lucia and the women." But no notice was taken of it, for each dancer carried a snake between his teeth and several in his hands, and trotting four times round the circle at last de- posited the reptiles in the sacred lodge—a small tent of Cotton-wood saplings covered with buffalo skins and shaped in the form of a crude human</p>
        <p>head. "Are the snakes deadly poisonous ?" asked the Senorita. I nodded, and put my finger on my lips, as a warning for the lady to hold her tongue. The feeble high-priest was bent with age, he hobbled along with the aid of a staff, his weird and device-stained face ghoulish. At a sign his sub- ordinates sprinkled the sacred altar with a most delicious incense, and the votive offerings were placed at the foot of the sacred lodge. The tallest and thinnest Indian I ever saw then twirled an eagle's feather and sling, producing a sound like falling rain. The choristers imitated • the devotees weirdly danced with live snakes between their teeth ; and with their eagles' feathers and I:MA4air cords the corn-throwers ir.itated the uatteitVo Qs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>-The foe is invacimg Ler coon :re:- rrens gasped. "A stout resistance awaits them in yonder entrance, for the defile is no-row, sod her penple are stn. The few trails from here riht no the, heart of Golden Vale are dangerous, :,111 gdarded by trusty warriors. No foe, hoiieeer strong, can conquer Golden Vale, for no man are Weaken their united defence by dissension; and If by opening the lake sluices they let the water flow into the defile a foe would be overwhelmed in the flood. At either end of the defile is an embank- ment, and the sides neither man nor animal, nor hardly any creeping thing, can scale. Once a horde of Pah Utes advanced beyond the inner embank- ment, when the dykes were flooded and retreat cut off. Then liana's warriors came down upon the foe and slew every man not even sparing the leaders as hostages. They who shut their eyes have little hope of escaping danger; it behoves us, therefore, to be cautious as day dawns." We dared not strike a light, but I could just discern the girl pressing her aching temples with _ her fingers as she thought a minute or two. Sud- denly a hopeful idea seemed to flash through her brain, and Pecos interpreted her plan. "Olive says that a vigilant duty towards bet , dead relatives has given her special knowledge of. the Royal caves, and the way thereto by the cliffs , 410 and slopes on either side. Her mother, Kano, ' shewed her these secret paths, and instructed her , as to the disposal of the dried bodies of her ancestors. If we all swear not to touch and se profane them—in which there would be great risk ' from snares set for the unwary—Olive will venture, to defy the wrath of her priests for the object ifl view, and lead us ,through into the valley. Art thou willing to follow her? " Mark assented, and we rejoined our party. Orders were given in case of surprise, and the explorers consisted of Olive, Mark, Pecos and myself. The others were removed further up the mountain to an almost impregnable spot, but when Lucia divined our intention there was a lively scene for a few minutes. "Mark," she cried, throwing her arms about his neck, after he had tersely explained our mission, "let me join you. Why run into danger? Why tfiryt,heearyn etrumosttioynoirsaselfhteo the guidance of this girl? Indian blood runs in her veins, therefore she has the wdmoi Ifed eilleeafwrnt dortigaolcedoenilt mournilwvyeYh. om yi iosii "ill r a bblloss, Mark, I love you," she urged, with suppressed between resltfraihneerd life eris o"fChulooseore tow killedictohmofufootrrat yoursomt as . value to you than mine, then you will refuse to take me with you." He hesitated, and she added: "Look at the risk you run. This conflict is not of your making, nor are you called on to interfere. Besides-0 Mark, do listen to met—it is not right to leave us here in this dark and lonely spot, exposed to danger, and all for this stranger!" Be pressed her to his heart and fondly kissed her burning cheek; and then, pointing to a streak of faint grey light over the eastern hills, he quietly observed: "Seel the dawn breaks! Cheer up, Lucia; we four may be running into grave danger; here you have many willing and able to defend you. One narrow gully is easily defended, and if the worst befell you might barricade that with boulders, and escape by the royal caves. The heroine of San Leno will not lack ample means of resistance for her own courage will command resources." .1 "Now, that is very gracefully put," replied the ' Senorita, "but most unsatisfactory." ; "Let us go now, Lucia," urgedour leader in s winning undertone, "for every moment is precious.° "If your affection is not mere profession, then don't expose me to unnecessary danger. I arn willing to brave the worst at your side." "Here you have a chance of escape. I know not what lies before me; but I think the gratitude a Kane for the saving of her daughter will make ample amends for the risk. If a case of dire necessity compels you to follow us—and nothing save that must tempt you—the entrance to the , caves will be easily found, for we shall remove time brushwood and adobe paste which binds the stonesto.o Lucia wept for a moment and trembled in Owl limb, as though scared by her prescience. She w 55 ' neither petulant nor irritable now, but by bef, ' subdued manner and soft, persuasive voice r• - guessed some of our future experiences were fo r shadowed. "Mark," she said, "you will rue the day wise Olive's company is more welcome than mine.' - Believe me, I am not jealous of this half-caste, but simply disappointed that you put implicit faith ill a comparative stranger. Smart people laugh lit their sleeve at the stubborn. No bogey could frighten a man of undaunted spirit, but the greater the leader the more glaring his mistakes. If Mare which syonids a gave o yonder, his presence of j eis u m adgmeenntace i, bec • ftoigruasee of your foolhardiness, you yet writhe with pain--; :0 if the anguish of your mind is unspeakable torrasse' i —remember, I warned you!" i lewassdeeply was,imri for resse Lucia made n dLubcythisasPeecop speech, prhenibri 11 me, but more in sorrow than in anger she joifl the waiting women. Then we three followed OHO up a narrow and tortuous path, finally reaching thick brushwood, where Pecos gave the signal ta * halt and clear the ground in front. After abco: ten minutes' labour the opening was made, an° lighting a lantern for Olive, we followed aloe@ after into the dingy air of a cavern. But Disrk hushed us, and with'a puzzled face retraced his stiell to the mouth of the cavern, where he stood ne° listened. I had wondered whether the Senorita would secretly stalk in our wake for sinister Fur; poses begotten of jealousy. I had even fancied a beard a sigh, and when Mark beckoned us to return and began to pile up stones and brushwood, Partil to hide the entrance, my suspicions vve"' . .. _</p>
        <p>strengthened. "Why these precautions?" I asked. "To prevent sly coyotes sneaking in after us,' 11 !tirelessly replied. "Come, let us make hast&amp; illimet much relish the job."</p>
