        <p>The small diagram will enable our readers to understand the nota- tion by which the moves of the game are recorded. Black occupies the j squares numbered 1 to 12 and white those num- bered 21 to P. The o. figures thus (11-15) indi- ,• .; cate that a move is made from and to the squares so numbered. Black has the opening move and ----.........____ .. ' . •''.. ;' l the pairs of figures ftlY alternately to Black ad White to the close. 40- XATII OF A WARRINGTON SERGEANT AT CAIRO. ~,e military authorities last week conveyed ttoT 'I ' r. William Kerfoot, of 26, Sankey-greet; the ' iittelligence of the death at Cairo of hie son, c." 11, who was a ~;rgeant, in the Army Service , on' P,B* Lle succumbed to an attack of enteric fever itt,tee 16th instant at the Military Hospital Cairo, i 4' wata buried in the English Cemetery, n the tg:latien capital. Sergeant Glitch, a comrade of 10„,(leceased sergeant, has forwarded to Mrs. Ker- laef i further particulars from which it appears that the'geant Rerqoot arrived at Cairo from Atbara on f ho 701 October, feeling a bit unwell winch he I/le ugi.ht was owiri to the change from the Soudan. Abe uad enjoyed the beit of health during ;Derr, reePht expedition to Omdurman and ipf th'rn3el his work to the entire satisfaction 'lnn e °incurs under whom be served. On the 12th 14tettnt he became seriously ill and died four days The funeral was attended by the Officers, rasa. cleamissioned Officers and men, and the de- was carried to his last resting place by four I.Vants. A splendid wreath had been subscribed ti Y the deceased's Company. During the cam- the late Sergeant bad written several inter- .: letters borne, one of the them from Omdur- apig nd be was then in the hest of health and "a• U 5 bad been in the army about 5 years.</p>
        <p>C k PITA T. "' CHAT.</p>
        <p>Sir F ‘rrest Fulron',s ,31lizgeRtiOrl nt OPP, (..Yd 13f,i1ov that, whereas at petty sossioriS peo,,outlons constantly Conluctel by an inspector of poicP, the latter should under no circumstancea be allowed to cross-examine a prisoner giving evPence under the Criminal Evidencs Act is arousing some controverw in the legal profession. The suggested prohibition meets with very general sympathy. But it is re- garded as a matter of doubt whether it could be justified under the Act.</p>
        <p>It is now generally admitted that in the near future London must obtl,in an additional supply of water, probably from Wales. and the vast cost of such a scheme, co-r.bined with the purchase of the rights of the existing companies is a matter that has beei engaging, the serious consideration of the Fi- nance Committee of the County Council In ordinary cases the period fixed by Parliament for the repay- ment of Metropolitan loans is at the most 60 years. It is held, however, that in the case of a great water scheme such as that under consideration more lib- eral terms should be allowed. The water companies' interests would be acquired once and for ever, and if in addition the present ratepayer undertakes a charge for which he gets no immediate return in order to secure a lasting supply for posterity, the burden, it is contended, should be made as light as possible. Hence the Finance Committee, which is presided over by Lord Welby, suggest that the period allowed for the redemption of the debt should be not 60 but, 100 years. The inquiry which the Duke of Norfolk proposes to hold concerning the pay of postmen will, I hear, extend to all parts of the country. The announce- ment of this inquiry came rather as a surprise to the leaders of the men, but they are taking steps to second the Postmaster's General's desire that the inquiry shall be as thorough as pissible. Of course, they know that the Duke has acted in opposition to the advice of some of the permanent officials, and they express a fear that the latter may, in some measure, defeat the object of the inquiry. The Duke will be asked to allow the Committee to re- ceive evidence from every class of postmen.</p>
        <p>eFromiety fort the of the er o next the Royal Geographical tmsmesesion it may be said that the Society promise is very interesting. The president will open with an address in support of the projected Antarctic expedition ; and there will be separate addresses by distinguished men of science on the different branches of investigation in which the expedition would engage: Papers will be read on the Caroline Islands, of which something was heard during the American and Spanish war ; on the Christmas Island, in conr.ection with which Mr. C. W. Andrews will give an account of his year's ex- plorations there; on the Atlantic Islands o the United States, by Professor Davis,of Harvard,whose reputat on as a scientific geographer stenos higher than that of any other American; and on some pro- blems connected with the Canadian Rockies,on the rre,•Tnr, Socotra expedition, and on certain features of Central Africa, Mr. Bell, the general secretary of the Amalgam ated Society of Railaysy Servants, has so far sue- eseded in the remonstranees he addressed to the Board of Trade. in reference to the dismissal of railway employees for belonging to trade unions, that Mr. Ritchie has been inbreed to promise to make further representations to the railway com- panies concerned if he will supply th 3 Board o Trade with satisfactory evidence that the men have been dismist,ed for the reason assigned. As usual ,in such cases, the reply of the companies to Mr. 'Ritchie's first inquiry has been that the men had hsen dismissed on other grounds nit ogether.but Mr. Bell's offer to prove his allegations, and the support he is receiving from the Parliamentary Committee of the Trades Congress, will compel the Board of Trade to do something. Mr. Bell called for an in- quiry, and the Department says it has no power to institute one. Nobody would ever dream of re. quiring the Board to order the reinstatement of the dismissed men; but as its powers stand at present, the companies are not even bound to make any reply to the workmen's charges. The numerous Cheshire friends of Lord Delamere will be glad to learn that he arrived in London looking none the worse for his two years' expedi- tion in British East Africa. I learn that his col- lection of sporting trophies and natural history specimens is one of the largest that has ever been obtained in that region, and includes tesnlendid lot of ivory, skins of lions, several very interesting species of water buck and other antelope, giraffe, and a great many other varieties of animals. From a zoological standpoint the results of this expedi- tion should be interesting and important, a great part of the country visited by Lord Delamere be- ing almost unexplored by naturalists. It is almost certain that his collection contains several forms new to science. The greater part of it hasJ under- stand, already arrived. Lord Delamere left London on Monday evening for his home at Vale Royal, Cheshire.</p>
        <p>Not the least interesting of the funetions aris- ing out of the Sirdar's return is the banquet at which he is to be entertained by the Army and Navy Club. Although "the Rag" has the distinc- tion of being the first military club to permit the introduction of visitors within its walls, it seldom ' pays an ontSider the double compliment, which has been decided upon in the present case, of giving him a dinner as we'd as making him an honorary member. The Duke of Cambridge, in whose honour a banquet presided over by General Sir A. Herbert was given in January, 1888, had then been presi- dent of the club for nearly forty years; apart from which the occasion for the gathering was unique in that 1888 was not only the fiftieth year of the Duke's military service, but also the jubilee of the club. But Lord Kitchener's election as an honorary member is not even yet an accomplished fact, the decision of the Committee in a matter of this kind requiring confirmation by the general body of members. A special meeting for this purpose and to discuss the arrangements for the proposed dinner has been called for donday week. It may be noted by the way that amongst those who are or have been honorary members of the Army and Navy Club are the Prince of Wales, the Duke of Clarence, the Duke of Saxe-Coburg. the Duke of Connaught, the Duke of Tork, Prince Henry of Tlattenberg, the Duke of Teck, Ismail Pasha, the crown Prince of Denmark, Prinee Christian, Lord Roberts, and Prince Henry of Prussia. There cannot be any doubt that the Admiralty find it very inconvenient to have their operations in the dockyards chronicled so faithfully end com- pletely by the press. The chronicles are brief enough, but the Government cannot be blind to the fact that it is these daily revelations of the recent abnormal activity in the dockyards, and the exten- siveness of our naval preparations, that have done more than anything else to reconcile the Chauvinist opinion of Paris to the evacuation of Fashoda. It is taking refuge now in the compensatory convic- tion that the Egyptian question is to be raised in its entirety. As yet this is a newspaper opinion, delivered by men who are blind to the fact that the matter could not. be raised at. a more opportune time for England or a more inopportnne time for France. It is net quite shared in by French statesmen out, of business, and the new French Government have not yet had time to formulate a proposal which is as likely to lend to war as the French retention of Fashcia At all events, iv is a reason for England continuing to strengthen her- self, as it will be for France and Russia doing the same. What then becomes of the Czar's peace pro- nrosal ? Perhaps the moment has arrived for England to settle the Egyptian question, once and for 11, and the opinion here is very strong that she should.</p>
        <p>CHANGE. Vainly our thOnAta with most extensive range seek for an objr-ct that will never chance, For change throughout the Univcrap is streal, And all who live must mingle with the dead. From birth the e'o,ing scsns r+f death. We change for ever with each flee'ing breath, In childhood, youth and manheld's sober nritne Bow much we alter with the march of time. Where are th^ friends in early cease we knew In middle age so different and how few, And where are they who 1 d our coun'ry then Ere yet we mingled with the strife of men. The heart remains not as in days by-gola Affection changes as the years roll on Our t tter nature yearning for the right, As lovely flowers lean forward to the light. And love, ah love, life'i fairest prr cisua flower That sweetly 1.4 oms in freedom's sunny bower, With freedom only can tbs be pure, Deceit and bondage why must love endure. But all is charging, and th. darkeq night Draws ever nearer to the morning light,' Dense clouds of sin do not for ever stay, Triumphant changes bear them a'l away. S. lI*NYON. Warrington, October, 1898.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER MAN'S SURPRISING</p>
        <p>CURE.</p>
        <p>ACUTE RHEUMATISM AND OTHER PAINFUL</p>
        <p>DISORDERS:</p>
        <p>A reporter of twenty years' standing on the staff of the " Manchestee South District Advertiser " says : I re,:eived certain information which led me to proceed to the residence of Mr. James Higg, junior, of 12 West- street, Hooley Hill. near Manchester, where I heard for myself some extraordinary evidence which this young man was anxious should be known to all the world. I burst at once into my subject. "You are the young man," I queried 'who have b2en so near dead?' "Yes," he replied, as hearty as a bare.</p>
        <p>"How was that ?" I mked.</p>
        <p>He retorted " Sit down and I will tell you as shortly as I can. About eighteen months ago I was attacked with what the doctor called rheumatism. I had pains in my back and in the region of my heart of a most excruciating character, and nignts and days my aunt and my mother applied hot remedies, which, though they relieved me, did no permanent good. For weeks, I was confined to my bed, and though the paina were less acute they continued, doctor's medicine appearing to do me little, if any good. At last one day I saw an advertisement respecting Dr. Wil- liams' Pink Pills for Pale Peopie.and I thought I should like to try them. The only advantage I felt from the use of the first box was that my appetite, which had almost gone, revived, and I began to eat solid toad again, leaving off the brandy and. whisky recommended by the doctor. I had taken the greater part of the second box when I found that my pains had left me, - so I persisted in their use until I had taken five boxes, when I felt .‘' / • I [(et. ..... ~ . -.-- myself quite able 1 rerew work,arid lave stuck at it r since." 1 reply to the !stion a s to where he worked, and what the nature of his em- ployment was, he said :—"I work in the dyeing depart- -l•-...y... . ..,i-•:- obi!, works of Messrs. I i Joseph Wilson and • Sone, at Dent'n. I . /i am quite strong "IN - "v THE again, and capable</p>
        <p>- HAT WORKS." of following my empins ment without pain'or fatigue. I ' have racom- mmded the use of Dr. Williams' Pink Pllls to all my friends, and if ever I am atachel again I stall fly to them once more."</p>
