      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NEW BOOK, MA.GAZINES ETC.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOK, MA.GAZINES ETC.</p>
        <p>MACMILLAN AND CO.—A volume of Kipling I stories may be relied upon at any time to deeply interest both young and old. The gifted Indian writes possesQes the knack of combining in his stories an equal amount of simplicity and thought- fulness, mingled witn quaint humour. As a novelist Kipling is a success in his own peculiar way,but we think his best work is to be found in the numer- ous short stories which has been the product of hi+ pen. Messrs. Nlacmilltin and Co. have just issued another volume of these delightful creations. It is a companion volume to " Many Inventions," and though the famous Mulyaney,Ortheris. and Learoyd are absent characters, "Toe Day's Work" is an equally charming book. We fail to appreciate the appropriateness of the title,nor have we discovered the source of the inzpiration which impelled Kip- ling to select it, but it has occurred to us that if the title was "After the Day's Work." none more suitable could have been chosen. We have yet to find a book the superior of either "Many Inven- tions,' or "The ' Day's Work,'" as sources of unal- loyed enjoyment during an evening by the fireside when one's daily toil has temporarily ' ceased. In this new volume there are a round ' dozen complete stories, many similar and many dis- similar. One or two we have met before, but the I majority are new to 119, and all without exception are delightful. Perhaps the palm must he given to "William the Conqueror." the longest of the tales, and sketched with exceeding cleverness. It depicts I a typical incident in Indian life—combat with the , I plague—interwoven with which is a vein of senti- went combining naturally with the surroundings ' and bapoenings of the story. An excellent sample of Kipling'e power in the rendering animate of things inanimate is" The Ship which Found Her- self,' while" The Walking Delegate" and "The Maltese Cat" give admirable scope to his genius for endowing the dumb brute with the language - of man. To see ourselves as animals see us is an experience, the pleasure of which we are indebted to Mr. Kipling for. " Bread upon the Waters" we judge to be in the i author's most humorous vein, and Chief Engineer I McPhee will be found to closely rival the heroes of "Many Inventions." The price of the nook is 65., and the stories are not dear at a couple of shillings each.—The same publishers have just commenced the issue of" Hugh Thomson's Illustrated Fairy Books," the first of which is the time honoured joy of our childhood "Jack the Giant Killer." We are sure that the famous chronicle of brave Jack's ex- • ploits was never presented in a more attractive form. The book is nearly all pictures, even the margins surrounding the printed matter being occupied pictorially. The illustrations,perticularly the coloured ones, are in Mr. Thomson's beat style. Giants of the most ferocious aspect and terrifyine size are depicted carrying off beautiful maidens or falling beneath the might of nandsome Jack's trusty sword. The new "Fairy Books" are well worth t':e is charged for each. A. CONSTABLE AND Co —Notwithstanding the fifty year's of its existence Samuel Lover's master- piece, "Handy Andy" must be strange to many scores of thousands of people who naturally love a humorous story. Therefore Messrs. Constable's edition de luxe will be welcome in many quarters.lP s price, 65., will perhaps make it prohibitive te tie masses, but with the classes it should be immedi- ately popular. In his capable introduction Mr. D. 'P. O. Donoghue says of "Handy Andy" that " In the whole range of modern comic literature there is not a mor3 amusing personage," and tlae story has only to be read to convin-e the reader of the truth of Mr. Donoghue'e rerv•k. Andy's escapades like " Charley's Aunt " "1 " make a cat laugh "could the feline dome ti cated animal only reed—" Beyond the Bo r," by W. D. Campbell, is a superbly decorated of fantastic stories of goblins and trolls. He Stratton furnishes over 160 faithful illustrat • . which add largely to the interest, attached to • somewhat uncommon tales of creatures exi only in the imagination of people dead generatio ago. They are distinct from the ordinary fairy tale, end to the young mind will be found intensely</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
