      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>A. LIFETIMES ILLNESS CURED.</head>
        <p>A. LIFETIMES ILLNESS CURED.</p>
        <p>A shock in childhood often carries with it conse- quences which remain for years, and in some cases, life- long weakness or infirmity may be caused by an acci- dent in childhood's days. A representative of the St. Helens Newspaper and Advertiser recently had a con- versation with Mrs. Sarah Robinson, who resides at No. 4. Back Scholes-lane, Thatto Heath, St. Helens. She is a young woman about twenty-five years of age and has been married two years. "When I was a little child, and residing in Wales," sain Mrs. Bobinson, "I had a narrow escape from drowning. I fell into the water, and my father just managed to rescue me as I was going down for the last time. It was with con- diffi.</p>
        <p>siderablo</p>
        <p>culty that I was brought round,</p>
        <p>and Chen I wss</p>
        <p>long 111, a weak- ness remag. which troubled</p>
        <p>ill wq et n 9 got rid r good. When I got up recently. iolninfhe happily eppfitol morning I used to have a</p>
        <p>;icily.- -- feeling</p>
        <p>and my eyie would be full of</p>
        <p>wbouutllmoyftheerialth did not improve, water; I would feel giddy and faint, _ upon hbaevvinog e vanie,d. Twc ye ea ;ye tabgi on gI gotl ha had eatenmarried, although I sought medical advice; the slight relief I ob- tained was only temporary. " In such a condition my state was a miserable one, and what was worse I suffered from paini and palpita- tion. Indeed, if anyone spoke to me suddenly I used to think my heart had jumped into my mouth,such a shock would I have. I had become very anmmic ; my face became deadly white, and my lips blue. I could not walk many yards without taking a rest and was entirely unable to perform my household duties." " And to what do you attribute your cure,Mrs. Robin- sore appearance left no SOH ? " (asked the reporter, who remarks that Mrs. "To Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. dltabbi nt sale] t' so hreors r ecsheenetkesx acenldl epn ltuhme My husband was reading in the paper about cures by them and I decided to get a box. After the first box the palpitation left me, and after the second box the giddiness and sickly feeling in the morning went, and I could get up without any unpleasant feeling. I have had five boxes altogether, and ever since I commenced taking them I have found a gradual improvement. My work is now a pleasure to me ; my washing, which had to be done by other 3, I can now do myself without any enfrooltodrttib; case now. e haettreemasopedretvolowoas4 Ia wisesw aljohatcs sure ti catch es, hut that is During the time the reporter was in the house, William Robinson, Mrs. Robinson's brother-in-law was c"</p>
        <p>which wswti'olalthe mind a faithful 1 interesting. Mr. Campbell handles his uncouth' characters most capably. The book is priced at 65., and is value for the money.— We b library haveek a arll sooos f histori- cal Macfar- lane received nLo(Tee'lns6dcland romances now being issued under Ao) f, oßneetaidooinfn, Constable's yAetb bi teY, oeoor nes art . of modern so, f tad al te dhtr yno, i the guidance of Mr. George LpaousrseenscseesGmomermitse.The novel under notice represents the reign of Stephen wanodrkwshoifleimnaogtiansa attractive as rrao:htnikyeeoonmfmc pecu- liarly its own, and lovers works class amnnvaden3ic ss. "Strand Magazine" is a it is Mr. H. G. Wells' weird production " The thatpi cie picture olri Reg oho ntf ot 1 ihr Be casualw N period,gnoso i number. perusal, smc boe._r . T hAi s notable , mouth's itetn in Stolen Body," which rather too closely resembles another story by the same author. Mr. Wells hae some curious fancies, and takes an especial delight 1 in the supernatural. " The Stolen Body" is of this class and is ingenious and thrilltng. Spiritualists, clairvoyants, and believers in thought and soul [ transmission will find it quite to their tare. Con- tributions of a rnore sereous character are" In Mr. Gladstone's Village," by Charles Marley and Hillis Friederichs ; "The Mushroom Coves of Paris," by Ni. D. Griffiths, and M. Bril ; and " The Story of the Carriage," bY Welland Wright. In addition there are stories of absorbing interest by Conan Doyle, Grant Allen, and G. M. Robins, the whole of the pages being, of course, copiously and capably illustrated.—" The Wide World Magazine" con- tinues to prove the adage that " truth is stranger than fiction." Another lengthy in- stalment of "De Rott4enslat's " inaposssible adventures takes first place. The publishers do not claim the story to be true, but the disclosures con- cereing its author add materially to any interest , attaching itself to these far-fetched inventions. Toe other Pages of the magazine teem with accounts o marvellous and hair-raising experiences; " How I was buried Alive," by Baron Corvo, and " A Brush with a Sea-lion," by Theo Gift, being the most excit- ing.—" Hans Andersen's Fairy Tales " are being issued in fortnightly parts (7d.), fourteen in mem- ber. Messrs. Newnes have undertaken the task in a fashion which ensures the new edition of these delightful stories an immense success. We need not dwell upon the excellencies of the tales themselves; who is there unacquainted with them? But we must not omit ts state that with the , splendid illustrative all of Miss Helen Strat- ton they cannot fail to revive the happy 'memories of our childhood to a remarkable degree The Younger generation is indeed fortunate in hav- ing the opportunity of peru.ing Andersen's popu- lar works in such a delectable form.—The same publishers have also commenced the re issue of some of the greatest copyright works on travel, history and biography which have recently dis- tinguished commence the English literature. They series with Paul du Chaillu's " Land of the Mid- night Sun," which will include all the original illustrations and some new ones. The first two fortnightly parts are to hand. They are beautifully printed on excellent paper, and well worth the 61, charged for them. Twelve parts will complete this new edition of a famous work. CASSELL AND CO.—The current number of "The (31agazine of Art" commences a new volume A special feature of it is a series of exquisitely besot,- ful illustrations in colours representing the adapta- tion of flowers to artistic fancy dress. Nothing that we have seen in the pages of a magazine before approaches even nearly to the lovely designs sub- mitted in this series. Nature is almost excelled in the richness of the colouring. Mr. Wilhelm is the artist to whom the public are indebted for this , addition to the attractive pages of the "Magazine of , „I Art." the volume just Commenced will be replete 1 with coloured illustrative work equal to the stand- . erd set this month. The articles in this month's num- 'i ber are all oF a high class,while in addition to the or- . 1 tnary illustrations and series of designs mentioned 1 above there are five plates by well-known artistes, . i reproduced in the best style of the printer's art "The Magazine of Art" has been before the public . now for 21 years, and during the whole of that</p>
