        <p>time its progress has been upward, and even yet improvements are continuous and noticeable.— " Cassell's Magazine" for November concludes another volume of this justly popular periodical. The complete stories by authors of high repute, the racy and instructive articles on all manner of sub- jects, and the superior illustrations still constitute its main feature. The competition of the past few years has brightened the pages of " Cassell's Maga- zine "very much, and it yet stands in the front rank of the innumerable monthlies osZ to-day. The serial story by Mr. Joseph Hocking, " Trevanion " comes to a striking finish, while we also have " The Gift of the Emperor," one of the most enthralling of Mr. TC.W.flornung's chronicles of the doings of A. J. Raffles, sometime cricketer and criminal ; amongst the general items of note are "Parliaments of the Empire," "London s Leading Chess Amateurs,"" Character in Cats," (by Louis Wain), and a "Talk with Madame Dreyfus." The new volume,is from all accounts, to be even an improve- ment on the excellent edition of 1898.— "The Quiver" is a little ahead of its Companion magazine and begins a new volume this month. Presented with Part 1 is a copy of Mr. Holman Hunt's picture, " The finding of the Saviour in the Temple," executed with skill and taste. There is also a special article on" The Em- peror's visit to the holy Land," illustrate I from photographs ;• " Pledgei,' a new serial story by Katherine Tynan; "As L'haplaia to Mr. Speaker," by Dean Farrar "The Minor Canon's Daughter," an- other serial by E. S. Curry, and numerous other contributions which testify to the enterprise and discrimination of the proprietors of the principal magazine for Sunday and general reading.—New issues ot papular works are continually emanating from the headquarters of Messrs. Cassell and Co and among those just ta hand is " Cassell's Papas, lar Educator.' in sixpenny weekly parta.--An en- tirely new and criginal work is "The Wars of the Nineties," ( 61. nasnthly), written by a well-known war rrespondent. It is profusely illustrated with stirring battle pictures, portraits, maps and plans &amp;.^.. The work promises to be of unusual interest and will be a valuable history of the warfare Of the concluding deca le of OA ninst ienth century.— Peril and Patriotism," (61.. monthly) is another a Iditian to the long list of true tales of heroic deeds and startling adventures. stirring illustration have been specially prepared for the wark, which judging by the first number, will be a exceedingly interesting account of perils afloat and ashore There is already a good supply of such literature. but we feel confident that " Peril and Patrioti4m has only to be known to command at once a large circle of subscribers. It will appeal directly to that love of adventure and danger which is inherent to the nature of every true Englishman. Satan, ELDRIL AND Co.—All the best qua:ities of past issues of " The Ccrnhill Magazine " will be found in the November part of the publication. " Inkermana " is the theme on which the Rev. lar a Fitchett dwells in continuation of his " Fight 3 for the Flags," the eleventh of the series. The Marquis of Dafferia and AVD, writes tersPls with respect to " The Seige of Bomarsund ;" an Old Whig "details with point "Some memories of Kensington Palace;" Canon Wood, D.D.. in a piquant way throws new light on " Witchcraft," and the Rev. F. L. Bernaya furnishes" More Humours of Clerical Life " with a pleasant chattiness WARD, LOCK, AND co.--Hr. Guy Boothby'a en- trancing serial story "Pharos the Egyptian con- cludes in the November "Windsor Magazine." Tha denouement is dramatic, and quite in keeping with this extraordinary story. Infinite variety is a char- acteristic of the "Windsor," and among the many valuable contributions to the current number are complete stories by Walter Wood, Spencer Leigh Hughes, Mayne Llndsay,and Captain F. M. Peacock. The articles include "Tommy Atkins on his Autumn campaign," by S. E.Waller; "Some Historic Fens' Frederick Do'man ' • and "Canadian Empire- builders of to-day," by James Ramsay. Truly the person who cannot find something, to interest, elevate, and amuse in the pages of the " Wind.; Magazine," must be a queer composition of the essentials which go to make upa human being. All manner of sensations and specialities, including a 6s novel gratis, are promised for the Christmas number.</p>
