      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>INDRPRNORNT LABOUR PARTY.</head>
        <p>INDRPRNORNT LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>At a w-eillTitZTias.-mee.7l/4—under the aust,i e of the above, held in the Druid's Hall, Warringtcn last Sunday evening, Mr: J, Hudson of Manchester. gave a recital of Tennyson's " Locksley Hall." Afterwards he proceeded to give a lecture entitled, " Toryism, Liberalism and Socialism" which was listened to very attentively. A number of ques- tions were asked and good discussions followed,</p>
        <p>PIZOSECUTION OF A SHOPKEEPER BY THE CO i? PORA.TION.</p>
        <p>0.3 Friday morning at the Warrington Police Court, before Mr. Hepherd (in the chair) and Councillor Srhethurst, Samuel Gough,furniture dealer, Winwick-at., was summoned, at the instance of the Corporation, for unlawfully and wilfully displacing the pavement in Winwick-street, by taking up 324 square feet of ger, pavement.—Defendant,who was not legally represented, pleaded not guilty. The Town Clerk said that 324 square feet had been illegally occupied by defendant, which would make the maximum penalty £6. Defendant had two shops in Winwick-street,which in 1893 he had built on the site of three cottages which formerly stood there. He then began to expose goads on the pavement, and be was warned more than once by the police for doing so. John Sutcliffe, assistant borough surveyor' said he had taken the measurement of the ground in front of de- fendant's shop. Three hundred and twenty-four square feet of land had been displaced. Mr. Longdin, borough surveyor, said no application was made to him by defendant to remove the cobbles and replace them. The Magistrates Clerk : Mr. Gough has removed the stones, and that is what he is charged with. John Booth, road foreman said he bad been in the employ of the Corporation for 47 years. He could re- member the premises when they consisted of a few cot— tages and brewery. Three cottages were on the site of Mr. Gough's shop. He had never seen any goods ex- posed in front of the shops, until a Mr. Williams kept a shop there. He could remember the Old Ball being rebuilt, and the land in front of the defendant's shop was paved by Joseph Whittaker for the Corporation. A tiled footpath was made and the remainder was cobbled. Ann McMillan, 7, Winwick-road, said she had pre- viously lived for 26 years at 103, IVinwick-street, on the site where Mr. Gough's shop was. She did not remem- ber any goods being exposed en the land before Mr. Williams had a shop. There was a pavement of tiles and cobbles. James Radcliffe, 31, Winwick-road, said Mr. Gough was the first to expose goods in front of the cottages. By Mr. Gough : Be remembered other people placing wringing machines outside their doers. Mr. Gough submitted that there had net been a free passage over the boulder pavement or the laud would have been dedicated to the public use. Ha had witnesses who would say that furniture had been exposed on the land for 25 years, and that the landlord bad not in- tended that it should ba dedicated t 3 the public. use. They had a right to the land, as the public nil not re- pair it. Frank Walton said he had known the land for 22 years and goods had always been exposed on it by Mr. Wil- liams, Mr. Gough. and Mrs. Taylor. Mrs. Taylor had a stall on the land for three or four years. By the Town Clerk: He did no:: know that Mr. Gough was convicted in 1891 for exposing his goods on the land. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The magistrates inflicted a fine of 40s. including costs. Defendant was then charged with causing an obstruc- tion by placing goods on the footpath in front of his shop on the 24th October.—Re pleaded not guilty. P.C. Whittaker said he was on duty in Winwick-s;reet at 10.10 a.m, on the above date. He saw a quantity of goods, consisting of an iron safe, table, cr ickery, and oilcloth,in front of the shop on the footpath. The goods were also there at 1.50, and he and P.S. Dean went into the shop and the Sergeant told defendant that he would have to report him for causing an obstruction and he replied "I have been told that many a time "—Ser;zeant Dean gave corroborative evidence, and the defeadaut was fined in the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY COU RT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY COU RT.</p>
        <p>TICURSDAY.—Beforo His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. ALL ABOUT A COAT.—John Daly, farm labourer, Heatley, sued Messrs. Hillier and Co., clothiers, Lvmm, for £2 24, the price paid for a suit of clothes, which it was alleged were not according, t 3 pattern. Mr Browne appeared for the plointiff, and the defendants were represented by Mr. Forshew.—Mr. Browne stated that the plaintiff coate oded that the suit was not of the pattern ordered and that the coat did not fit. Plaintiff gave evidence. —His Honours (to Mr Browne): You don't call evidence to show the coat doesn't fit.—Mr Browne : Plaintiff can put it on row.—His Honour : I an, not a tailor, and I cannot undertake to say whether it fits or not. He can put it on, and you can judge for yourself.—Mr Forshaw• If itdees not fit we will make it. —Plaintiff put the coat on,and his Honour asked him what was the fault.— Plaintiff replied that the sleeves were too long aed wide. —Mr. Forshaw : Did n3t Mr Hillier inform you that he would make it fit? The defecce is that we have executed the order and delivere I the goods. The order was that ' the coat and vest should b 3 of the quality of the pattern, and the coat and vest should be black and the trousers striped. We have car- ried out our contract. When the suit was ordered plaintiff made a eposit of 103., and when on the following Saturday he tried the coat on he seemed perfectly satisfied, and paid the balance of the account. —Walter &amp;heard Hillier said he traied as Hillier and Ce., Lymm. Plaintiff decided to have a black coat and vest, of the quality of the blue pattern he took away. There dil not happen to be a black pattern of that quality in the book. When he tried the coat on he acid the sleeves were a bit to og, and he (defendant) said he would alter them far him. P aintiff was very particular about the clothes, and they were very particular in executing the order.—Mr.Browne ebserved that plaintiff in the first place said he would have blue, and was determined to have blue.—His Honour did not see why the pattern should have hen.' riven if not to denote the colour, and gave judgment for the plaintiff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SOCIETY FOR. PROIIOTING CHRISTIAN</head>
        <p>SOCIETY FOR. PROIIOTING CHRISTIAN</p>
        <p>KNOWLEDGE.</p>
        <p>MEETING A 1' WARRINGTON. A meeting in connection with the on-aentenary of the above society was held in the Co-aperative Hall, Sankey-street, on Monday evening, the chair being taken by the Bishop of Liverrool (Dr Ryle), who vies suppor- ted on the platform by a number of local clergymen and others representing the soaiety. The Bishop of Liverpool said he had come to Warring- ton that night to saY a few words on behalf of the Christian Knowledge Society, a society they all knew I very well by name. But many pesple did not under- stand the work of the soci sty as well as they should do. If they wanted a Bible or a Prayer Book they got it from the society, and the more of these books in the world there were the more Thankful they ought to be. A volume had recently come into his hands which gave a history of the society fr un 1693 to 1893, showing what a valuable work bad been done during that time, and he advised his hearers to re id it whenever they , had an opportunity of doing so. Is ha l certainly taught him many things he had not known before. First and fore- most the Follett, promoted the circulktion of the sorip. turn; and the Prayer Book, and if for nothing else they ought to thank God that such a soafety existed. The society not only published tracts —(laughter) he was not there to speak badly of tracts--but secular and natural knowledge in a cheap form,aa well as the works of holy men who had long ago passed away from this world. The socirry had fo,toer prorneted the interests of the Church of England in every part of the globe. Let them think of the number of bishops they had now —east, west, nor)h, ani south, Hs rernemhered the Pima when they . had only three Or four bishops in Australia, one or two in Indio', one or two In Canada ; now they had from 90 to 100, and it was the S octets for Promoting Christian Knowledge (the oldest Church of England society), that had load something to say and do about the formation of almost every dicc,se in all narts of the world, subscribinz liberally to the funds requirrd. It had also been the rivals of increasing the number of the clergy,of helping to build cathedrals and churahes; it had, in fact, done a great deal of work in many other ways in the interest of true religioo. Allusion was made by his lordship to the queatinn of emigration, and he said that was atso a work in which the society rendered very valuable aid. Ile honed, in conclusion, that the meeting which WAS being held that evening in Warring- ton would be the means cf arousing increased interest on behalf of the society, and that the amlience would derive some goad by hearing of the work of such a USe- ful institutioe as that for Promoting Christian Know- ledge. (Applause.) Speeches were also de'i7ered 1)-., the Rey. J. Bridger, vicar of Rainford. Rev. J. Silhnk, vioor of St. Helens ; and the Rev. W. Robinson. secretary of the Society.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPILOVEMENT SOCIETY. The weekly meeting of the Wesleyan Improve- ment Society was held on Friday week in the vestry of the chapel, when a paper was read by Mr. J. Hodle, entitled " The Mystery of Suffering." An in- teresting discussion followed, in which the follow- ing members took part : Messrs. Shake Sheft, T. H. Ford, H. Blease, Gleave, Pierson, A. Ford, and Ditch field. DEATH OF MR CHARLES RIGBY. The death took place at the Warringtcn Infirmary on Sunday of Mr. Charles Rigby, for many years the Warrington and district representative of Messrs Ind, Coop, and Co. brewers, Burton-on-Trent. Mr. Rigby, who died from COMB, was 55 years of age, and lived at 42, Wilderspool-road. For twenty years he had acted as secretary to the Warrington Shire Horse Society, and the energy devoted to the ' work has tended to further the succes3 of the insti- tution. He was also agent to the Warrington Cor- poration for coricentratri manure. Mr Rigby was at one time a keen sportsmen, and with regularity followed the old Chesil re Rounds. In these pur- suits he gained Many friends, ezpecially in the Chester and Defamere Forest districts, and his loss will be felt by many Cheshire farmers. At one time Mr. Rigby was tenant of the Holly Hedge Farm, Acton Grange. His remeins were buried in Dares- bury Churchyard on Wednesday morning. Two , sons survive him.</p>
        <p>GENERAL NhWS.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that the name of Major Hsterhazy hcs been removed from the roll of the Legion of Honour. The death of General the Right lion. Sir Edward Lugard occurred ou Monday at 19, Marloes-road, Kensington, Loudon.</p>
        <p>Tasihlar Sundarasa Iyer, while on plague duty, has been stoned to death at Hhadudur, on the border of Madras and Mysore.</p>
        <p>The death sedtence passed upon Dr. Whitmarsh has besn respited. The term for which he will be imprisoned is not yet announced. Herrings have been caught in such enormous quantities at Waterford that they have been selling in the open market at 550 for la 6d, or id a dozen. The paddle steamer Spurn was run into and sunk whilst fishing off 1-carborough by the Herman steamer Otom, for Burntisland. The crew were saved.</p>
        <p>Colonel Waring. formerly Commissioner of the New York Street-cleaning Department, has died from yellow fever contracted during a visit to Havana, The British and African Cempany's steamer Calabar has gone ashore at Great Bassa, and it is feared will become c. total wreck. No loss of life is reported. A Macclesfield coroner's jury on Monday returned a verdict of wilful murder against William Repton for causing the death of Mary Murray, with whom he had lived. A Havre telegram says the German steamer Sonneberg, in entering the port, collided with the French steamer Augustin Norman, and the latter was badly damaged. An Englishman named Langworthy has committed suicide at a well-knowa Paris hotel. Having discovered his wife dead in her bedroom, he blew out his brains.</p>
        <p>In the Queen's Bench Division on Monday, Miss Rosins. Mary Ann Ran, a London dressmaker, was awarded £353 damages against Arthur Genese for breach of promise. An inmate of the Saffron Walden Union Workhouse named Wallace was attacked with a hammer on Monday morning by a casual Inmate of the tramp class, and his injuries proved fatal. At Balmoral on Saturday, the Queen presented a set of colours to the 2nd Battalion Queen's Own Cameron High- landers, the ceremony being witnessed by members of the Royal Family.</p>
        <p>At Camila, Crete, on Saturday, fire more prisoners were executed for takinz part in the attack on the uritish trovs on the 6ih ult. Four more barb been sent to penal servitude for twenty years.</p>
        <p>In the Sheriff's Court at Preston on Tuesday, Eliz,beth Knewstubb, dressmaker. Lancaster, was awarded £,•25 dam- ages a2alost George Fletcher Obey, a London clerk, for breach of promise.</p>
        <p>A plague-stricken barque, the Duchess Anne. has arrivedat San Francisco from Hong Kong. The ctptain and one sailor did during the voyage, and two persons on board were down with the disease. Another death from plague—that of Nurse Pecha_haa occurred at Vienna. 'There is now no other known else . the city, but two doctors and two infirmary attendants are at present under observation. Three children of James Irvine, hairdresser. were burned to death it Ann-street, Greenock, on Monday evening. The ' mother had gone out and locked the door. The children, it is supposed, were playing with matches, and set fire to their • clotting.</p>
        <p>A man was arrested at Laniard Fort, Harwich, on sus picion of being a spy in the service of the Russian aovera- • ment, but on inquiry it was found that he was a visitor - from Tpswich, and that he had simply put questions to the sentry out ot curiosity.</p>
        <p>Damage amounting to about £lO,OOO resulted from a fire at Burnley on Tuesday, at the brick, tile, and sanitary wars •' establishment of Messrs. J. Duckett and Sims. The D'ioe- room, with machinery and moulds, was destroyed. The damage done is covered by insurance, A special train conveying British Marines and sailors to British Columbia jumped the track near Winnipeg on 11 Monday, and was thrown down the embankment Two boys 1 from the training-ship Agincourt at Chatham were killed. .11.• stoker and an ordinary seaman were injured.</p>
        <p>According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette." thenumber of Failures In England and Wales gazetted during the week ending October 29th, was 87. The number in the cor- responding week of last year was 89. showinz a decrease of 2, being a net increase in 1893, to date, OF 194. On Saturday, an open hearse containing a coffin come Into violent collision with 'a van in the Borough High-stree' London. The coffin was thrown on to the pavement, and the hearse so damaged that it was necessary to await the arrival of another before the procession was able to proceed. A handsome memorial tablet to the memory of the late Mr. W. E. Gladstone was unveiled on Sunday morning In St. Thomas's Church. Seaforth. in the presence of a lsrge congregation. The Rev. H. S. Sanders, Rector of Bastnor, preached an eloquent sermon of the deceased statesman. In the Divorce Division on Monday. Mrs. Elizabeth Ann Hutoloinson, now living in Leeds. obtained a decree nisi for dissolution of marriage on the ground of the adultery of her husband, who had been porter at Bramley Workhouse, and latterly at School for the Blind, Hardman-street, Liverpool. , The " Globe" learns on cod authority that the contract for the new Admiralty docas and the harbour works at Gibraltar has been given to Messrs. Topharn, Jones, aril Railton, of Westminster. We gather from the estimates that the works are expected to coat between £2,000,000 and £3,C00,000, I</p>
        <p>Miss Ellen Terry's birthplace, in Market-street. Coventry, on Monday had a very narrow e-cape from destruction by fire. The premises next don... occupied by a hatter named William Cleverley, caught fire and t'oe premises were saved after CC:lll3i ierable difficulty. The whole of the contents of the stockroom were destroyed. On Tues•lly the Liverpool police raided a but, or'shed. at the Canada Dock, which, it is alleged, has been systematic- ally used for betting purposes. Seven men were arrected, and token before the stipendiary, when the prinelp ii. who asked to be sent for trial, was remanded. The others were bound over to be of good behaviour. At the Nuueaton pollee court 'on Tuesday, Henry William Burbicl.ze, a cashier, was chaeged with emnezzling E,OO. the moneys of the Ansley Hall Colliery Company. in North Warwickshire. The prisoner left England for Quebec at the commencement of September, and was arrested by the Canadian police. He was remanded.</p>
        <p>A Shanghai telegram reports that another rebellion has broken out against the Pekin Government at a place forty miles south-east of the city of She-si. Frott Pekin it is announced thee two soldiers alleged to have been implicated in the recent attack on English engineers have been flogged In the presence of a member of the British Legation. Sir E J. Reed, sp?aking at Bristol on Eistnrd ty evening, said we had a Navy vastly superior in numbers to France, and of muck superhr quality. It was a question to him whether ths battle would last MAIII7 minutes if the British navy could get alongside a foreign squadron. Ile believed it would be won almost with the first discharge of the power- ful battleships. A man named aficnael Goodwin, a shiner attempted to murder his wife by hitting her over the head with a hatchet In Glasgow on Saturday, and afterwards oarnmitt.ed suicide by cuttiag his throat wills a razor. The man was only re- cently out of prima after being there six months for stabbing his wife. Jealoosiv is said to be the cause of his crime, The woman is in a critical condition.</p>
        <p>• An estraordinary railway acciaent occunei on Monday evening at Penryn, near Falmouth. The London mall train when travelling over an embankment was de.railet and the engine and all the vehicles were overturned. Fortunately they did not roll down the emb ink meat. and though the engine-driver was vverely scalded and the passengers all re, calved a severe shock, no Lves were lost.</p>
        <p>Professor Veritems, enzineer. who invented several elec- trical appliances, loofah's ia coaneetion with lighthouses, committed suicide at Pontoise. France. on Monday, together with his wife. The latter was only 35 years of age. The professo- wee 65. Untottunate tpeculations, resulting in the loss of hit entire fortune. ha 4 +.termlned M. Meritemel to Mire his life and iso Mid little difficulty in persuading his wife to shire his fate. They polsonel themselves with arsenic.</p>
        <p>A disaster to a Great Northern express train near Peter-- borough was only averted on Satur lay morning by the prompt action of some roiiwnv men. Two goods trucks which were being shunted ran off the rails and blocked the main line at the very moment when the express had re- the safety signal to pa,s. The alarm was at onee given to the signalman. who instantly put the signals at danger. Two shouters ran along tloi line swinging red lamps, and the express woe brougat to a standstill close to the obstruction.</p>
        <p>At the Old Bailey, London. on Tuesday, Sohn Neil, des- cribed as a drill instructor, and Marion Braley, of no occupa- tion, were brought up for sentence for procuring Margaret Mee Conway to lead an immoral life in the Went End.— Police-inspeotor Richardson said that a gentleman who went to the house where Neillived iii Poland street had complained that a ,oyereign W4S demanded front him, and that a huge mastiff was Pet on him bv a bully. was sentenced to twelve months' and /Paley to nine months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>Rain fell Irsayily in many pats of Brigland and Scotland on Tuesday nicht and on Wednes tav morning. and was auiompanied by a strong gale. In the Lakr digtriet there were heavy flOO l°. 61W.19111Z districts in the Keswick nolghbourhood were seblnergel. Derwentwater and Bee- senthwaite bscarne unitel in one great lake. At Kendal the water was higher than in the record flood of 1874. Stramon- gate, lice street leading from the centre or the town to the railway btAtlon. was covered to a depth of four or five feet. fe.t, and tome extraordinary scenea were witnessed. Our correspondents st ste that two bridges were carried away.that farm builAings were partly desixoyea, and that the damage dono by water z4uld not be leas than £IO,COO.</p>
