        <p>IN many respects the pre- Our Position in sent opportunity is a good one Egypt. for calmly examining our posi- tion in Egypt. We remain in occupation under a promise to retire when our mission is accomplished. The restoration of financial and social order was our declared aim, and to this we have added the recovery by Egypt of her complete control over the conquered provinces of the Soudan. The work is still in progress. It is the fault of France if difficulties have been raised respecting one ol the provinces. It will be her fault if the larger Egypt- ian question is brought forward, and this country is compelled to assume a clear and bold attitude respecting it. We hold a trump card in our hands. If France makes unacceptable demands, if she in- spires the KIiEDIVB to be reckless and anti-British, ' and if she thinks that she can count upon Russia in her plans, we must play that card. That I is, we must proclaim our Protectorate, and support the proclamation by a big naval demon- stration. We are obviously getting the naval demonstration ready, and most of us hope that it will have to be made. We desire to see our posi- tion in Egypt regularised, and to let Europe know that we shall not tolerate French intrigues, whether they take the form of secret expeditions to the Nile, intrigues with Abyssinia, or political tumult at Cairo. The whole of the country is united on this point at least. There are no bold advocates for evacuation. A feeble voice or two may be raised here and there, but that is all. It is now obvious that we cannot clear out of Egypt as the French cleared out of Syria. Our interests have been increasing ever since we put down the Arabi rebellion. We have not always been satis- fied that some of these augmentations were com- patible with our promises and our policy. But France in her desire to get us out of Egypt, has made it impossible for us to retire with honour and safety. The last effort she has made has brought matters to a climax, because it shows that she would not hesitate to plot against Egypt if it served her interests in Central Africa or afforded her any gratification in embarrassing this country. Hence the quick ripening of public opinion. Baron DB COURCEL says that he detects a hardening of the heart of Lord SALISBURY within the last fortnight. It is probably true, and it corresponds with the progress which has been obviously going on in the public mind. The re- sult is what we have already indicated. Our anomalous position has become inconvenient and well-nigh intolerable. We desire to see an end put to the reproaches of critics and the hesita- tions of politicians who remember our promises. A Protectorate exists de facto. What is impor- tant and urgent is to convert it into a Protector- ate dejure. We have to inform Europe of our intentions, to support them by a great naval demonstration, and to risk the enmity of France, whom we have been vainly endeavouring to appease by surrenders elsewhere. The opportunity and duty are great.</p>
        <p>Oun opinions respecting the aims MciP4l of municipal government have Duds. been largely modified of late years, in correspondence with our ideas of Im- perial administration, which have also changed considerably. We favour a more pertinacious and detailed intervention by the State in matters affecting industry, public health, and other cog- nate subjects. We are for reducing the control of the central government in municipal affairs, and for restricting the operations of private companies. The municipalities are eager to accept larger re- sponsibilities, and to go back to some of the civic ideals which made the older cities of the world almost States in themselves. There is a good deal of resistance to be encountered, and it will proba- bly increase instead of lessening as we push the powers of municipalities into such matters as trams and telephones, to say nothing of the con- trol of the traffic in intoxicants. It is important we should have a clear notion of the direction in which we are travelling. Sir H. FOWLER has laid down two points of importance with this end in view. Enterprises requiring the breaking up of streets should be within direct municipal control, and all enterprises undertaken by the municipality should be for the common good. The first poiat includes gas, water, trams, and telephones. The second iccludes a greater variety of matters, as parks, libraries, labour bureau; art g alleries, schools, the control. of the drink traffic and many branches of food supply,with improved regulations for dealing with distress and pauperism. - -</p>
        <p>i - - i Municipalisation, n this form, s the revival of an old ideal. The city was a state of itself, of ten absolutely independent of anything external. We cannot hope to build up municipalism into this- complete form. The nation must have some con</p>
        <p>trol in reserve. There must be Imperial laws; there must be supreme authority. But the municipality must be entitled to control within its own area all enterprises and interests essential to a healthy and prosperous community. ,Granted that, and we shall hardly be much concerned about details, because they will harmonise themselves with current opinion. There will be advancing and there will be stationary localities. The local bodies cannot move in advance of local opinion. It is still essential that less legislation should be required t a con- solidate local interests and powers. As it is, too many private bills are required to accom- plish local objects, and there are too many ob- stacles in the way of comprehensive reforms. When we have time, it is desirable to recast the powers of municipal bodies, and to consolidate the laws respecting them. The power to deal with public-houses might very well be entrusted, under certain reserves, to municipal bodies. Possibly, very few would care to undertake such serious responsibilities ; but some of thetn would, and we believe that it is in this direction we shall reach an equitable solution of existing problems. The municipal idea is a noble one, and we cannot make it too comprehensive if we maintain our present temper. The aim is to make a community healthy orderly, progressive, and to cheaply administer public services in the public interest. Any at- tempts that fall short of this ideal are not to be commended.</p>
        <p>There seems to be no driving into the heads of some of our dispensers of justice (?) the correct interpretation of the conscience clause in the new Vaccination Act. In consequence cf this magis- terial obtuseness a number of conscientious objec- tors had been hardly dealt with. One of them, who deserves the thanks of his brother anti-vac- cinators, has declined to accept the fiat of the Stipendiary Magistrate of Sheffield, who required to be satisfied that vaccination would do harm to the child on behalf of whom exemption was claimed. This objector took the matter into the Divisional Court and this week applied for a man- damus against the magistrate, which was granted.. The Court at the same time pointed out that it was the parent's, and not his own conscientious belief as to which the magistrate must be satisfied. The terms of the conscience clause appear to us too simple to admit of all the contortion they are sub- jected to, and we feel convinced that in many cases the opinion of the magistrate with regard to vaccination itself influence him in the interpre- tation he puts on the wording of the Act. If the objector and applicant for exemption had only been required to take the oath as to his conscienti- ous objection, then all these difficulties would have been obviated, for no alternative would be left the magistrates but to grant the certificate. But as it is the conscience of an applicant gets scant consideration in many quarters—which is obviously unfair. We have no inclination to take up the cudgels on behalf of anti-vaccinators, but we insist that the new Act should have a fair trial and the conscientious objector a just and im- partial hearing.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Wright, in adjudicating on the con- tempt of court motions arising oat of Mr. Hoo bankruptcy, has not after nll drawn a blank, as was freely predicted. Mr. Rucker smarts under the loss of £2OO, and also law costs that cannot be inconsiderable. In a previous case substantial justice was net by the payment of costs alone. Mr. Hooley's evidence, while not insincere, was recognised as probably faulty. It was the same thing over again on Tuesday. The Judge did not feel safe in acting upon the ex-millionaire's evid- ence, and had there been no other incriminating circumstances forthcoming it is presumable that Mr. Rucker would have escaped lightly, or per- haps have escaped altogether. The public will no doubt feel that the fine was a just one. No doubt Mr. Rucker and his friends can plead many cir- cumstances in extenuation of the fault that was committed. It was to the interest of the com- pany's shareholders that their directors should not be" drawn through the mud." A good name counts for much with the investing public, and once lost it is difficult to recapture. But this theory that the good name of a company and its directors must be safeguarded cannot_ excuse a wilful attempt to smother a public examination which has been instituted for public behoof and benefit. In short, care for the interests of a com- pany can be carried too far; it must not be allowed to overlap the general weal. Mr.Rucker, we pre- sume, will learn that lesson well, and though the cost has been rather heavy it is not disproportion- ate to the gravity of the offence.</p>
        <p>The man of the moment is Sir Horatio Her- bert Kitchener, Sirdar of the Egyptian Army, and the hero of Khartoum. His return to England last I Thursday was triumphant. Dover and London crowds, metaphorically went down on their knees and worshipped him, while the scene at Victoria Station was almost past belief. Excitable Lon- doners surged in thousands round the exits of the station and on the platforms, cheering themselves hoarse, and causing their idol an immense amount of inconvenience. Sir Herbert is, by reputation, a modest man, and acted up to his remark that he would rather tackle another Omdurman than face I the receptions prepared for him, by escaping down a side street and avoiding the ovation awaiting him along the anticipated line of route. The old fighting spirit of the Briton is as much a dominant I force now as in the days of Drake and Wellington. Notwithstanding the quietening influences of these alleged" piping times of peace" an English- man's blood is still fired by valorous deeds done in the name of the old country,and he cannot refrain from wild exultation and hero-worship when the conqeeror of even a horde of dervishes returns to his native land. It is just as well that the com- bative spirit of the Anglo-Saxon race should be fostered and at times find vent, or the greatest nation on earth might find itself relegated to a subordinate position.</p>
