      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ATHLETICS,</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS,</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALT.</p>
        <p>To see Warrington iu a scoring mood is a treat very rarely accorded the frequectors of the Wilderspool ground nowadays tut on Satur lay this extreme Oa -- sure was afforded the 4,000 people who had been en- couraged by the victory at Rochdale to ve for thera• selves what Warrington really could do. And they were not disappointed. The " Wirepullers " treated the crowd to one of their very best displays of Norzdern Union football. Tueir opponents, Tyldesley, were scarcely expnted to give Warrington very much trouble but that tuev wouil te trounc-q1 to th. extent of 21 points to 4, wa; Lot anticipited. Yet this really hlP- pened, and Warcingt ma's dashing, epedy play was res- ponsible for thi handsome score of 3 goals, fire tries every point being we-earned, and the outcome of good football.</p>
        <p>Since I was at Itcchdale there have been very foic dry periods, and it looked odds on the dele:ge of the pre- vious Saturday being rep3ated at Warrineton. But Jupiter Pluvius was kind, and turned off the tap an hour or two before 3-30. The drying capabilities of Oe Wilderspool turf were well exercised, ad by the time for the commencement of hostilit es the field was in exe+ lent playing order. There vv,s scarce'y any breez ,and the afternom no beiqg to warm was well suited fOr the fast, open game which was witnessed. Tylde6. are old and respected opponents of Warrington. In the West Lancashire Cup days thy were one of the rroni- est Rugby clubs in Lancashire, and always uscd to give Warrington a good game or a good: t'irashiug. Saxe the advent of the Northern Union, however, much of "The Bongers" glory has departed. Their close,scrum. maging style of play proved ill adap'ed for the ri.'w rules, and it was not long are they began to accept lick - nags from nearly every club is the League. Last year they met Warrington in the opening match of the rs, mon at Tyldesley and won by 19 points to nil. In the re. turn encounter they were defeated by 9p- hits to nil, so that with a win and a draw in the year before, they still possessed a margin of points against Warrington. That margin, I think, has been converted into deficit at last. Warrington are amply revenged for the crushing defeat of more than twelve months ago.</p>
        <p>Tyldesley are yet a big, sturdy lot, but weight is by no means everythiag now. Uuless speed and resource are combined with forward strength, the heavy scrim.' magers are not an unmixed blessing. They proved rather an encumbra^ce on Saturday. In the scrimmage itself they could render 'a good account ot therm•evee but once the ball got looseo and they were quite bewll' dered by the activ ty of their lighter opponents. I re" callect one of my predecesson reporting an encounter between Warrington and Barrow, at the time wiled Warrington were a strong lot. He headed his report of the match with the phrase "Warrington Strength V. Barrow Science,"—and it is said the howl of rag, that went up from insulted Warringtm could be heard all over Lancashire. It was certainly injudicious cf the reporter, and I believe, scarcely correct. At any rate he went about in fear and trembling of the aforesaid "strength "for some time Last Saturday the boot wee on the other leg. Tyldesley Strength was opposed to Warrington Science. Man for man the visitors were much the heavier all round, but they were quite out class‘d in the finer points of the game.</p>
        <p>Five minutes from the start Warrington were credited with a try, and on decidedly goad terms with them. selves. Makin, the ldesley custodian, made a couple of mulls. He was allowed to recover from the first, but the second time the home forwards bore d)wn on tile unprotected ball and flustered full-back like ts! avalanche. Both were dribb.ed over the line and dropped on, u try being awarded the man on the balls who happened to be Swift—both by name and nature. Great jubilation prevailed, which was slightly dashed when Mason failed to put the ball ovtr. Onee shown the way to the line, and Warrineton were continually travel- ling there. A score bad been narrowly missed 00 several occasions, when the ball was passed out to Fish in mid-fiPld. The diminutive right"- winger electrified everybody by a brilliant and daring feat. Though faced by half-a-dozen oppor- ents he began a cork-screw journey to the line, made rings—m-taphorically and actually—round the Tyldesley backs, and finally crossed the 11-ne wtn one of the cleverest tries ever seen on tt e Warrington ground. What a cheer there was ! It it was not beard all over Lancashire, it was too big tor Warringtin to hold and travelled far and wide. The failure riltha goal-kick could not lessen the uproar. Hardly had tile game got going again before Fish completed another pet' sing movement by scoring near the corner. He wal recalled however, the ball having been sent a little fete ward. Sensations were still not at an end, for I:her wood receieine at the "25 " calmly popped the oral over the bar. Then the" Bongers " hal a look in, and getting to the other end et the field were awarded a. penalty kick, ard France landed a good goal. B•fore the interval Bate beat his opponents and ran to the visitors quarter. From the succeeding play he charged a kick down and added another try to tlie list, Mason this time placing a eplendid goal from touch. Thirteeo points stood to the homesters' credit when the interval arrived.</p>
        <p>Tyldesley looked like making a fight of it for Gil first quarter of the second half. Little advantage accrued to Warrington from a bout of exchange kicking, %hilt' France made a couple of strong dashes for the line; Warrington at last got int.) concerted actio again. arP the ball ranidly travelled to Fieb,who was hemmed in 011 the touch-line and sent the ball again among the f 01.7, wards who continued the movement in fine style unt" . Deakin capped all with a try near the posts. Anoth`f goal came from the toe of ALoon. Warrington seemed to rest on their laurels for a 'pen, and G. Berry rgeized the opportunity to rneh over from a paso,which alas foe, Tyldeeley,was adjudged to be forward. The visitors di`" succeed in notching a second goal, but before the 008'3 Warrington roused up and Holcroft dashed in ne tba corner with the fifth try of the day.. "Time " arrived with the board denoting 3 goals, 5 tries for Warringtoll, au l 2 goals for Tyldeeley.</p>
        <p>Once again is it difficult to single out the bee nr moo the Warrington side. The team is showing such fro- provement all round that every man has a share in the' praise. Fish is certainly coming out, and bids fair be a most valuable acquisition. Holcrott maintains 131 s improvement, and I shall certainly have to alter madf previous opinion of him. He is developing into a real', good wing. Isherwood was not seen at his best on Satyr' day. He relied tlo much on himself, and stuck to ttiv ball on numerous occasions when he shmld have passed. Haram was also at fault eccaidonally, and will have f° check his eagerness or he will some day make a WO!, blunder. Bate again proved him title to being the VS", half back in Lancashire, while no fault could be Nun,' with his assistant Ashunt. The forwards all played IP brilliant game, and followed up wi..h wonderful sPee4' Deakin, Taylor, Fairhurst, and Naylor were the beer, though the others woe scarcely inferior.</p>
        <p>--- The Tyldesley team is decid.dlv i unwieldir and could . be much improved by ths addition of 'picker men i 6 the front rank. At half they are not baaly represen'eut while G. Berry and Fiance can p'av a very serviceable.. game in the threequartsr line. Makia was a failure 61° full back all through the game. The positions of the clubs and the results of matcbe up to date in the Lancashire L agul are as follows :.-- Ron Against. . P. W. L. V. G. T. P. G. T. r ' Widnes - 70.. 4 3- it.-14 Broughton Rang. 8... 7... 1... 0...15 26-108.. 3 3- 10-• 4 Salford - 58.- 3 3-- 15.-lz Swinton - 95 .. 3 2- 12-. 'I lo lu Oldham - 79... 6 9- --' g Warrington - 45... 3 10- *•• 6 St. Helene - 70... 3 13- 42,:' g Le'gb 9... 4... 5... 0...15 16- 78- 6 18- bl" 6 Tydlealey 9... E.- 6... 1... 6 7- 33.13 22- 8.1."' 6 Runcorn - 12... 5 12- !!!." 4 Wigan - 39...17 13- p_ • - 4 Rochdale Hornets 7... 2... 5... 0... 6 6- 27...14 20- Stockport 6... 0... 6... 0... 2 4- 16...18 21- 9w-. 0 Morecambe - 7...11 24- 94••</p>
        <p>I regret to have to say that J. T. Bate has &amp;GOO; to play for Lancashire, though pressed to do so. 08 13,8 n several reasons for his refusal, but they are not avor'll particularising. It will be a source of regret to.ae lovers of the g ame in Warrington that their favourlt., half-back should refuse to assist his ccunty wber;gl3„jo services are urgently required. Bate is doing hitnael'by good by this refusal, and I must confess that I th°°. be would have pocketed his ot jections on this occaso,?; and gone to the as.istance of the Red Rose represen, e dyes. His decision not t I play causes A. Lees to. lor6, his place, and the vacancy behind the scrimmage 0'11„), Blind by Messenger and Nelson, (Broughton It4ot-re neither of whom is up to county form at present. will no doubt be a good many Warriugtonians at Salf°;ci this S.rturday but there would have been far more ifl the Wearington skipper teen taking his proper par" the fray.</p>
        <p>R. Bate has been put down in " Smack's " Pit oe the team, to do duty against ?dillona, the rest 0, backs being the same as as week.</p>
        <p>---- (bt Fletcher Russell'a plocsd another win to their Cre ig on Saturday last, bebt,og S. El•-lens Wlrte Rose bio, e points to nil. St. 1:1( lens commenced operations hour behind the time and Fetchers at once forCeL,;l76 game, Scct7, dashing over in the first five minute,- ictio ' next try was obtain?d by 'W,Evans—, pew ocroic 0 ~, has only to keep up Battu lay's fcrm to starnP t0ra,80.3 : 0°,(3 first class threequarter. 11. Scott, who played a C31,1:3 13,', half back game, was rfsponsible tor three trie3 81;9 ur ' was ably assists d by Fodt n,who knows al, the triC"„pe: the trade. W. Evans scored two tries and widees Radford ,d To-day Fletchers' visit Widnes White Star at will have all all their work cut out to Orin 2 P away with them, •.</p>
