      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>A</p>
        <p>RUNCoRN AN: 1) W1)NI.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY COURT. I: On Tuesday, His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes held , the usual monthly court at Runcorn. There were 14 - 'Alaints, 2 default, 1 adjourned, and 16 judgment sum- - Inonses, heard before the registrar (Mr. W. D. Jolliff). : There;was no business of importance before his honour. ASSAULT WITH A BASIN AT WIDNES. On Tuesday at Liverpool County Quarter Sessions, . Mary Ann MOSS (27), of no occupation, pleaded guilty 1 : to having assaulted and wounded James Sheehan with : x basin, at Widnes, on the Ist October, and was sen- tenced to one month's imprisonment. DRUNK IN HIGH-STREET. On Wednesday morning, at tho Runcorn Police Station, before Mr. F. J. Norman, John Howard was ' brought up charged with being drunk in High-street, and was fined lOs and costs or 14 days.—Eloward had been previously convicted. ST. Pages P.S.E. WIDNES. A large audience assembled in the parochial rooms on 'Saturday night, Mr. J. Williams occupying the chair. The speaker for the evening, Mr. J. W. Davies, gave an excellent discourse upon temperance, taking for his sub- ' ject 4. Sunday Closing, and Local Veto." There was the usual programme, the musical items being much appre- '-ei sted . BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS. On Wednesday evening the members of this class were accorded a fine literary treat, when Mr. F. Littler read a paper on "Some British Methods of Civilisation." At the conclusion of the reading Mr. Littler was accorded hearty applause. The speakers who followed were :—Messrs. F. G. Millingtm, W. Unsworth, A. Dodd etc. Mr. Simister presided, but the attendance was °WV fair.</p>
        <p>MILTON P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the usual open service last Sunday afternoon, notwithstanding the inclement weather. The chair was taken by Mr. F. La wrinson. The soloisits, Meesrs. Thomas and G. Lacy, gave good renderings of their respective pieces, being heartily ap• plauded. The speaker, Rev. M. H. Chapman, gave an excellent address on " Everyday business a divine call- . nage* SUNDAY SCHOOL ASSOCIATION MEETING AT WIDNES. The half-yearly meeting of the Prescot Rural Deanery Sunday School Association was held at Widnes on Satur- day afternoon. The Vicar of Widoes (Rev. W. Hodgson) Presided over a large attendance. Mr. J. Bird, of Fresh- field, gave an excellent address upon the teaching of 'l- imits and junior scholars. In the evening a special ter- /tee was held:in St. Paul's Church. RUNCORN SHORTHAND WRITERS' ASSOCIATION. On Monday evening, the Provisional Committee of the above Association, met for the purpose of framing rul es, etc., at the Technical Institute, Runcorn. It was intimated that Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., had consented to b President, and several other influential gentle. „las, n had consented to become Tice-Presidents, Draft "e,ee were submitted and agreed to. It was decided to _l*ot:emend that the Session extend from the let October to the 31st March in each year. That ladies and gentlemen be admitted as members, and that the district include Widnes. At the general meeting te be held on the 14th November, the float arrangements will be completed and the society will commence its work. MEMORIAL SERVICE AT WIDNES. On Sunday evening a memorial service was held in the St. Ambrose Church, Widnes, with reference to the late Mr. Harrison, organist of the chui eb. The church rM crowded, much sympathy haying been expressed ,eler the family of the deceased in their sad bereavement: 205 e present included Mrs. Harrison and family, Mr. 13Thomas Harrison, Liverpool, brother of the decea.el, Mid the members of the Hearts of Oak Ledge. The Rev. .14 Bert, vicar of the church, preached from the text, "Be ye also ready." He referred in an affectionate !harmer to the gentlemanliness and the good life of the ',lts Mr. Harrison. The special Psalms, xxxix. and x0.,1 zer the burial of the dead, with hymns appropriate to , 111 i e occasion, were sung, the concluding hymn being ' Days and moments quickly flying." The service was .441.2ccluded with Chopin's Funeral March, Mr. Riley offi- "stieat at the organ. A SCENE IN BRIDGEWATER-STRKEY. /1311 Monday, at the Runcorn Police Court, Annie beollias a was charged with being drunk und disorderly in urieeewater-street, en the 30th October.—P.C. Gregory Stated that on Sunday night, at 9 45, he received a com- Plaiat about some women and sailors being in a house 1:4 Handley'e-eouvt. off Bridgewater-street. He went to G house, in company with P.C. Smith, and found ware* women and six sailors on the premises, together With a man named Haddock, the landlord. A girl 16 Years of age sat on a sailor's knee. They turned the sailors and the women out of the place, but the prisoner, e*..120 was among the number, refused to go and com- menced using bad language, so much so that they were obliged to lock her up She was drunk.—The Chairman !Dad it was a disgraceful state of things on Sunday even- ille.—The prisoner was committed to prison for ons 122antb's hard labour. BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE. On Monday evening last, in Brunswick Schoolroom, a PUblic meeting was held under the presidency of the .Itev. C. W. L. Christien in connection with the above. , There was an exceptionally good attendance notwith- tending the unPropit does weather. The Chairman, in, an interesting and impresive address showed that drunkenness was often associated with danger. debt. dirt, disease and death. Mr. T. Simister, in a few well fhellen remarks, showed the beneeti arisen from total Abatinence, and advised the young people present to: Sign the total abstinence pledge that night. During the e'en:Meg-Bungs. violin solos, glees and recitations were tttsclered in which the following took part Hall- ding, Messrs. W. A. Unsworth, and S. S. Robinson, Lilly Faulkner, Annie Spra-ing, Lizzie Tickle, Willie .I,,antlrison, James Lea, Alfred Lea arid John Parry. Mrs. ' 1,,7 Pickthall accompanied with her usual ability. At eele close of the meeting a good number ot pledges were taken. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. ...The usual fortnightly meeting of the above association held in the schoolroom, Greenway-road, on Satur- `MY evening. Mr. Edward Key occupied the chair. tfter the routine besiness had been transacted, Mr. t.eartiel Bisbrown said that since their last meeting their Ftvision had sustained a loss, and he was sure that their alterts would go out in sympathy for the bereaved one. of their energetic workers, Mr. Thomas Clarke, had 1.41 his wife be death. She was a woman whe was 4sPecked and beloved by all who knew her. In manner- cn with the deplorable accident at the Morp.th Dock, tile of their friends aria brothers had been drowned, viz., 17er. Arthur Williams. To know him was to respect him. re, Was a good man, and held in high esteem by his lends. He was well grounded Intemperance principles. When very young, he entered the cadet order, and sub- Zclaently he was transferred to the adult society, and ever been a constant Son of Temperance. Ha had .7.ew suddenly cut down in the midst of life. He was Zee he was voicing the feelings of the whole of the mem- MI in expressing sympathy with the family. The ',..Tewing officers also expressed their deep sympathy itb the bereaved relatives :—Mesars. John Jones, "erglas J: Holt, James Johnson, and Thomas Whyte.</p>
        <p>PEARL LIFE ASSURANCE COMPANY. %CM Tuesday night, at the Masonic Hotel, the staff of tlin. above Company (Runcorn District) met together to al- bomber of about 30, and under the presidency of Mr. Iry Higgins (the district Superintendent), spent quite o:ouvivial evening. After justice had been done to a ,FDital tea provided by Mr. Peter Corns, games and t4illefing were indulged in the duties of master of cere. - Well being fulfilled in, Mr. William Brienelew in a ;refectory manner.—ln an address delivered during ae.° "Wag Mr. Henry Higgins said that the Pearl As. tlaranee ACompany was practically only a young one,and °tile been in altistencel for about 32 years. But i;;Lblr that time it had made considerable progress, and at 4 years alone it had mode £2OO increase in erensiums. Which was up to the present time the greatest I,,,trease made since the formation of the company. t:Uan he came to the Runcorn District about 9 years ago Rib" were only two collectors.,and the district was a abli,4l.l)trict of St. Helens. It bad progressed so remark- di,4 knee then, that at pressnt there was a superinten- en-P two assistant superintendents, aid 16 collectors ; he himself was district superintendent over 44 divi- giOps COniprising six countiss.—Mr. John Price (assistant 41944%1 superintendent) who was also present gave an ta the collectors present brimful of valuable 14.4.ortriation as to the way a book should be worked..-. te"er the usual votes of thanks, etc., the meeting di - tired, a most enjoyable evening haying been spent. tiNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE —LECTURE BY THE ART MASTER. kr?n Monday evening Mr. 'l'. Ilanmer Hutchings, the a Master at the Technical Institute, gave a lecture and 4ilanstration in clay modelling in the lower room of the ' Institute. There was a ederate attendance. Mr. Hutchinzs explained tflat the lecture ha I .been promoted. to help tile rig class, and with a view to Increasing the of students. The Technical Instruction C am- irt“lttee had made pr nara•iores for a larger number of lai:l4entB than were attending, and they had provided a jaz.set tannber of examples for mod,llinrr. Anether ob. ttpte jf the lecture was to endeavour to form an applied tilin 'alias the students o; which would take up such broir as re•peus:6 m-,tal work, wood carvirig,ern- Vras to Start &amp;c, i and decorative painting. The third des start a sketelinc, club, to be worked by the `t'atits of the school. Pi the course of. his lecture. Mr. focrrn. lagB dealt with (1) S utter of plant Meow With a vi iw to their use for orna- • Purposes and flat ornament, as in ett4t.'-ulstinct'on to work in relief. The slide illu- ,ll°,,Tit Of fiat ornament included examples of s'ene- etf,-s:ca,rgna for printed plaques, &amp;c. (2) An intermed:- %11 'ata.where the ornament was neither flat nor yet IR Of ttelief, toe illustratons of these including examples and "le Weak and pate- mr-pa' e, decor tted clay ware. tenernfectioners' sugar icings. (3) Ornament in full leateerelaY modelling, wood carving, and embossed ROrne ,• The lecture was strikingly illustrated with 'xcellent lantsrn slider, the lantern being skilfullY • ',lent °td by Mr. Clement Handler. Some a the ations were those of tbe lecturer's own handl.</p>
