      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>MONTH FOR WIFE DESERTION AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>A MONTH FOR WIFE DESERTION AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes yesterdaY,Joseph Mitchelmore was charged with deserting his wife and three children, leayibig them chargeable t 3 the common fund of the Prescot Union. The prisoner pleaded guilty. The prisoner left his wife and family c,n the 25tra of Mar, and since that time he had not communicated vrith the guardians. The total cost amounted to £l9 83. The pris mer left his work on the 9th of May on his own accord. At that time he was earning 28i. a week. The prisoner, who slid his wife was a bad, drunken woman, was sent to gaol for one month.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED WOUNDING WITH A BRICK NEAR WIDNES. Yesterday afternoon, at Widnes Petty Sessions. bsfore Major Lewis, Edward Hastings, Ditton, was charged with having unlawfully wounded Daniel Gilbride,a fore- man at the Broughton Cnpper works, Ditto's, who re- sides in Ditton-road, Ditton. Last Saturday night about eleven o'clock, Gilbride's servant girl, wred 16 years,was returning home after having bsen on an errand, when prisoner accosted her and commenced pulling her about. She told him to leave her alope, and list then Gilbride came up and told the prisoner to Als we the girl alone' On seeing that he was being followed by complainant, prisoner threw a brick at him striking him on the head and causing a lacerated wound two inches long, pene- trating to th s bone. Gilbrido was renderad unconscious and wnen he recovered consciousness the prisoner had gone. Mr. T. J. Peters, solicitor, defended prisoner, who gave evidence and said that he simply walked home with a girl, and afterwards Gilbride followed him, knockod him down, and kicked him. Prisoner was com- mitted to Livorpool County Quarter Sessions for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>DEATH FROM OVER-EXERTION.</head>
        <p>DEATH FROM OVER-EXERTION.</p>
        <p>A SAD CASE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Runcorn Police -Station, Mr. J. E. Worsley, held an inquiry on the body of Mrs. Mary Wright, wife of Mr. William Wright, 25 Rutland-atreet, 12 who died suddenly on Friday evening last. 'right, 26, Rutland-street, Duke's fields, said Duke's field S, he was the husband of the deceased. His wife was 67 years of age and had been in poor health for some time, and had a very bad cough. The cough remained with her after the doctor had been dispensed with. If his wife hurriiid herself at all the cough got worse, but he did not think it was anything serious. On the Friday deceased bad been sawing a piece off the bottom of the door, and had to go down on her knees to do so. He wanted to take the door off its hitiges, but deceased per- sisted in doing it herself. She told him to go out into the yard flt a smoke, and he went. Hardly Mires minutes afterwards his wife came running out to him and blood wa, flowing from her mouth. He sent for a 'doctor and Dr. McLennan came. His wife was dead before Dr. McLennan arrived.—Eliz tbeth Hopkins, wife of Mr. Simon Peter Hopkins, 21, Rutland-street, said she had been a neighbour of the dirteased's for over twenty years. She remembered some 18 months ago when deceased had a similar attack. At that time it was said that she had broken a blood vessel. It was then attri- buted to the fact that deceaaed had besn sick. Witness could not tell who attended her. Deceased had been very ill for some months and suffered from a very bad cough. She had no I.medical attendant, and used to "doctor" herself, first taking one thing then another. Witness always noticed that the deceased breathed badly when over-exertel. On goinq into Mr. Wright's house she stw deceased foaming blood very Ueely. She sent for the doctor but deceased died before he cam 6 When she went into the house first,there was a little girl in the room. She spoke to the little girl and she said that" Oranima cough id and bl aol came oit of her mouth."—The Coro er in summing, uo consid tried that death was due to the breaking of a blood vessel din to over exertion. It was clear that she persisted in sawing the Mace off the door, and there was no doubt that tbe exertion brought on hemorrhage. He would advise them to bring lea verdict of .death from "Natural caus 39 due to the breaking of a blood vessel."---Yne jury oon• curred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CAMDEN WESLiAAN BAZIAR. LADY BRUNNEEt PERwoßvis THE OPENING CEREMONY. FIRST DAY.</head>
        <p>CAMDEN WESLiAAN BAZIAR. LADY BRUNNEEt PERwoßvis THE OPENING CEREMONY. FIRST DAY.</p>
        <p>On Wedneeday.afternoon, in the presence of large gathering, Lady Bruuner opened a three dap/ bazaar, promoted in aid of the Camden Wesleyan Church and Sunday School at the Camden Wesleyan Cbapel, Lowland's-road, Runcorn. It was intended that the bazaar should have been opened by Sir John T. Brunner, Bart., M.P. but at the last moment Sir John found himself unable to leave London, so Lady Brunner kindly consent e 1 to perforce the ceremony in his stead. 'The proleedings commenced at 3 p.m., whe 3 the Rev. J. A. B. Malvern read a telegram which had been received from Sir John Brune'. The telegram steted that Sir John Brunner had b ien eta tined in London, and ha i ask od Lady Brunner to reprirsant him at the bazaar. He was also pleased to intimate that he had hid a suc- cessful interview with the authorities of South Kensing- ton in reference to Runcorn affairs. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Malvern felt that they whilst regretting that Sir John wee detained, would accord Lady Brunner a hearty welcome amongst them. (Applause.)—After the singing of the opening hymn, and prayer by the Rev. R. G. Clifton, the Rev. C. W. L. Christien (superinteedent of the circuit) introduced Lady Brunner. In the course of his remarke he dwelt upon the influence and large amount of good that was done by the fetnalis community in the Church. They were very glad to see Lady Brunner amongst them, (Applause). Lady Brunner slid she was very sorry that her hus- band bad been prevented from b ting present. Had h 3 been present they would have had a epee eh from him. She was very much interested in all that Mr. Christien had said. That kind of work brought people together in a good cause. It was to be hoped that a god, sub- stantial sum would be realised, as the Sunday schools gore excellent institutions. It was pleasing to note that there was quite a change from what used to be the case, and that now it was getting impossible to keep children away from Sunday Sceools. (Hear, hear). She had much pleasure in formally declaring the bazaar open. Mr. P. Speakman proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Lady Brunner for seeming to open the bazaar. Mr. A. R. Norman seconded, and pail a high tribute to the interest manifested by Sir John and Lady Brunner in all the good work in the constituency. It only seemed as if Sir John was &amp;sinus that all his money should be directed into a channel for doing good. (Rear, hear). Mr. James Pritchard supported very heartily, and the vote was aceorded with acclamation. Lady Brunner, responded, and was afterwards pre- sented with two beautiful b ruquets of 113weri, by two little girls (Misses L. McNorton and K. R swlieson), on behalf of the Bazaar Committee. The room was got up in the usual way, and though not so replete with articles as might be expected, b evidences of generous and zealous work on the part of those responsible for thr carrying out of the venture. There were also several " side shows" of the nature of competitions, forty le-telling, and lime light effects etc. The officials were : Chairman, Rev J A B Malvern; vice•chairenen. 8 C B ick .tt, Esq., and A Harrison,Esq. ; secretaries, C Ditchfiel I and 8 Littler ; treasurer. J Dutton ; general committee, Messrs W Ackerle9e S Bar- ley, John Bazley, C Itradahaw, A Dutton. S Dutton, F Duncalf, R Davies, T Holt, H Liam, C Hanker 3, J Han- kers J L Littlemere.W Lambert. WLittler, T Millinger. J R Abbole, T Roe, R Marsh, T Sweetman, J IV Temper- ley. W Title, W Walker, L Woodward, H Worrell, Mrs EI Pere, Mrs P Torndemen, Misses Suakin, J Crosby, Teeter, L Temperley, J W sod ward. and the etallholders. Stallholders: Church stall: Mrs Malvern,Mrs Harrison, Mrs Millinge-. Mrs va,s`i, Mrs Santley, Mrs Woo Award, Mrs Wilkes. School stall: Miss Chatiwick,Miss Dobson, Mrs J puttee, Mrs Littlemore, Miss J Crosby, Miss N Littler. Refreshmtsnt Stall : Mr 3 Morris,Mrs Beckett,Mr J Bazley, Mrs Britland, Mrs Bell, Mrs Craddock, Mrs i Flent.Mrs G Littler,Mrs J W Temperley. Young Merest Stall : Messrs. I Sweetman, I Gresty, R Davies, F H Lloyd, A Abel, A florowoed, J Littlemnre, 3. Dean, H Huehes, F McNorton, C Bradshaw, G Blythe, A Geodier. Flower stall : Messrs A Geredier, A Wood- ward. the Misses Goodier, J Lambert, Tittle, Britland, E Harsison, E Dutton. Fish Pond : The Misses N Tem- perley, Rogers, Shaw, Bazley, Santley, Littler, Inman, ' Harrison. The preceeds of the first day amounted to 05. SECOND DAY. On Thuesday, the bazaar was epenecl by Mr. J. Brock J.P., who was introduced o those present by the Rev. R. J. Clifton. The Rev. R. J. Clifton said he was pleased to see Mr. Brrek present and they would all welcome him for his kindness in bringing the weight of hi. influence to bear on that ham r. As Chairman of the United Alkali Com- pany Mr. Brock Was well known to them. (Hear,,arar.) Mr. Brock said he could assure them that it gave him very much pleasure to come amongst them. He was always anxious and willing to take part in any good work and could wistaria thou that if it were possible he would bs delighted to shower fortune and happiness on ry hand in Runcorn. Runcorn, be thought, had got down to it: low water mark, and in the future they could lookforward to something better. (Hear, hear,) Mr. Brock referred to the advantage of a sound religious principle in every businsiss man, and said that the work of the Sunday schools would be greatly de- pendee 'neon the future for that principle. Finally he hoped they would be succsissful in realising the sum they required namely £2OO. (Hear, het sate open.t).Hehad pleasure in declaring the second day (Anplause), Mr F. J. Kerman p”ors,d a vote of thanks to Mr. Brock, whom they were all delightel to see present. Mr, Samuel Taylor seconded with extreme pleasure.</p>
        <p>The vo`e was heartily accorded. Mr. Br cok sytably acknowlPdgee. Berore a Vita WM paid to the btalls Mr. Brock was cresested with a beautirul " button lots" by Miss Ruth Rodgers, and Mrn. Brock with two pretty bouquets by Misses J. Shaw and Annie Barr:son, ou behalt of the Committee.</p>
