      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMINER' FRP =AY. NOVEMBER 4, 1-98</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER' FRP =AY. NOVEMBER 4, 1-98</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPDNDENr]</p>
        <p>Tne Runcorn School Board election is coming on apace—though the date is not yet fixed—and cer- tain influential ratepayers have thought fit to take up the cause of the undenominational schools, from an undenominational standpoint. It is high time that such an attitude should ba adoptel, and mucn good may come of it—pr3vided the candidates are purely undenominational.</p>
        <p>On this occasion, the ratepayers will bs called upon to select nine representatives to serve them upon the Board. Now, education, or rather secular education, is what Ate School Boards cater for. Therefore, in face of this fact, it would be batter for the proper management of these schools that the authority" governing them should be in the hands of disinterested and unbiassed administrators.</p>
        <p>Where are they to be obtainsd from? Well, ons is inclined to think that they should not be selected ,entirely from the ranks cf ths clergy. Keep the .clergy off altogether Dri it out of charity to themselves I They like clever; they are useful ; they are sensible men; but there is greater scope for their cleverness, their usefulness, and their sensibleness, in the work to which it is supposed they are devoted.</p>
        <p>For your candidates for the forthcoming election look around you and select men that possess most if not all of the following qualification :—Educa- lion, broad-minledness, time, iuclination, capacity for adminstration, au,' above all your own confi- dence.</p>
        <p>There is some talk of cleaning and renovating the Parish Church.. It is a goo i.ides, and should be attended to at once. I hope, whilst they are about it, that the ruling body will do something to make the means of egress from the aide aisles easier. At present there is a broad aisle blocked up by a great stone pillar, here and there, and only one person at a time can get oast. It is a decided nuisance. By cutting out a curved portion of the seating oppasite the pillar, the comfort of hundreds of people would be added to, and the loss in seatinz wauld not be appreciable. •</p>
        <p>FOOTBLLL. Saturday seas another match in the Northern Union Senior Competition at Runcorn, when a vi.it will be paid to Irwell-lane by Morecambe. Sc far Morecambe have sustained what is generally termed a" Weekly Drubbing." It is to be hved they will continue t 3 figure under that heading for zother week, yet it is n3t unlikely that Runcorn will have all their work cut oat. rIMIWw.•••••• RUNCORN URBAN DiStitlCT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the above Cmicil was held at the Town Hall, Huocorn, on Thursday morning. There were present : !deism Samuel Taylor, j.p., (chairman), D Bisbr3wo. W Collier, W Headley, El J Once, James Pritchard, E Imison,T Dean. P Speakman, J Hoisefie'd. T Wisyrall, U Hewitt, with Mr. E Marshal (secret iry),Mr. F A Lake (law-clerk) Mr J Wilding (sur- veyor), and Dr. F McDougall (medical officer of health). MEDICAL OFFICER'S saner. Tnis report showed that 34. deaths hci occurred dur- ing the mouth,four of which were subjects for inquests. It gave a per cent ige of 23 per 1,000 of estimatsd popu- lation ; aad of 20 pr 1.000 exclu:i re of inquest cases. There were 53 thrths reg;stered. During the corrmpond- ing period last year, 24 deaths, and 37 b ales were regis- tered.</p>
        <p>TENDERS, ETC. Mr. D. Bilbrown propeled, and Mr. T. Than seconded that the following, among other, recommendations of the Hi4l3ways and Buildings Committtes be agreed to:— Tie tenders of Me ;ars. H. Heys and Co., for the supply of 300 yards of 3-inch Hasliugden flags, polished barn; of Mr. Thomas Robinson tor Boning ; of Mesars. Brie drit and Co. for the supply of 365 yards of 12-inch by 8-inch channels. and 80 tons of 1-inch macadam ' • and of Messrs. Datt m Eshire, Liitel, for the supp'y of 40 tons of 1-inch macadam be accepted ; thy, the prem4lea occupied by the following person be registare I for the retail sale of explosives under Division A. :—John W. Hogg; 20,'Bridge-street: William Pendlebury, 40, Lord- Street; Samuel Hooch; 25, Bryon-s.:reet ; J3seph Bazley, Shaw-street ; and that tne plans of Mr. A. Du;ton, ot alterations of shop window; and Mr. Yates, fB. the erec- tion of a scu lary ne approved of.—Tbe proposition Was unanimously agree I to.</p>
