        <p>Subsectinitly th int (rain. was 'ill a sunsirtnui r mast rising provii,d h 7 Mr. J4sspli Walker. Wh the tab'es had bled cleare I,</p>
        <p>Toe Cmirman (Mr. Davies) gavotte usual loyal toast/ which were duly honoured. la p•op,sing the health of the fo'raim en, Mr. Thomas Wright, the Criairman sail he had always b een kind enough to support him 3n those onessimas in maintain. I ing the ancient fame of that court. He felt very much indebtei to him for being so punctual and regular in his attendance. Re thanke I th Inn all sincerely, and fe't that they cld him a great, faveur in rn seeing him annually on thou etc 'sinus. (Hear, hear.) They k iew at the present time that thee were 'o certain extent unde• a war cloud in this cluntry. 113 Was 0.4 enough to reiollect the Indian Mutiny an i the Crimlan W 5..., as s ime of them did also. Re WAS al/m ad minuet to re• colleet the Is. 61. income tax which f allowed that ow- ; break in the Crimea. The" wo ili be able, ho trusted, te uph ild the British Arms with honour whatever might oome tnis Fashnia dispute. Again, the subj act mate- Was like many other sulj-ict matters of war, no-, worth the es': of war, but he thought the time had arived when tney bad to assert their rights and show that the old Eiglish spirit Was not d'ad within them, and that when these ditnceltim arose they were ready to drip their party differences an 1 dissentious, if they htd any, and Filo together in one great united cry that the honour of Efig'aid must be preserved. (Hear, hear.) In the early ages of their Eoglish history that war, sp it in wh,ch they now sat had b•en the. mno of battle. Anyone who wishrd to know what tbs s•ige of a nestle Was in old time; should read loanine. He would fin lin the pages of that celAtrarecl work of Sir Walter Scott an account of the seige of the castle of Torqueston. HA (Mr. Davies) had many times read it. and pictured to himself Halton Castle, with its tower, buttresses, walls and gates, as being Torqueeton, and being attacked by th at body which S;r Walt 'r Scott got together over a paltry quarrel on.' of those quarrels in which the balance ef England had often been concerned. Warfare war very mach note d astructive than it was then. At the lame time, where saerificee had Co be made, where the glory and hnnour of old En ;land had to be supp3rted,they were all of them, he believed, in one mind. (Aoplauee.) He knew that if a war threaten'd them th,re would he a general call throughout the ceuntry, and there would be very many privations to undergo ; 'wilt at the same time timi the history of Eigland was a history of such sacri- fices and such efforts and he felt sure that they in their small way, would not be the most backward of all to supp-art and rally round the old flag of England, bat would answer any call male upon them whether from Halton Castle in the cnunty of Lane aster, or on the opp,;site side in cluatv of Cheater, or from the country at large: (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright regondli. Mr. Davies had said he WAi und r obligation to turns, bu: thnuzht they were, under a very much greater obiizatiou t .) Mr. Di7i(ll for kindly attending the court. 'Nevi were all very yrepri to gee him pre,ent that day, and ids) Mr. Kirkmanm, It gave him toe very gvatest pleasure to meet Mr. Davies in health and strength, and they hoped he would long be soan.d t continue these annual visits. He proposed tie health of Mr. Davies and Mr. Kirkconnel.</p>
        <p>The roast waa recsived with enthus a3m. Mr. D ivie3 remanded and saii he W 4,1 very thankful ti) be spired to come amongst them. With all the draw- b %clog there were life, he confessed that a; he was gettince to tee end of hi; life he hsd enjoy° I and been thankful for his life, and for his mans. friendslipi Tyr- h4p4 more than for any other single honour. He hoped they m'ght meet again on these happy occasions. In the words of the poet he was right glad that he was born, and thankful that he lived. (Applause.). Mr. Kirkconnel also responde f.</p>
        <p>The liealth of the represintA-ti-veq from the out tow:- ships, the health of the host arid hostets, and cther toses bllowed, and the remainder of the evening, was t-nent in a convivial manner, the vocal and instrument 1,1 si Is beirez ably ett tained by Mrs. Walker, Miss Walker, Mrq. Gulmette, and Messrs. Watson, Joseph Walker, Cooper and others.</p>
