        <head>laj,)W. TO LISTEN TO HA.YDN'S "CREATION."</head>
        <p>laj,)W. TO LISTEN TO HA.YDN'S "CREATION."</p>
        <p>BY JOHo4 °ROWDY.</p>
        <p>"Next in popular acceptance after Handel's werk The Messiah, comes Heydn's oratorio The Creation. I hive eaid, in giving some hints ou listening to the former. that the clue to th' appreciation of its music lies in the sense of its words : the same may be remarked of Ilaydn's work; and should be true of all music attachrd to serious text. In such unions the words define the outlines of the artwork ; the music supplies colour : if tue colour is untrue to the outlines, or otherwise mis- appliel, the work is to t oat extent false art, however dazzling the colour. In Haydn' oratorio, as Handel's th -re is relevance throughout of music to verbal text. But minife itly the subject of Elaydn's work, though in- t orestine, is second in sublimity to that of Handel's ; the verbal text deals not with tie spiritual, but with the phymital ; and he general result is a Jig iter, though an admirable and a pleasant production. The' book' to which Haydn has at ached his MASiC consists of two elem -nts, judiciously set forth. In the course of Parts Land Il..the Bible nirrative of the Crea- tion is introduced, piece by piece, generally in recitative assigned to one ot the three angels (Raphael, Thiel, Gabriel) wile are suppedl to take part in telling the story. Then each portion of the sacred text, after de- livery in recitative, is supplemented or extended by the author of the words, in sentences exceedingly well de- vised to carry musical colour : these portions us sally ferm tin text of an air, dot, or tie); and !eel up to a chorus, the full stream of which is sometime' suspended for the momentary and most pleasant iacroductim or short passages of duet or trio. These successions of r.citative, air, chorus, constitute the framework of Petits I. and H. ; the choruses firming a series of clim- axes ; culminating at the end of the first part, with 'Dee heavens are telling,' at tin end of the second part with 'Achieved is the g'orious work.' Tee first step towards being able to listen well ' to the work is the perception of them the main lines on which it is laid out : let it next b 3 noticed that a differ- reit and appropriate treatment characterises each of the three classes of numbers; the mu•ic composed for the declamation of the actual Bible words in recitative is invariably full of easy dignity, and compar itively re- served in style ; in the descriptive settings of non- diblical words whice f nllow, on the contrary, the com- poser lets his graceful fancy have full play, phrase after phrase of the text being suggestively and specially p tinted as it comes ; in the cherases, last y, gushes out exuberant praise, so brilliant in its purity, so celestial in its simplicity, that the str tins may well be attributed, as they are, to tee heavenly host th inaselves. In Part 111, the words are all those of the author of tin book •, the newly-creat id Adam and Eye are intro• euced,and to :diem are assigned the non-choral numbers, the chorus breaking in, now and again, upon their dual strains. In the penultimate number, the recitative" 0 happy pair! and happy still might be, if not reis'ed," &amp; r., the angel Ur el reappears, and throws in a dash of sa loess by allusion to the coming Fall ; hut this is merely a momentary cloud—The only one which passes acres% the bright heriz on of the work—and the oratorio eads with a glow of sunshine in the fieal chorus " Sing the Lori, ye voices all." With this sketch of the general framework of the oratorio, watch the inns iding iistener may read with ad- vantage b :far I the peformance commences, I proceed to some rema•ks on the several numbers in the order Of their occurrence. The instrumental introduction to The Creation, labelled It-presentation of Chaos,' is an orchestral pre- lude which aims at fulfilling its intention chiefly by its harmonies ; which as the merest glance will reveal, are of that cies s in which chromatic atterati ins, c instantly succee line each other in one or other of the parts, i i- d ics a quiet streint of weird mysterious modulation. As the number progresses th rhyt Ira; get more aniin a- te 1 and varied ; triplets of quivers, played staccit occur in the b IS ' • the middle p sets are divided into shor'er pulsepulses,aitd at length break into t•iplet I of seal quavers ; the chromatic modulations continuing. Tao uppei pies presently get inn in see rapid motion; and then the whole suheides. after accomellehing tee composer's Pu-Pies of Presin sine is C 7.4 • ire as of an unsettled, restless condition nf teings. Th m stee'y to be ilieetret d is then introducei in th' first re :it ttive re• her, ' Ira the beginieog Gel cr sated tee h-even and he , etrth.'A few onus c d eentenc es, well cornet te admit i)f in effective decking it iry op .ring of the sweetly irry them Biblicil words ; and the hearer shoal 1 mark ho v a S,ngle link of ha.nto my—t oh end of 13 flat—at its el eel, brings the ear to a 10 le-exp.:semi, careful! • pe- p are i for, rest in the key of E ft tt, in which is c ist th soceeseling chorus, the first of the work, An 1 the spirit of Go! ' Sn art a, in this :aunties it is full of telling tnimees worth attention. Ma -k first, the sooth- ing restful effect, already mentioned, of a definite sib'' discs int the key of E ft tt, the ' relativ • mij ort as musicians term it, wet the true cadential home, as I wid call it, of the re itiv minor key of C with three flats, round which, mainly, tee previous unrestful her- mony has terre.d Ma•k the quiet short lead of trebles with which the chorus begins; the slight heiteht ming of colour in the harmony at the wor Ii • moyel aeon the face of the' the silence of all accernplei me it at • An I Gel said,' and again at' Let there be geese The setting, !witty, by flay In of tie foir weell ' there was light' i-itch, the first cheral nurnh-r o' bit work, is a well-knowa point of masic elf sot, Of she'e four worts the first three are eno rimed by the v oiees ' nnaccompaniei, in moentoae anl unison at the word " light " there is a burst of harmony from tn. whele force of VOTe3 and instruments, the churl these,' 11 en! —to put it somewhat piradoxieilly—ehaeof the rewire, miner of the key with its third made ortajo•. After a few bars of eloquent chorus, the B be story is carried on by a few words of recitative, at tne end of which the simple narrative of Rely Writ is interrunte ; and we have an example of that descriptive extensile of the subject which has already been referred to as on. element of tin' book.' in the sir anl (evens : • Now vanish before the holy bevies the gloomy shales of ancient night: the first of dsys appears : Now chaos ends and order fair prevails. • Affdghted fled Hell's spirits black in throngs : down they sink in the deop abyss to endless night Despairing. cursing, rage attends their rapid fall. ' A new-created world springs up at (fan command.' Readi ig the words rot this number the listener will he ready to perceive how appropriate is its en isical treat- wilt. Of the three sentences in which it is printed above, the middle one presents a strong contrast in eons ;Of neeanine to the tone of the first and third ; seed it is to the celoering of the contrast outlined by the words that the master's music is directed. At first the text is lit lie solo, to an aria for the angel Ifni .1. The ehara eer of which changes entirely at the words ' Affrighted fled Hews spirits , the change in the voice pa-t beteg emphasis .d, of course, be the orchestral parrs, as the listener must net fill ti notice. Preseetly the chceui enters, the bass voices hreakine in with tee words ' Dsspiiriiff, cursine.raee to a cru voteat,claroma. tie theme, well calculated to illustrate the sense. This theme an li-8 turbulent ancernpeniment rag. o as a storm ; then the clouds pest. and the ion airnhleP: throueh, s the v deem, singing softly, revert in qu et a lierineniee to the key, at the words • A new-created world.' The solo voice returns for a few hors to tee so-my thm, tiro wed by a musical seatenee or two in the same violent phraseoloey for th, eterie: then Genies a second tine the centrast A new-ereeted world,' end a number well worth listening to with the understand- ing as welt as the ear ends in peaceful sunshine. Tine narrative now advances in a solo, for Raphael first, to the next succeeding portion of -he Bible narra- tive : this is thin extended by furner &amp;scrip' ire text, giving reference to wind, lightning, rain, hail and snow ; in all cynic's, as through suit the 'oratorio, is to he marked vivid and appropriate orchestration, imitative, or illus- trative of the laneueee of the nook. A bright solo. ' The :nary Vous work.' accompanied by a chorus, simple. Felony, end effsCti.e. " And to th ethereal vaults req3 furniehes. in its combination instance of several similar resorted t o in this charmine orat erio. The well-chosen course of treating the subject now with l choir, in iws.' prosently ior the listener's attentloe ac oaPsar otfer salientleteetrs d' principal ets mge pdr eecrip ejackrdgair nro:sun'ad IR:to"ognifivnee'fuOl joiann:. B llowy figures of accompaniment preveil as the Ringer sings of the ' boisterous sea ' ; when ' mountains and the accompaniment changes its char- ;emanerigae ; a' in when' through th' morn piaies out. hlng wide in serpent error rivers Hew • : the char- acter o graphic air change; finally, emerging from the minor hntore nt to the maim. timed, at the words Softly purling g'ides on through silent vale, the limood brook.' Of the well-known air which Comes presently, breath- inc spring in its every phrase, the always delightful ' With verdure clad," it must be said shertly that it is one of the purest outwellings ef music that ever came from the EMII of a composer :in listening to it let the learard musician forget his learning, and lend his ears unembarrassed by his science ; the unlearned listener may, for this time at least, forego all effort, and let the music filer in Upen his soul, making its own impression. The chorus • Awake the harp ! the lyre awake ! 'pro- vides the desirable climax of a full triumphant number to close the narration of the third day of Creation : in the music of the fourth day mark first the sunrise pre- lude to In splendour bright is rising new the sun ; and net° how the time and style of the voice part and its accompaniment change for a moment, an illustration of the wo.ds ' With softer beams and milder light steps on the silver moon through silent night." This listened to, ep ‘cial hut not latiorieus attention must he bespoken , for the well-known chorus a The heavens are tolling,' into which is interj .cted re. -seedy a trio of the three angels, ' To day that is coming speaks it the day ; the night that is gone to f ollosring night.' Close urea the dusky harmonies which carry the last limb of this sen- tence,- allusive to nigh's, burst in again the chorus ' The , heavens are telling ;' after which, with the came lovely . effect as before, comee the trio of angels, In all the lands resounds the word ;' the chorus breaking in again presently, and carrying the number in a triumphant , swine of celestial j sy to the end of the first part. Four sf the elle days of Creation have now been de- ' scribed inn words, and the deseriotion eoleueed by =sic:</p>
        <p>in ths second part tho task is resumed, on the same plan as before. To Gabriel is assigned, af cer a recitative in Bible words, the air • On miihy pens ;' wherein, as in the similar descriptive numlyrs, the instrumentation studiously fAlowi the sense ; vigour and vapidity in the orchestra accompany the voice as it mentions the soar- ing eagle ; the lark suggests a b ,r of twittering triplets; the cooing of the dove calls forth another figure, in two parts and special ingenuity is devoted by the composer with pleasin, effect, to an instrumental imitation of the song of the nightingale. All these points will be obvious t 3 this listener if he listen at all. No chorus follows this air, but the narrative proceeds in a number of some I elaboration Gar the three angels, 'Most beautiful appear;' in whicn, atter a sprightly instrumental prelude, a solo is fint assigned to G ibriel ; and the graceful music of this rewated, by Uriel, to otner In lofty circles play,' while a short solo assigned to Raphael in turn pre- sents other leading images from the world of nature, and at every successive phrase the master puts into the picture a pertinent dash of instrummtal colour, rapid bass passavis of an amusing tournure introducing ' th' immense leviathan.'</p>
        <p>The three angels having, immldiately upon this, united in a combine] movement, ' flow many are thy works, 0 God 1' the d. voices are presently joined by the entry of the full cnorus—now for some time silent ; and a numb:r of importance, briliance, and some grandstur ("roe Lord is great') resu!ts, in wnich the last few bars f orming a cadence of imposing solidity, are one of sever LI effective points. The many instances of curious orchestral illustration which presently come will speak for themselves ; and the livener will scarcely need prompting to notice the ' roar' of imitative sor:. in the orchestra as the tawny lion' is mentioned, the chromatic runs which introduce ' tde fl!xible tige•,' the change of rhythm which heralds the' nimble stag,' the buzz of the ' uost of ins ,cts,' and the significant orchestral expression invented to accom- pany toe words 'in long dinyousion creeps with sinuous trace the worm.'</p>
        <p>For a bar after tSe voice ceases, the worm crawls on in the orchestra ; and wirh tais last touch of ingenuity the number &amp;nem. After one more piece of imtative accompaniment heard presently at the words 'By heavy beasts the ground was tr el,' this element of effect, carried to the brink of ludicrous, disappears from the oratorio, giving war t a more digaified style at the words which prelude the creation of man. The sentences Biblical and other, in which this act of the Great Crea- tor is set forth, are assigned to strains of a grace and appropriateness habitual to Haydn ; and succeeded pre- sently by tie chorus AClliflVed is the glorious work.' Early lei this is interpose.] the b•sautiful and religious duet, 'On Thee each living soul awaits.' Variety is further developed in this number by a pags age tvsingle bass voice,,' But when Thy face, 0 Lord, is hid and a trio fr the thr2e angels, 'Their sendest forth Thy breath again ;' elab ated episodes which should all be noted as contributing to the due divelopment of the work a: this stage. The chorus presently return!: 'Achieved is the glorious work --hallelujah !' and a number of con- siderahn vi your and breadth brings us to the end of toe second pa • r. A short third section, in which Adam and Eve, as al- ready notic-d, tak s for the most part the place of the angols as principal singers, amolifies the last scent of the story.—" Cassell's Magazine."</p>
