        <p>ROYAL COURT THFCATRR.</p>
        <p>We are promised a light entertainment at the Court Theatre next. week, in the form ofoa musical c3meily style I "A Trip to Rum-Futn." This has been "on the road " four years, and its success can- not be doubted. It is full of fun and " go,' amid specialities of an unusual character. THE LIGHTING OF VEHICLES AIFTBII SUNSET. Notices have been issued by the Town Clerk with reference to lights on vehicle. The 113tiCe states that on and after Monday, the 14th inst., the bylaws made by the Canned requiring all vehicles to have lamps attached to them after unset will be strictly enforced. THE SCALDING FATALITY AT WAIUUNGT3N. I NQUEST. On Tuesday evening mr. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest in the Police Parade-room touching the death of Sarah Ellen England, the 18 months old child of Mary and Thomas England,32, King-street. rne child died on Monday from scalds received on October 18th. The mother of the deceased said she was ill in bei at the time or the accfclant. On the morning in question she heard screams, and subse- quently ascertained that the-child had bean burnt. 7--Nancy ngland said she was giting the children their 'breakfast when deceased pulled a cup and saucer of the table, with the remit that the con- tents went over her left arm and severely scalded her. Lime water and oil were applied until Friday last, when the child became worse, and she was taken to the Infirmary, whore she dieii on Monday. _A verdict of "Accult n at dcatli " was r,turned.— At the conclusion of ti- irquiry a juryman called the attention of the Coroner to the fact that the jury had had to walk a mils to vitw the body. They were willing. to give their servic.s, bul took excep- tion to walking such a long di:tance.—The Coroner said that wee a matter for the Corporation, and not for him. •</p>
