      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>UNIVERSITY EXTENSION LECTURES AT WARRINGTON. THE ENGLAND OF SHAKEiPEARE AND ELIZABETH.</head>
        <p>UNIVERSITY EXTENSION LECTURES AT WARRINGTON. THE ENGLAND OF SHAKEiPEARE AND ELIZABETH.</p>
        <p>Mr. A . G. Ds Burson delivered the third of the coarse of six le3tuies on the history of the En elish psople from 1514 to 1689 in the Co-operati re Hall, Sankey-street, Warrington, on Wednesday evening. There was a good attendance. At the oateet the Lecturer reverted to the preceding lecture in which he showed th 3 assertion of national unity, which carried with it an awakening of Dower in every field of energy, especially in maritime enterprise and in literature. The centre of the national awakening, he pointed out, was Qeeen Elizabeth her. self. She priers led to a remarable degree the absolute. ism which characterised the Tudors. All of them were determined that no person and no foreign influence should own one inch of land or one jot of power within the English realm. It was that patriotism reflected in the policy and armpit:des of Elizateecri that won for her the enthusiasm ane admiration of leer people. The netional enthusiasm for her was reflected in Eiztbethan liters. turn. The age of El:z abeth was at ege of national awakening aid Pati eal energy. E syry intim had two sides to its history—its internal history and its relations outtrde with other people. At the last lecture be showed the national awakenitie from w:thout as die. played in the struggle a eainst Spain ; he now wished to turn to the inner life and history of the English people. There were three great pliets to br noted, first, the growth of cemntarce and trade; sec mdly, the ran of a great body of English literature ; aid thir-Iy, certain asp iCiS in th-i social and tee-mornical life of the time. Dealing with the first pint, he traced the history of maritime enterprise from the expeeition to North America by Cabot. The lecturer observed that fisheries were the most important el-ra ent in English centrnerce prior to the reien of Elizabeth. With the reign of Elizabeth, there ceased all isolated efforts at the development Of COMM 're and then bean thal systematic progress in trade which had gone on to our own day, The great aim of all merchants and seamen in Elizabeth's time was to reach the commerce of the far East. Expeditions were sent forth to discover the North-East and North-West passages to the East. Itc- ferenca was made to those of tee Muscovy company,and the voyages of FroWsher and Davis, and of Gilbert's tract on the North-West passage. Then as the powers of Southern Europe—Spain and Port ugal— begaa to decline it was natural that the nations of Northera Europe should endeavour to poach on the commercial monopoly which Portugal and Spain had held. The voyages of Hawkins and Drake, the Eat Indiari voyages round tee Cape of Good Hope, and the ultimate formation of the East India Company were touched up en. Proneedirg, the lecturer referred to the projects for planting celonies in the new world and the ideas which animated Raleigh and Gilbert in this connection. Raleigh's projfcts for enlenising Virginia and other expeditions in Nerrh America were dilated upon, an 1 the lecturer ascribed the failure of these early colonisation schemes to the fact that the pioneers did not understand the necessary rroneer work that had to be done in planting a colony of Englishmen, used t ) the life at home, ie a foreign oily riet amid hos- ttle native tribes. But nevertheless Raleigh must he considered the father of English colonisation. Turning to the great outbreak of good literature that marks Elizabeth's reign, the lecturer remarked thtt literature In the Case of any nation was the expression of national life. The work of E imuni Spencer was reviewed, the lecturer stating that Spensrr's poetry reflected the ideas and feeling of his natiun and age—patriotic energy, hatred ot Cateolicisna and Spain, moral earnestness. The life and life-work of William Shakespear.i were next spoken of. Speaking of the rise of the Eliztbethan drama which culminated in Shakespeare's plays, he said the rice of the drama was the most splendtd page in El v.. hethen literature. The lecturer divided Shakespeare's life-work into four epochs, and showed how far he was affected by national e: t ausiasm as seen in his historical plays, and by the growing s-riousness of Erie. lish feeling seen in his later plays. The condition of the people in Eizebeth's reign was briefly touch upon. At this time England was an agricul-nral country and the majority of the people lived an i got the r livoie upon the land. Tne agricultural classes d nine Elizabeth's reign suffered from very serious distress. The conversi lu of corn lairds iota pasture lands and the enclosure of common lands by tile lords of the manor were largely respo esibl a" e for the prevalence of vaTr" and pauperism at this Vale. Tte methods of relief, including the Elizabethan poor law of 1601 wrr briefle referred to. In conclusion, the lecturer sketihed the life of Sir Mail. Raleigh as illustrating th central feat ere of the national awakening which was its richness and e variety. Raleigh reflected most fully the versatile and rich enthusiasm that marked the age. "His career as courtier, governor, coloniser, sailor, and in his later years during his long imprisonment at the Tower. AR a writer and rientist were dialt with. The men of Elizab ,th's tim realiesd that England was a nation which belonged to them and which. it was their duty to tree o preoeseravn.td. Elt n gwi was that re•olutim to maintain England national unity invioliata that had rendettsd tiheeel Inc many wa in apite of their faults and vices, future generations. (APPlatlSet)</p>
        <p>Warrington haven° Lupe encounter this Satin,lay. but joutuey north to play the capable Ali nom fifteen.vato, are carrying all before. Urns In the second division, I ran Warrington syheoruvldelons3e, belt*: HeuclaPers*'e' to tell. Still th 9 Mill m lads have accomplished one good perfornincea a:a;ost s`rong opponents. Up to date they have placed ix mitehes, won then 3;1, and scored 188 pail t, o:air cppotents fire.</p>
