      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>IHE BENEFITS OF SUE N E.</head>
        <p>IHE BENEFITS OF SUE N E.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is swell as will benefit all &amp;asses of people. 6erne of tne greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly beneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of tha best medi- cinal preparations, however much they may do to re- move one class of disease, or suit some constitutioas, at the oarne time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it entirely untouched, or it they benefit one class of patients, they may injure another. New the great need of the age is game scientifically arranged preparaticn which will cope effectually with the preva- lent diseases of this country, which wit be certain to d) good when fairly tried,which will be equally adapted to the needs of the merchant and the workman, the pr a- fes3ioaal man, and he who wins his bread b:v the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory laand,the miner, and the roadside labourer.lt should be, too, such a preparation as contains RO injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with impunity by the weakly child,l or the delicate :ady, as well as by the stronger constitu- ted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal r'reparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable Boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed ,e)on has been found, has been tried, has been proved, and wherever proved,it has been found satisfactory. We refer to Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, which when once tried,has been always recommended, and hes proved successful when all other medicine has failed to give re!ief, and we may say further, that it has proved permanently benefi cial, when other preparations at best only gave tempor- ary relief. It is strongly rocommended as The Best Remedy of the Ago for Indigestion in its different forms, such as Sick Headache,Pains in the Side, Giddiness,Loss of Appetite,also for Nervousness and Nervous Disorders, Sleeplessness, Neuralgia, Low Spirits, and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness, either after an illness, long confinement to ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause. It strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts of the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evins' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic, and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledgeel to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease.lf you suspect that your health is beginnine to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excel- lent Tonic, which is sold everywhere in bottles at 25.93. and 43.6 d. each,but ahould any difficulty be experiemed in procuring it, the Proprietors will forward same for the above prices, carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing, see the name, " Gwilym Eq.ins," on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole proprietors Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Cnepany, Limited, Llauelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BEF;ui TtiE A.W.6 tiA</head>
        <p>BEF;ui TtiE A.W.6 tiA</p>
        <p>THEFT OF CASKS FROM BANK QUAY GOODS YARD. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. James Fairclough (presiding), and Mr. James Hephercl and Alderman Thomas Sutton. James Rush, carter, 50, Wellingeou-street, was cnarged with stealins, twelve oil casks sonic time between 11 o'clock on Tuesday morning and 12 o'clock noon on the following day.— Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Constable said the prosecutor, Mr. a spinal-1. was a publican and also dealt in oil casks. He had a number of casks stored at Bank QUAY goods yard. On Mon lay morning ho counted the caaks, and found there were 82. On the foilowing day when he count=d them tilers were twelve missing He gave information to the police, and in company with P.C. Woods found the twelve casks in a yard of Mr Dawson's in Aikin-street. Mr. Aspinall identified them' by his private mark A. Mr. Dawson in explanation said a man had been to his house in Arpley-street and wanted to sell him casks, as be could buy them cheap at Win- wick. He said he would leave a sample at the yard and it was agreed. Mr. Dawson went down to the yard to view the casks and saw the sample had not ben left. He paid the man for 12 casks at toe rate of 23. 31. each P.C. Woods arrrs-ed the prisoner who wa • subsequently identified by Mr. Dawcoa and a lid names Alfred Taylor who WAS also present at the buyin • f thd casks.—William Aspinall,licensee of the Railway Taverns actory-lane gave evidence bead ag out the Chief Con-, stable's statement. The casks were worth 4s. each to him. They were stored at the goods yard at Bank Quay at his own risk.—Thomas Davvson, cooper, 28, Arpley- street, also corrobarated.—Alfred Taylor, cleaner at the railway shed, and living with hie uncle (Thomas Dawson) at 23 Arpley street, eave further evi fence for the prosecution.— P.C. Woods said prisoner was under the inflrence of drink when he arr.ested him, and said " I ana innocent/ He had 53. Bit in his prcket and every oppottunity was given him of sending for someone or proving an alibi.—Prisoner denied that he h3d stolen the casks or had been to see Mr: Dawson.—John Howells, railway detective, said carters had the run of the place at Bank Quay and could take away any g:ods for which the Railway Company were iy,t responsible, without ques- tion. The Company themselves had suffered severely lately.—The b3tich-coses3(l3re-d the case proved and sen- tenced the prisoner to two montls imorisonmeat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
