        <p>FBlDLY.—Before_htr.J.unee Hopher.i. (In the chair), Dr.</p>
        <p>• - Gornall, and Councillor Smethurst. , DntrNgertiBs AND NEGLECT.—AIIos Grey and Joint Grey (working cutler) were charged. the woman with being drunk and disorderly on Thursday afternoon in Buttermarket-street at 2.30, and the male defendant with being drunk at ten ' minutes to six o'clock in the evening The female defendant, WPB also charged with exposing her two children, and the male defendant with causing the same to be exposed.— Detective Superintendent Monaghan se.id about 2.33 in the afternoon of the day in coesti us the woman had a little child in her arms and another child by ' her side, walking with bare feet It was raining at Has time and defendant was drunk and singiar. He advised her to go borne and she uscd ttasialikpgßaga to him. Later in the evening the male defenl me was drunk. The z:hild at the Police station told him that her hoots and stockings were in the lodging-house, her mother having made her take them efe before going out.—Catherine Daley, 37, Scotland-road, and Lucy Burke corroborated.—Detective Delahey said he took the two children to the Workhouse. Their bodies were covered with vermin.—Thebench committed defendants to prison for 14 days, and tho children were ordered to go te the Workhouse. OB,TRUCTION BY EXHIBITING Gonns. —The following were fined for exhibiting goods, and causing obstruction no the public footnath. on October 24th.—John Funnel', furniture dealer. Bewsey-street. 10a. including costs ; John Manfridi, ice-cream dealer, Winwick-street. ss. including costs ; H. V. Morton, pawnbroker. Winwick-street Sc. including costs, A CASE OF MISTAKEN Ingeurrry —George Clue was Sum- moned for assent, lag Martha Peers on Octeber 23th, and Samuel Beswick was charged with assaulting George Clare at the same time and place.—Mr. Grainger apneared for the complainant in the first case and defen 4ant in the second, both Cases being tried_ at once —George Clara. 59, Hamilton- street, said on the day in tin-ea:Hen he was looking for Some friends in the street, fie saw the defendant with two women dressed in black, and thinking lb was his friend he put his hands on him. Defendant turned round and he(complainant then Saw his mistake. The defendant struck him a severe blow overate left eye.—Witnesses were c tlled.—The bench in the one case fined Beswick 103 and casts and in the other Clare was ordered to may the costs. QTYARREL BETWEEN WOMEN, —Elizabeth Lomax was charged with assaulting Harriet Taylor on Oct.22nd.—Deert- dant pleaded guilty—Harriet Taylor, 17. Bac.&lt; Crossley- street. said net Saturday the defendant came to her and after some words butted her In the face and then struck her with a jug.—Sarah Stringer corroborated. Del endant was lined ss. including costa, SATUBDAY.—Bef ore NTr. J. W. Hayward (In the chair), and Mr. 11. T. Fairclough. " WANTED TO BE LOCKED Up,"-James (Forma. of no Mead abode, WAS charged with being drunk and disorderly at 10-50 on Friday night. —P.C. Hesp. who proved the ease, said defendant was drunk in Bank street shouting that he wanted to be locked up.—Ths bench imposed a fine 3or 5s and costs. MONDAY.—Eefore Mr. fireball (chairman) and Councillor Smethurst. A Reunions TRACT SELLER.— Mary Ann Steadman. of no settled abode, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bewsey road, at half-past three on Sunday af torn( in. P.C. Dennett Rt.-AAA that he found the defendant lying across the footpath in a disgraceful condition. An old man was with her and. he said she was his wife. He was rather thew Tse for drink. Witness • old him to take defendant away, but on cc mut of his r mdition he was unable and witness had mre a leant rt and convey the worm.n Cr tbe Police Station. The Chairman : It's a wonder to me where these p-3ple get their money for drink —The Chief Constthle : She was sell'ng relijou ; tracts yet trday. That Was how she got her mon.sv. Defendant lam very sorry. Do let me go. God bless you.—The H lAL:es' Clerk : How many times have yon asked that before Defendant : Never in my existence, and I have bin coming here for many years.—The Chairman: Coming where' Defendant To Warrington. I am a hawker and have be on here several times. She was fined 53. Defendant : Can you give me so long to c.a. it.—The Chief Constable Seven days. (Laughter.) DRUNK AND Df9oEDEl'T,T.—Patrick Blake, I. Plat.t-strce.. was charged with In "0 been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket str. :t at t flty-five minutnn pat two on Sunday aft ;moon —P.C. ier snit he saw the defendant fighting with another man. Dc'endant wiC drunk but the other man was sot ,r. He (a:tness) re): 1 1 them ta go away. Defendant would not g..). and he ru oblige 1t ) t ,ke him t the Pogue 64- tion.—Defenclant st + I that lie' mic Is drop of liquor to cure his cold end it got hold of Istrn.—The Chairman : That drop of liquor 13 answerable for a lot of things.—The Chief Constable said defendant had bade big drop. He saw him at eight o'c'c afid he was then very wild. He allowed torn' ,be t iled out two hours '1" A fine of 5s was imt mc d.—Sarah Narbu•y. 2.6, Dallarn-lane was charged with having be n drunlc and Aisorcler y On' her 17th. P.C. C.I: per said he was in Dallam-lane and h. .rd defendant say that she would trtoh all the windows in her daughte.es house. He put her into the house, and late' in the evening was Plain cath It tier. Defend Int said she was a widow and worked for her own living. She wa- 7o and had never st 7 tin the &gt;int before. She was not drunk and did not thr eaten' break windows. A fine of 2.9 ea was Inflicted.—James L mardy, labourer, 1. r.'.:,'13-Yard. wee summoned for a similar offence on the 23rd ult. Se,'ge nt Evans prey. 1 the Case. Evidence as to prisoner's bad record was given by Superintendent Monaghan• acid r prisoner was sent to gaol or 14 days with hard labour.— Sarah Hickey, 8, if ill,treet, was fined 53 and en ti for drunkenness and disorderly conduct in the Crown ant Sceptre yard on Sunda night. Sergeant Evans alm provzd this case.</p>
