      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 4, lBB.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 4, lBB.</p>
        <p>013.3C888 LABGITAGY, BY WOMEl.—RliZlbetal UM:LOWY .9 Tanner's-lane. was tined 53 for using obscene language in Berry's-yard on October 24th. Sarah Hilen lo:nn, Robinson yari. Penne'rstreet, was fined Sr for a similar offence. DIBIRDERLY CosDucr.—liebe3ca Riley, 32. Po tutain-st.. was bound over to keen the neace and fined in the costs for disorderly behaviour in Tanner's lane on October 26th. OWNER 3 n TROUBLE.—WiIIIam Quinn, 4 Dallam- lane, was fined Is for allowing his dog to b 3 at large fin- muzzled in Dallson-lane on October 7th. and was ordered to pay the costs for keeping a dog, without a license. Defend Mt Bud he was mistaken about the dog's age. -Amtlia Jetn. Knutsfo-d-road, was fined 21 for allowing her dog to be at larg-o unmuzzlel 011 October 23rd.—P.S. Smith proved the I one.</p>
        <p>TILTESDAY,—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair) and Measre. Birchall and Shair Green. .. _</p>
        <p>TRSIPoRanT-AM'T-Ho-a•Tr-TT:c.isillia-M—Graio-r--y- applied for temporary authority to sell at the off license at 22,Cozahedge- lane. The police had no objections and the application was granted. His 29TH API. TaRAISCE.-joh, Quinn, pleaded vuilty to being drunk and disorderly in Knutsford-road at 3 o'clock on Saturday afternoon.-P C. Corcoran proved the case.-The Chief Consrahle said defendant had baen 23 times before the court.-. The defendant, in answer to the Chairman, said he was a native of Warri ileSon and was a fireman at Crosfields. His home was broken no on Saturclay.-The magistrates sent him to prison for seven days hard labour. WEDNESDAY.-Before Mr.; R; T.Jaii•alough (in the chair)</p>
        <p>and Mr. Geddes</p>
        <p>TRAVELLING WITIOUT Trcitrr.—Wllll:2,m Brown, Liver- pool-road. Cadishead, was charged with travelling ou the Cheshire Lines Railway !rorn triarn to Cadishead on Tuesday without a ticket.—Mr. Winter prosecuted for the Company.— S. Dutton, ticket collector on the Central Station, Warrington said the defendant was in a first.class compartment on the 11 o'clock train from Manchester. lie was given every oppor- tunitv to pay his fare, glee his name and address or give up his ticket. --Anoth sr ticket collector corroborated.—The de- fendant said he got into the train and was told it would stop at every station. He booked for !earn station and thought he would be put down there. The collector accused him of travelling without a ticket and he lost his temper—The Chairman said if he had only be.n civil to the servants he would not hays heard so much Mout it as he had booked to Mara. He would have to pay his fare from Irlam and the ssts. They did not consider it a bad case Talmene.Y.—Before Mr. James Pairclough (in the chair), Mr. James Hepherd, and Alderman Thomas Sutton. A BAD CASE.—PAulina Oultram was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-streer, at a quarter past seven on Wednesday night.—P.C. Chrimes proved the case. Re had to get the assistance of O.C. Di teen and a handcart to take the defendant to the police station.—The Chief Constable said it was hr 33th appearance.—The defen- dant (weeping bitterly) said her baby had just died, and that had caused her to take drink.—The bind] fined her 5s and costs or seven days.</p>
        <p>actor, and time was nearly up befors Halifax,by the al, of Kntlewell, scored a try, thus gaining a victory b' three p Ants to nil. HOLBEOK v. BRIGHOUSE. A very well-contested game at Holbeck was the result of thi3 match, victory resting with the borne team by two gcalsnad a try to a goal.</p>
        <p>LANCASEIIRE "A" TEAM COMPETITION. WARRINGTON V. TTLDESLEY " A" TEAMS.</p>
        <p>This match was pl tyed at Tyldasley oa Saturday, be• fare very few spectators. Thl visitors kicked off and for a short time play was of an even character. From a forward rush the homesters scared a try and a goal resulted. This had a good effect on the visitors, who roused up considerably, and Elea, Moores and Harris scored trios with little lapse of time between. Of these Jones improved on two, making the result at half- time 13 points to five in favour of Warrington. The home team restarted the game, but after some grand passing, Moores got possession and scored his second try. Jones failed at the kick. Nothing fur:her was scored up to time leaving the final rasult,Warrington 16 points, Tyldesley 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SE3SIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SE3SIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDLY.—Before Mr. W. Mortimer (in the chair) Mr. Jostler,. Ball and Colonel Ridgeway. MISCELLANEOUS OFFENCE9.—The following were fined for being drunk and disorderly :—Richard Jones, Burtonworal, October I.4th. So; Edward Jones, Burtonwood, October 20th, 2s 60 wad costa; James Wrench, at Newton. October 16th. 5s ; and Ann Ilampson, Earlestown, November Ist, 58. Albert Payne, Sankey. was fined Is and costs for having his dog at large on Octobor 21St. John Bradley, Burtonwood. October 21st for the same offence was fined 5s including costs. John Ford, Sankey. October 10th, Edward llorrobin, Penketh and Thomas Dutton, October 31st, were each fined Sc for driving without having proper control. M ARISE STORE DEALER Prompt—Thom al Barlow, marine store dealer, 22. Liverpool-road, Sc. Helens, was summoned by Inspector Greatrex, ES PC A , for cruelly working a , horse on the 20th October. at Bartonwood —The officer stared that at 1-35 p.m. on the 20th October he saw defend- ant in charge of a brown mare pony in Collins Green, Burtonwood. The animal was an aged one, and was painfully lame. It we pulling a very heavy load, althouzb it was not fit for any kind of work. Defendant told him the pony had been lame for a long time. Witness cautioned him the first day he had the pony.—Frank Kerfoot. sanitary inspector to the Urban District Connell. gave eorraborative evidence.— Defendant stated that he bought the animal at a sale for £2 ss, and he did not know it was so lame until he worked it.—A fine of 5s end costs was imposed. ALLEGED TREVT AT WINWICK.—John Arthur Bald of Winwick, was summoned for stealing on October 21st five easks, valued at 355, the property of John Robinson Heap, late licensee of the Old Swan lan, Winwlck.—Mr. Rigby Swift, barrister of Liverpool, appeared to proseente, and Mr. Knowles defended —air. Swift gall that Heap left the Old Swan Inn on the 19th October, leaving five casks in the yard. Two days after he sent a railway man for tile casks, when it was found that they had disappeared. Some time betwesn the 19th mid 21st October defendant went to the present licensee of the house, and told him that he had come for the casks. Defendant was allowed to take them. and he subsequently sold tlaern.—Mr. Knowles said they did not I dispute the facts. Defendant took the casks whilst in a fit of drink, and he had promised him to siga the pledge. He thought the case would be met by binding defendant over. He had cost the prosecution 205., and they were Prepared to pay that.—The magistrates hound defeedant over to come up for juignsent when cal!et upon, and o:dered him to piy the costs. A :11.SE WITIIDRAWN.,-BlOlrtra Thompson Lowe. farmer, I Rixton Hall, was summoned for maliclomly wounding Wm. Griffiths, a farm labourer. There was a charge of assault on Lowe by Griffiths. Mr. Grainger repraseated Mr. Lowe. and Mr. Rigby Sw ft was retained by Mr. A. Browne for Griffiths. Mr. Swift said that Mr. Lowe was prepared to compensate Griffiths. and he and Mr. Grainger had agreed th‘t the case should ne withdrawal. —Oa application the magistrates granted the withdrawal.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDA.Y.—Beore Messrs. W. Timmins Oa tLe ehatr) anti</p>
        <p>John Ockleston. OBSCENE LANGUAGE. —James Evans, 5, Arthur-street. in answer to a charge of using obscene language In Arthur' street, on the 29th Inst.. pleaded gulltv.—P.O. James Bowyer proved the cam—A tine of 5s and 8s 65 costs was imposed. SToNS THROWIN4.—ANOTHER ROY OFFENDS —Williamn Davies. aged 13 years, residing at Curzon-street was sum- moned for throwing stones in the street to the danger and annoyance of persons using the same.—Dayies pleaded guilty—The Chairman, having commented mom the danger aud frequency of these cases, a fine of 5s and 8s 65 costs win imposed and paid. A Wolf AN REFUSES TO Qui r LICENSED PRESllsES,—Benigna Kiff . married woman, residing at Parelvars-lane, was sum- moned for being drank and refasing to quit the Mariner's Arm', tenanted by Mr John Bentley—PC. Smith testified to hiving been sent for to eject the woman. He found her in the kitchen In company with her husband. She was very drunk and refused to go out.—John Bentley, the landlord. said he knew the woman. She lived at the back of his house in the" stables." She came into his kitchen and was " row- ing " with her husband and daughter. He asked her to go nut, but she would not.—Defendant had nothing to say.— Fined ss. and Costs, or a month. A OISE DiaafissEri —Mary i:olelough, married Woman. Norton-street, was surntroned by Sarah Hand, married woman, of the same I )3alitv. for using obscene language and with hreatening the complainant.—After hearing conflicting evidence the Bennis dismissed the ease. TNE SCRIM BOARD AND COMPULSORY' CkT lON . —Prior to the hearing of a number of school eases, Hr. J. W. Light- burn (clerk ta the Runcorn School Boards submitted a state- ment. lie said the parents In Runcorn somehow got it into their heads that once they had been before the magistrates they were exonerated. But It was not so and the sooner they got rid of the erroneous impression the better. Through the fault of parents who would not send their children to school. there was a serious loss to the rates. School accommodation had to be provided, though it Was not used. There was a loss of something like £32 in the fee grant from the Education Department because of the affacted average attenianee. Host of the children they were proceeding against were those who ran about the streets, an i thou) who were nost in ne3 lof that education—hut the School Board were determined to bring those children into the Schools.—Tue cases were then dealt with.</p>
      </div>
