      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LA.NCASHERE SENWR. COMPETITION. WARRINGTON ANNIHILATE TYLDESLEY.</head>
        <p>LA.NCASHERE SENWR. COMPETITION. WARRINGTON ANNIHILATE TYLDESLEY.</p>
        <p>THE HOIIESTERS IN A bCOEUNG MOOD.</p>
        <p>The old-time fixture between Warrington and Tyldes- ley was heought off at Wilderspeol on Saturday before B'3lllo 4,000 spectators. The weather Was satisfactory considering the r die* of the provioue week, and no fault could be found with the condition of the turf. Warring- ten played their selected fifteen, in which Taylor and 1 Sudlow diaplaced E lee and Hatzon. Tyldeshay were ads: strongly represented, though they had to find a substi- tute for Leather at half hack, he having been laid up with trphoid fever. Much interest was shewn in the home team, their win at Id ochiale the week before having en- couraged their supporters to expect even better things, and they got them. For nne9 iii a way the wearers of primrose and blue distinguished themselves, and at the same time extinguished the "Bengers " very effectively. Mr. H. Eagles was the referee, and performed his duties with marked ability. The players in his charge were : Warrieeten : Hallam, :ull back ; Fish, Duckworth, Isherwood, and Hole oft, three-quarters ; J. T. Bate and Ashurst, half hacks •, Taylor, Deakin Naylor, Board. man, Fairhur it. Sallow, Mask's, and gerifte forwards. Tyldesley : Makin, full back ; France, Blakemore, G Berry, ant Eakersley, three -quarters ; Miller and Han- sen, half backs ; Berry, Worthington, Yates, Waywell, Clewes Nuth ir s, Craven, wad Riley, forwards. Deakin start-ad f the. h euesters, and by the instru- mentality of Taylor no return was forthcoming and the ball Was rushed to the Tyldesley line, where it Was semred be Makin lust as Fish raced up. This was a narrow escape for the visitors, but worse for them fotloweciL The hall was dropped out, and Isherwood I punted back with interest. Makin mulled awfully and the hams fer wards were over him in a moment, Swift grounding the ball over the line. Mason made a good attempt at goal. Toil early stneSSS put the homesters on the hest of terms with tioemaelves,and they kept their onnoneats busy defending. B.lrry and Eokersley Obtained slight ralief by skilful play, but a couple of awl marks drove them back. Tney last got a f cot- in in the borne half through a mist tke by Isherwood and Bolero!: as he wont for the ball simultaneously. Tyldesley gave some trouble ere they were cleared out, but Bate eventually charged a kick down and ran to cants° with the ball. A rnisSeld by Blakemore lost further ground for the visitors, though Berry prevented an 7 imm.diate danger. From a scrimmage in the Toldealey half the home captain socure d and beating his vis-a-vis got clean away amid roaring closers. When challeneed he passed to Mason, and the ball finally travelled through the hands of Taylor and Deakin, the latt c-oesing the line. The try AWSS disallowed how- ever, owing to the touch-line having been invaded. This VOSS lair I luck far the homeetere,but they were soon compensated. Fish hid barely failed to get in at the corrrr, before, he received the ball at centre and threading his way through lhs whole of the Tyldes- ley haeks,eluded Match and scored near the pests. Thun- ders of applause greeted the feat. Duckworth took the niece kick, but made a poor attempt. Warrington main. tined the pressure, and Aehurst smiing telsherwood,the latter dr Ipned a clever go it. Hardly had the spectators recovere I from their astonishment before Fish was in at the opposite corner ; only to be recalled for in pass for- ward from Dockworth. A penalty gave Warrington the chance of further increasing their lea's, but Mason was unsuccessful. At this stage Tyldesley were not having a chance, their line being continually in danger. Hol- croft was the next man to cross the line, but he too had his try disallowed through stepping in touch. At last France created a diveraion by making a speedy dash down the touch-me, bit his progress was checked by Ashurst before he became really dangerous. His eff sot brought geed fruit however. Warrlngtoo were t)o anxinis to get back te the other end, and being penalised, France kicked a good goal. Warrington roused up again and rushing Makin, the latter carried the ball over. From a scrimmage en the line Miller kicked but forgot the watchful " Smack" who charged the ball down and got an easy try near the earner. Though the position was a very difficult one, Mason this time landed a grand goal. Miller and Berry instituted an attack on the home citadel, but they were repulsed, and half tlme arrived with the score : War- rington, 2 goals, 3 tries (13 points). Tyldesley, 1 goal, (2 points). The second moiety opened in favour of Tyldesley, a Snell of exchange kicking not resulting to Warrington's advantage. Hanson. Miller, and Prance put in good work, the latter making a dash for the line, bat could not get past Ashore. Isherwood responded with a dash- ing run, but his final pass to Duckworth Went wrong. Tyldeeley got possession from the rcrimmag-s frequently, but made poor use of the ball, whereas Bate and Ash- urst were continually on the alert. Nice passing by the tv-meaters SAW Ifiah in pessession, but he could not pass Eigersley who shoved him in touch. Asserting them- selves again Warrington drove the visitors back. The ball went rapidly through the hands of Bate, Asburst Isherwood, Duckworth, and Fish and the latter being again hemmed in threw to Mason who in turn sent to Deakin, the latter completing a magnificent effort near the pests. Mason kicked a goal. Tyldesley rushed the hall to the onposite end and G. Berry got over, but his pass was a shade forward—hard luck on Tyldesley. For a time Tyldedey pressed warmly, but the heme front rank finally relieved with a grand dribble. A miskick by 'Hallam let Tyldesley min again, one of the forwards picking up and having a clear course for the adjacent line, when Duckworth sprinted up from behind and hauled him down. Tim vbitora were having the best of it but lost ground through obstructing Ashurst, theugh they regained it the next minute. and France kicked another penalty goal. Holcrof r, was here prominent with a dashing run to the Tyldesley line, hut he failed to get through. Bate gave him possession again with a long pass, and this time the righ`-wineer was succeesful in snoring far out. No goal resulted from Mason's kick. and "time" arrived a minute later, the conclusion of a fast, exciting game being in favour of Warrington by 3 goals, 5 tries (21 points), to Tyldesley, 2 goals, (4 points.)</p>
        <p>WIG&amp;N v. ST. BELENS. The ssiora " quite outelayod Wigan on the lattor's ground, the final E c lrn bein St. Helens, five goals, Four tries ; Wigan, one try, Traynor, Barnes and Car- eer shared the scoring for the winners. Prescot got Wigan's only try. ROCHDALE HORNETS v. WIDNES. At Rochdale, Batey sc,nred two goals for Widnes, and nlaced another from Peacock's try; whilst W. Rigg drooped tnr-e goals for the Hornets. Result : Widnes, three goals, one try ; Rochdale, three goals.</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS V. SALFORD. &amp;Word visited B oughton and returned home the winnei br two ge 't and a try to nil. Woolhead and Heath dW the ne ' OT:rIAM v. SWINTON. 01,3h5.m on thei- arounimaraq• wn th7s game a a llCPlettj 'o by one try ta nil. LEM II V. MORECAMBE. Thee tramq me. at Leigh. Molyneox secured txo triei, b th of whir h Dug! avin converted fr tilts home fiFteen. who (vpni-- ir won by two goalq and two ttiel to a try (Hadwie).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR. COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR. COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>LEEDS v. HUDDERSFIELD. L. •ia. was the veuue of thi3 match, a grol game 'c- baiting in a. '-in for the h‘ime team by one goal and one to nil. Rogan oot the try. LEEDS PATtiqrd CHURCH v, LIVBRSEDGE. This game at L vai tetribly one-sided,the Church .03 doinT murh liked. and winning by five g'l4 and fo,+- Walker, Fawcett, Malt house sod K&amp;the y,,m-; • liners' score. WAKEI- v BATLEY. A eli-' ga • r alt of thi3 enconntlr I'Vo'refiell, whom Davis dropped three and kccr.,-1 - by thre,. ' "no try to two psis o Thirker and Wl:natter got the PAWS for Wa'o CEi 'YID v. BRADFORD. o:dy one point i., Ihe scores of these teams, at CrNrlrford, the final result reading Bradford nine points, Castleford eisitit. Cooper el "ho seorine for the winners, while Smith and Fletcher got tries for Castle- ford, Townie-rid rvinverting on•. HULL v. HUNSLET. Flunslet hal much the better of this gum. at Hullond fin,dl3: won by a vrAl and two trios to nil. Ws.lsh.Armi. taste and 'Hannah secured the trios, and Wright kicked the a; al. RECTIMONDWIKE v. HALIFAX. This game at Heckmondwike was of a very even char-</p>
        <p>When rankinz for Cocoa., insist on having Csrnsusrrs.—lold on IV in Rickets and Tins as other Cocoas aze often sobAtf- tutei for the take of extra profit,--Advt (I)</p>
