      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>JUNIOR LEWIJE.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LEWIJE.</p>
        <p>_ FLETCHER'S GAS ENGINEERS V. Sr. HELENS WHITE ROSE.—Thil match was played on the ground of the first•named on Saturday, and resulted in a decisive vic- tory for the Warringtonians by 18 points to nil. During the first half trics were scored by Scott and Evans, neither of whlch were improved. lii the concluding stage the leaders claimed nearly the whole of the attack, and Scott, Radfgrd, and Evans adding tries, the En- gineers won by 19 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WARRINGTON Sr. MARX'S V. LOSTOCN GRALAM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON Sr. MARX'S V. LOSTOCN GRALAM.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Lostock on Saturday. Dur- ing the tint half Burton almlst scor3d for the visitors after a brilliant run. Loetock retaliated, and the out- come of some clever clay by the backs resulted in May scoring, this being the only tangible poirr, obtained up to the interval. During the second half Glynn and Faulkner added tries Lir the home team, whilst Burton scored in brilliant style for the VV,irringt)nians, this player also ed:ng the goal. The final result read as follows i—Listoir Graham, three tries (nine points) ; %Varrington St. Mary's, one go,' a9d a try (rive points)</p>
        <p>MEETING OF TdE NORTHERN UNION COMMIT TEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Northern Rugby Union held on Tuesday night, at th 3 George Hotel, Huddersfield, under the presidency of Mr. Waller (Brighowe), some impor- tant business was transacted. A long investigation was made into th 3 case of Badger, of Swinton, who went away for a whole week to visit his sick child. Applica- tion was made by the Swinton club to the hon. secretary of the Nerthe:n Union miring for his opinion as to whether Badger could play on the following Saturday, and that offic'al expressed a :Anne opinion against Badger playing. But, notwithstanding this, Swinton played Badger in their match against Leigh. The com- mittee now decided that Badger was not in employment during toe week previous to playing against Lett, and ' the Swinton Club, therefore. had broken rule 1, section (c), and fined th 3 club £25. Badger was exonerated from blame in the matter, the responsibility fallin enTtihreelyyourpkoAte CSovui,natyoncooffinn cri ailtet.ee r orted rthat the Hull club had broken rule 1, section (e), of the [rofes- s:oaal laws in not rep yrting that Savage had changed his eniployment, and ellowiag Ifm to play for them in the meantime. The Hull club was fined £5 and Savage suspends] for one month. A dispute having occurred between Hull and Hall Kingston Revers as to whether J. H. Davies was entitled to ploy for ether club, a long in- vestigatioa ensued. Kingston Rovers based their case on the ground that D ores hid played with them against I Widnes as an amateur an been paid for hei services. Thly, th Tefore, claimed that ha Wl3 a professional mernb3r of their club, although they admitted that he had not signed any professional form. flull,on the other hand, claimed Davies on the gronad that he bad signed a professional f orm for them, and had not signed for any other club. They were, therefore, entitl ed to his I services. The committee decided in favour of Hull and further found that, as Hull Kingston Rovers and Davies had broken rule 1, Section (b), of the professional rules in paying and receiving money 'whilst .playing as an amateur, the club be fined £5O and Davies suspended for two weeks. The following sub-committee of seven members was appointed to meet weekly and consider any point raised by the work clause, and to be an investigation committee for all purposes under that clause .—Messrs. Waller (Brfghous.), Hutchinson (Wake- field), Clifford (Huddersfield), Welsh (Stockport), Warren (Warrington), Houghton (St. Helens), and Platt (Old- ham). There were many other matters to eegage the attention of the comeatt-e, but th:se bad to be deferred owing to the lateness of the sitting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BRUNsiVICK WESLEYAN GUILD V. ST. JOHN'S</p>
        <p>GUILD (RUNCORN). Played on the ground cf the former on Saturday. Both teams were a man short Hough failing to turn up for the guild and Haigh being absent from the Sainte' team. St. John's won the toss and decided to play down hill, Cartwright k•cking off for Brunswick, who early on dis- played good form and after a little pressing in troet of goal Captain W. Leathwood opened the Recount of the home team with a grand screw shot. This was soon followed up by goal number 2, Weston receiving the ball ' after some neat passing and easily beat Duttoa with a neat kick. After this play was confined ta midfield where some good tussels were fought but the home team genet- ally obtained the upper hand. From a grand rUII by Cartwright,Ackers and Weston made rings round the St. John's backs and Ackers obliged with No. 3. From now to half time the visitors were playing to obtain a foothold in the home quarters, bet the defence ef the two Leath- woods, Baker, and o'hers was really too good to allow them much chance of obtaining any score. The ball was obtained by the Brunswick forwards, and passing and re-passing right to the St. John's goal, Cartwright banged the ball through. Half time was called with the score four to none in the favour of the home team. Littler commenced the second half with a rush, the Saints came up fie!d only to he cleared by Simkin and Pendlebury. Then the ball was taken down field. Weston and Ackers on the right wing baying things all their own way beat the Saints' half and full-backs Cagily, and puttieg in some fine centres. From one of the posses there was a scrimmage in goal during which Dutton was bustled through by Cartwright while holding the ball in his hands. The St. John's team was changed about a little after this Littler going left half and Harrop centre fo: ward. This did not have any beneficial effect, as the Wesleyan s were in great form, and it was only on account of scme neat clearances by Dutton that they were kept out for some time. Ultimately Lambert and T. Withington were to be seen careering down the left wing and Larnb:,rt with a very good cross kick scored goal number 6. Another change was tried by the Saints. Jones went centre forward and Littler goal,and'on resum- ing pley was kept for some little time in the vicinity of the visitors' quarter ; but after being repulsed With,. Ington hooked the ball tnrough and scored No. 7. Soon after Jones tried a long shot at goal which was the one and only shot received by the home custodian who easily cleared. To the finish the Wesleyans born- beaded the Saints goal and close on call of time. J. W. Leathwood had a long shot which juat got in at the corner. This was taro last goal, and the game ended in win for the home team by eight goals to none.— Notes.—The games was as may be suppoaecl very one- sided, in fact, the Saints never really threatened danger and although well led by Littler, he received no support from any of his team and any effort made by him was wasted by slowness of the ether forwards who dallied too long with the ball and were invariably robbed with the greatest ease by the Wesleyan halves or got in each ethers way. The play of the home quintet was grand, the combination displayed being quite a revelation to the visitors' supporters who could not he'p admiring the ease and judgment with which they tricked their oppon- ents. They outclassed the Saints at all times. Next; Saturday the Guild play Belsby "A" on the Guild ground near Highfield Tannery, kick-off at 3.30 p.m. FRODSIIAM V. CHESTER LOCOMOTIVES.</p>
        <p>These teams met in the Chester and Diatrict Junior League, for the second time this season. On the first essay, uhich took place on the Locos. ground, Frodsham were vanquished by thve goals to nil. Thus an up-bill game was expected. Having commenced operations, it was at once seen that the Loc.:s. had some good men in the field. They soon became dingerous, and for a time Hough was quite busy. Some heavy punching by Adams and Ashton, well supported by Tom Rosere, gave ti:e Frodsham back ample importunities, but it was evident they didn't like to take them. Williams at centre-half made a general mess of affairs, and let in the visitors several times.—At half-time the score sheet was blank.—The ball soon came buzzing about Rough's charge, almost as BOOR as the second moiety was commenced. Towards the latter stac;e; of the tame Thornley became prominent, and was instrumental in getting through fur the home team. 'fhsn it was that Williams thought he ought to do some- thing—for it is certain that he stood on his head. Con- tinuing Frodshana had slightly the better of affairs arid at the call of time were going immensely.—Final remit —Frodsham 1 goal ; Chester Locos, 0 goal—The visitors were slightly superior in tactics and judgmeat, but Frodsham won the day with sheer determination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EARLESTOVJN V. PARR.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOVJN V. PARR.</p>
        <p>For the second week in succession Esrlestown jour- neyed to Parr, on Saturday, to play their fixture in the Lancashire Allisnce. It was thought after their defeat [in the Lancashire Coo Competition the previous week, 1 that Parr would on this occasion be able to reveres mat- ters, but the result showed that they were not quite good ent.ugh tn in victory from the Earlestown lads. Marsh was again absent from the visiting ranks, and Hughes was p‘ayed in his place. There was a goJcl number of epectat ars, and play from the kick off was of</p>
