        <head>MILLS AND BABY.</head>
        <p>MILLS AND BABY.</p>
        <p>MRS. Ei</p>
        <p>Let us see if we cannot interest you in the story which a mother tells ahaut herself and her baby. The lady has kindly sent to us a photograph, which we are very pleased to publish by Way of illustrating the letter which is_as _follows ; " 2, Bock Cottages, Bell Earn Road, " Birminghan:, March 25th, 1898 " Dear Sirs.—l have much pleasure in relating to you the nature of my illness and baby's. Last October I was confined Of a daughter. Three days after confinement I contracted scarlet fever, and was in bed nine weeks. I was cot eXp.Cteito re- cover, but lam happy to eay I dii, but was left very weak and with a terrible hackirg cough. Medicine seemed t be of little us oto me, but I chanced to read one day of the great trnefit people derived by taking Scott's Emolsion, so I thought I would try it. I have been giving it also t I my tabv barely six months old. She was suffering from whooping cough. Now she seldom c ,ughs, and is a strong big child, and as livey as a crick;t. As for myself. I must say I shall nevvr be without Scott's Emulsion in the house, as it has proved a great friend to me. I feel like myself before I had any signs of an illness. Thanking you for the great benefit I have derived, also baby, I am, yours re- pectfully,</p>
        <p>•rating letter suggests will profit much if they will .be influenced by her experience. Tnere is no remedy in the weed so splendidly adapted to mothers of very young infants as Scott's EmulsiQn. This pre- paration enriches the bioed,ensures a plentiful supply of babies natural food, and in this way nourishes and strengthens both the mother and her child. During the period of ei owth of children, Scott's Emulsion is cesential to furMsh material for sound bones, aesist in the making of healthy flesh and make the growth natural and vigor. Scott's Emulsioh COntaics the purest Norway cod- liver oil, .cembined with hypopboaphites and glycer- ine in iniieiatfic proportions, and in a form that is easy on the digestive system. Scott's Emulsion IS an aid to diges, ion, and has a favourable influence upon all children who cannot retain or digest their food. Besides this, it i 3 a great point in favour of Scott's Emulsion that it is co sweet to the taste that children look upon it as a sweetmeat. Scott's Emul- skit has the encorsement of the medical profession, and is effectual where other remedies are of no avail.</p>
        <p>_ You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion hl sending tbreepenco to cover postage to Scott and Boyne, Li mited , Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great Saffron-hill, London, E.C., and mentioning this paper. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>month's imprisonment. They must protect the publid on the highway.</p>
        <p>Teresa Massa, ice cream dealer, Dial-street, was suat*. mooed for furiously driving a pony and cart in Winwick• road on the 7th October. Samuel Bell, cab driver, in the employ of Mr. Edward Connor, said at 11-45 p.m. on the 7th inst. he was dricr• log Mr. Kertoot to Winwick. When nearing Longford Bridge he noticed a cart with lighted lampi coming in the opposite direction. The vehicle was not on the proper side of the road, and he did not suppose tbe driver saw him. She was driving at a very fast pace. He called out to her anil she did ry to pull up, but coula not. Her vehicle collided with his horse, and one of the shafts penetrated the animal's breast about a foot and half and killed it on the spot. There were three wooer' in defendant's cart, and as far as they could make out they were thrown over the horse's back into the road. 3umped off the cab and Mr. Berfoot got eut.--The,. Chief Constable : Were nct a number of these peoP,' racing together ?—Witness : Well, they were corning rather fast.—The Chief Constable : Is it not a regular thing for these people to race ?—witness : That i 3 quit! right. Someone said to defendant "You should Del' drive so fast. You have been told about it before. De" fendant appeared to be very sorry. Defendant : I tried to pull the horse back; 1 was 11) racing anyone. Frank W. Kerfoot, sanitary inspector for the Ware rington Rural District Council, said he was being driVefi to Winwiek on the night in question. His driver wog perfectly sober. As a matter of fact he examined laihs before they started, to make sure. (Laughter.) was driving on his proper side of the road when witnefe noticed a conveyance coming in the opposite directioll at a very rapid pace, he might, say a reckless pace. Q. could see the other vehicle coming straight towares them, and the next moment a crash took place and lit found that the shaft of the other vehicle had penetrate" into the cab herse's breast. Martha Jones said she was in defendant's cart at elf! time. She and the othf r young woman did not fall otiD but were lifted out of the cart. Defendant was driVirli rather fast, but it was somewhat awkward, as they be" a new horse in the shafts. The Chairman said that as this was the first time de* fendant had been before the Court, though the offeec: was a serious one they were inclined to take a lenico view of it, and would fine her 203. She was liable 06 fine of 40s. and costa.</p>
