      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FURIOUS DRIVING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>FURIOUS DRIVING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>---- • TWO ANIMALS KILLED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrint,ton 80r0u,211 Police Ciurt on Mon- day, before Mr. Birchall (in the chair) and Councillor Smethurst, William Plinston,general broker,sB, Howard- Street, Sankey Bridges, was summoned for having been drunk in charge of a horse and cart in Winwiek-road,on October 20th, and with driving furiously at the same time and place. Peter Derbyshire, labourer,qaid he was driving a pony and cart, belonging to Mr, William Lowe' cloth mer- chant, Winwick, along Winwick-road, at five to six on the evening of the date mentioned, and when nearly op- posite the Blue Coat School he saw defendant driving towards him. Defendant was on the wrong side of the road, and was driving at a fast pace. He called out to defendant, but he did not alter his course lit r his pace. A collision took place, and the pony witness was driving was so badly injured that it had to be destroyed. The shaft of defendant's vehicle entered Its breast. He spoke to defendant but he only replied with bad language. He was drunk. Witness met P.C. Chrimes, and reported the matter to him. P.C. Chrimes said be was on duty in Winwick-road shortly after bix,when Derbyshire made a report to him. Derbyshire was leading a pony at the time. Witness ex- amined the animal; and found it was bleeding from the breast. He accompanied Derbyshire to Mr. Hewitt, veterinary surgeon, and he said the pony's case was a hopeless one and the animal would have to be destroyed. Witness then went with Derbyshire Codefendant's house. He found that defendant was drunk. Defendant was not in a fit state to make any explanation. Witness ex- amined defendant's vehicle, and found that the off shaft was smeared with blood.</p>
        <p>_ Mary Jane Hankinson, 79. Wiriwich-road, said she had occasion to go to the window of her shop and saw a pony and trap being driven in the direction of Winwick. The driver was on his right side. She also saw another vehicle corning in the opposite direction. That was on the wrong side. The horse was being driven at a reck- less pace. Thera was a collision. Defendant did not stop to e.scortaiu what damage had htea done. He was not s3bei'. He was ask6d to stop a minute, but swore and drove on.</p>
        <p>__„.. The di fendant then spoke on his own tehalf. Be kept on his own side, standing, up in the vehicle, and when he saw De. byt.hire coming towards him he shouted nut. Ile then pulled up. After the collision he said t., Derbyshire " Why didn't you pull on your own side and this thing would never have happened ?" Defendant's own animal was disabled, and he did not know whether it would be fit for work again. His animal might have been ki'led. When he put the quo-- lion he had mentirmd to Derby-hire he made no reply. He (defendant) said "You must have been drunk your- s, For otherwise you would not have come down numy Gide. To indicate where the accident had occurred, he might say that the place where the pony dropped was five feet from tin -urbstore. Jess" Wilsnn seie' he was at Mr. Plinstou's house at eight o'clock that night, and he was not then the worse frr drink.</p>
        <p>Ellen Quinn se,cl cher also saw difendant at eight o'clock, aid he was not the worse for drink. Defraadant : I was more excited at the time. I was as sorry for Derbyshire as I was for myself. The Chief Constable : With regard to the imputation that bas teen made against Mr. Derbyshire as regards his sobriety, I may say that ha was seen by the police Marne"; ..ely after the accident, and there was absolutely not a sign of drink on him. The defendant has hem 48 times befere the Court for various offences. He has a very had record ' The furious and reckless driving in Winwick-road is very prevalen`o especially among these peopie who attend fairs outside. The Chairman said the offence was a very serious one, and it was not the first time defendant had been there for that sort of thing. For the first offence he would be fined 201' and for the second 20s. and costs (£2 183. in all). In default of paynient he would have ta serve a</p>
      </div>
