      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>THE RHONDDA ROYAL GLEE SOCIETY AT PAIR HALL</p>
        <p>The concert given in the Parr Hall on Thursday last was a pronounced success both from a musical and financial point of view. It was the first time the members of the Rhondda Royal Glee Society had paid a visit to Warrington and as their fame load preceded them there were many anxious to see and hear them. The proceeds of the concert are in aid of the Warrington Young Men's Chris- tian Association and as a result, the finances will considerably benefit. The glees by the Rhondda Society were of course the chief feature of the even- ing. Capably led by Mr. Tom Stephens it was not long befere the members found themselves OA solid ground, and the swing and balance of the singing was very flue. The light and shade were fully marked while the volume of sound was rich, sonorous and of good quality. So far as the mere mechanism of harmony is concerned the members may be aaid to have attained perfection. This was especially noticeable in such descriptive pieces as "The Tyrol" (Ambrose Thomas) and " Italian Salad (Genee). Both were masterly expositions of what can be done by a well trained choir under a clever conductor. The most adequately rendered piece of the evening was the chorus "The Pilgrims" (Dr. Parry), in which the solo part was effectively taken by Mr. Dav:d Jones, A.R.A.Ist The efforts of the choir, about 30 in num- ber, were greatly appreciated by the audience as was evideneed by the hearty applause and recalls which were demanded, and freely responded to. Coming to the soloiats, the first place must be given to Mr. David Jones, A.R.A.M.., whose fine baritone voice was beard to great advantage in the songs " Blow, blow 7 (Sergeant) and " Queen of the Earth " (Pinsuti). Both are excellent songs and they received careful interpretation at the hands of Mr. Jones. Hewes deservedly encored and complied with a laughinz song. Madame Williams-Penn, R.&amp; M., made her debut before a Warrington audi- ence by singing Gounod'e "Serenade in F" with violin obligato. This was ouly a partial success, the muting of the violin bAng to some extent accountable for it. The vocalist was heard to greater advantage in her second effort when she essayed "For all eternity" by Angelo Maecheroni, and received an encore. She also sang in the humorous duet "Sol-fa" (Bennett)in company with Mr. Jones, and at the close of the concert she again appeared awl sang the sotto part in the National An- them, the chorus being taken up by the audience. Mr. Tom Salathiel poi.tissies a tenor voice or a rather hard quality, nevertheless, he gave a good interpretation of such old favourites as "Good Company"and"Alice where art thou." It is only fair to add that he was encored in the latter song and complied with "My Pretty Jane." The Pennillions by Eos Dar with harp accompaniment in which both English and Welsh were intermixed were a decided innovation and both interesting and amusing. The instrumental portion of the concert was well sustained by Miss Bessie Jones (harpist) and Miss Mabel Tansley (violinist). The harp solos of Miss Jones were greatly appreciated. Her playing of " Welsh Melodies" (Thomas) and " Greek Pirates " (Alvers) was characterised by great depth of feel- ing and delicacy of touch. Miss Taut.ley is a young violinist of great promise. Both the Legende " and "2nd Mazourka " by Wieniawski " received careful and musicianly treatment. Com- plying with a recall aftr the first-named piece she gave a soulful interpretation of the Intermezzo from "Cavaliera Rusticana." The pianoforte accom- paniments were played with much taste by Mr. Percie G. Smith</p>
      </div>
