      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting o[ the P.S.A. on Sunday atter- noon was well attended. The Chairman Lr the afternoon was Mr. 1). France, and the chief speaker was Mr. W. Plant, ot Ilaydock. Recita- tions were given by Miss Coatis, and vocal selec- tions were submitted by Miss Wright. Mr S Dring presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>THREE MONTHS or THEFT. At Liveip3ol County Quarter Sessions on Tues- day, Mary Riley (65),0f no occupation, was indicted for stealing tnree stockings, valued at 25., the property of Jemima Dloyle, from the backyard of tt.e latter's hou.,e at Newton-le-Willow3,ou the 10th September last, and was sentenced t 3 Oree months' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH CHOIR.. Special services in ail of the choir of above church were held on Sunday. The pulpit at the morning and evening services wos occupied by the Rev. R. Crozier, formerly resident minister at Earlestown, who delivered interesting sermons. In the evening a short musical service was given by the choir, solos, duets &amp;c, being excellently ren- dered, the principal soloists being Mr. F. P. Clare, Misses F. Holliday, M. Barr, and E. Clarke. A ser- mon was afterwards delivered by the Rev. R. Crozier. Collections were made in aid of the choir funds.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT To A Bo Y AT WARGRAVE. On Wednesday afternoon a la I named James Gibbs employed at Mr. Lawson's brass works, War- grave-road. Harlestown,met with an accident which necessitated his removal to the Manchester Infir- mary. He was turning a grind stone by the handle when the strap broke, and the handle catching his jacket pocket, turned him over, and on the next revolution the handle came into violent contact with his knee. He was remove' on the ambulance litter to Dr. Mouncey, when it was found that the knee cap was splintered,and he orderel his removal to Manchester, as stated. NEWTON CONGREGATIONAL LITERLRY AND</p>
        <p>„ DEBATING SOCIETY. The first meeting of this newly formed Society was held on Monday evening in tie Congregational Schoolroom, Newton. There were S 4 members pre- sent, Dr. Latham presiding. Recitations and read- ings were given by Mesirs. Hall, Sept:lton and McHardy. Discussions were taken up on vari3us subjects including Emigration, Irish patriotism, the Fla4hoda incident, and fortune making t 3 be derived from induitry. The advice of Polonius to Laertes alvo received a good deal of attention, and many of the opinions offered were of a far reaching character. 1.0.G.T. HoPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE. The usual weekly session was held on Monday preside I over by Bro. T. Cutler. One candidate was admitted. Digest of the No. 1 Convention was read. Next meeting to be held at Wigan on the 12th. The secretary read the report of the 2Gth Anniversary showing a good balance. It was decided to visit Golborne where a lodge was expec- ted soon to open; and to visit the ledge at Haydock and give them an entertainment. The secretary , read the new Quarterly programme, inch was ordered to be printed, A paper was then read by Bro. E. Newman D.5.1.T., on "The Good of the Order,” or" How to improve our Lodge meetings," thanks being tendered to the writer for the interest- ing paper. There was a good attendance. EARLESTOwN PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Special services were conducted in the above place of worship on Sunday in aid of the choir funds. The pulpit was occupied at morning and evening services by the Rev. E. Bocock, resident minister, but except in the evening the congrega- tion was not large. At the morning service the choir sang the anthem " Oh ! come let us sing," and in the evening " Rise and shine " and " Stand up and bless the Lord," the soloists being Miss Wo)ds, Mr. W. Sims and Mr. P. Tomlinson. In the after- noon the cantata 'The Promised Land" was very efficiently given by the choir under the conductor- ship of Mr. W. Banton, the various solos being c3n- tributed by Misses Toombs, Baxter, Stronacb and L Dickens, Messrs. J. Webster, A. Banton and J. Austin. Mrs. Latimer presided at the piano and Mr. A. Banton at the harmonium. Thera was a very fair gathering and the service was much enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ST, HELEN.</head>
        <p>ST, HELEN.</p>
        <p>APPOINTUENT FOR ASr EIELENT _SOLICITOR.</p>
        <p>The Visiting Cornmittse of th Rainhill Asylum have appointed Mr. Thomas Gtrcrr, of the firm of Nless:s. Swift and Garner, solicitor 4, S. F.lklen, law clerk in succession to the late Mr. William Swiic.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS CASES AT LIVERPOOL.SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpo 1 County Quarter Sessions oa Tuesl Catherine Alountford (42), Isundress, for a-rating clott- ing at St. Efelens. was cent to ga )1 for Ores months. George Naylor, (25). Labourer, charged with breakiag and entering the house of James Buechel, at St. Helens, on the lette September, was sant to gaol for four months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT AT COLLINS _GREEN COLLIERY,</head>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT AT COLLINS _GREEN COLLIERY,</p>
        <p>abocking accident occurred at the Collins Gre3n Colliery, near St. Helens, owned by th 3 Collins Green Co'liery Company, on Wednesday iiv.r.aing. Stmu'l Jones, 48 years of age, of Choral-terrace, Callins Gteau, was following his customary etnplo;ment as a collier, when a portion o the root of h,4 workto; plans gave way, and a large stone dropord up- n him. Assistance was readerei, bit lite wa' rv•inc: whca Iji b)(17 exticated. 001 V INFECTION IS SPREI.D.</p>
        <p>Sarah Jump, a married woman, 01 69, Ashcrof;-stre=t, was charged at St. Helene Police Court on M )nday wi:h exposing her child, Jrhn Jump, while sufferin.,. Iron scarlet lever.—Tne Town Clerk (Mr. Jeeves) prosecuted and stat.d that the defendant hal allowed a daughter to be removed ti the Sanatorium ; but when her boy. , one year and ten months' red,had contracted t3e discos s she refused t o allow him to es, to the hospital. Sne re- ceived instructions from Inspector Coates, and was warned to be careful, but notwithstanding nat the inspector afterwards found the child in the house of a neighhour.—Mrs. Jump said that her husband was a carter for the neighbour in question, and the child ha got through the back yard.—The beic3 said they mul protect the public, anl they imposed a firno of 55., in- ' gludiog costs. THEFTS FROM A GLASSWORKS. On Monday. at St. Helens Police Court. Thomas D .rbyshire, cf 3. Grove-stree-, and Joha Mort, of 8, Grove-street, wme charged with stealing a pair of lurry wheels, value £3. the property of Messrs. Pilkington Brothers, glans manufacturers, and Thomas Ball, of Burtonhead Farm, were charged wits receiving the wheels well knowing them to hive lo,en Ft )len.—M-,,..9. L. Riley appeared for Ball.—Chief•cnnvhble Wcoi that Derbyshire and Mort were labourers at Messrs. Pilkington's works, and Ball was a farmer and coal dealer. The wheels were missel from the works on tor 23rd August, and on Saturday last they were fr,und at Bell's farm by Constable Lyon, to whom Bell stated that be did not know who brought them there at first, but he afterwards said that Derbyshire and ano-,11, r m an did 41. Be denied that he had bought the whst-ls from the men, Both Derbyshire and Mort said that Ball had given then , morriy for drinks when thy took the wneell.—Mr. Riley, on Ball's behalf, said that his client pleaded not guilty to the charge. He lowl lived in S Helens for 40 years without a blemish on his character, and he urged that Mr. Ball was entirely innocent in the travant i n. Dcrbyshire anii Mort were each sent to prison for three months. and Ball was committed for trial at the next qua.ter sessions, bail being Llioed. EARLESTOWN, NEWTON', HA.YDOCK, &amp;a, )JUILDING SOCIETY. il..==</p>
      </div>
