      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 4 1898</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 4 1898</p>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION CONSERVATIVE</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>TUE EON. T. W. LEGEI. M.P., ON FASEIODA AND</p>
        <p>RITUALISM. - A meeting of the Newton Division Conservative Assn- ciaton was held on Saturday at the Town Hall,Newton. Mr. C. B. F.Borron presded,and there were also presflC tie Hon. T. W. Leers, M.P., Meesr.s. R. Stone, T. J. Gil- lespie, R. Pilkington, W. Holland-Owen, J. Robinson, Catterall, Barton and others. Votes of condolence were passed to the families of the late Mr. C. F. Clark 3 (Ashton), and Mr. R. Stuart (New- ton). Mr. Pilkingtoa proposed a vote of thanks to the Hon. T. W. Legh for his presence that afternoon, and ako for the able and efficient menner in which he represeated toe Newton Divisioa in Parliament. Mr. R. Barton seconded and referred to the agitation on the subject of Ritualism in the Courch of Engmn I, expressing confidence th3.t Mr. Legh would take a sensi- ble view of it when the subject came up in the House of Common;. Mr. J. Robinson supported, and dealt at length on the subject named by th previous speaker. In tee past they had trusted the Bishme would put matters right. They had not done so, and now nothing could be done, but ta ask Parliament 0 deal with it. Be was of opinion that if these ritualistic principles were put in the minds of the children of the thy schools by their cler-gymea it would result in the establishment of Board Schools, and the disestablishment of the church. Many had been driven te ateeism by the went of sympathy by the clergy. (Applause.) A large number of members of Parlament of constituencies round teeirs had promise i support in this matter, and he hoped M. Legh would do the same. (Applause.) Mr. Catterall protested that be was as good a churchman as Mr. Rebinsoe, bat he did not think that was the time and Wax t melei tse re nteci woieh had been made. The Cheirmin said he personally sympathise I with Mr. Robinson somewhat, bat he thought he was wide of the mark and spoke prematurely when he referred to the influence of the clergy in the schools. (Hear, hear.) The Hon. T. W. Legh in reply said the most important subject before them was that of Fashoda. After giving a small accountof the whole subject be elid if Marchand a as an emissary of civilisaVon, his work was dose, and he should now retire.but it had been acknowledge i tnat that was not so. Lord Selisbary had been perfectly prudent in this metter. and it was now understood that the present position of Marchaed was not to be tried as a ground for further concessims. This was a policy approve i by the whole country. He could not remember the time when public opinion was so unanimous on a subject. The opposition had been just as emphatic as the Government. The question was, would it lead to war ? There were two dangers : First the Russians might enter into an alliance with the French ; secondly tne internal differences in France were such as might lead the Government to make war to distract public attentinn front them. In Case it was a maritime war, they had no fear. It was time to end cenceasioes. On the subject of ritualism he had hid that afternoon a mild shock. Ht 3 might say he was a moderate man. He was not an orangetnan, and still less was he a ritualist. Th re should, he thought, be more tolerance on both eidt-s. An orangeman might think he had had undue provocation, but he thought there had been some slight exaggeration in the state- ments of that sftornon. Be did :not think that the difficulty was so widespread. Mr. Robinson was of opinion that an Act of Parliament was needed, but he (Mr. Legh) wits not of that opinion yet. 119 did not demair of the Btshop3, but asked that they would give them another chance. He urged on them patience, and coeciudtd by saying he was not preposed to support a drastic bill, the provisions of whin h 3 was in perfect, ignorance of. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>RHONDDA ROYAL GLEE SOCIELY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>THE RHONDDA ROYAL GLEE SOCIELY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>ra connection with their visit to Lancashire, the above renowned choir paid a visit to Earlestown on Tuesday evenin: in connection with the United Welsh Churches of the town, ad in aid of the funds of these churches. The fame of this choir had preceded it, and conse- quently the Town Hal was crowded with an enthusiastic anl highly appreciative audience, who appiaiaded vociferously every item sub nitted by the choir. Con- sidering the expense attached to the engage:nent of the choir, the Welsh bodies of Earlestown have indeed been most fortunate in securing their services, but the public have responded heart ly to their call, and probably there will be a balance for the ben:fit of the churches. Those who were present will certainly not regret the visit, and all must admit that a musical treat in glee singing was afforded them, the like of whicn has not previously been beard in Earlestown. Evenly balanced, with perfect harmony, the choir, consisting as it does of male voices only, can easily hpld its •wn with any other organiza- tion of the kind in the country.. The conductor . has perfect command of the singers, whict was quite patent in the opening ite.n"Cornradei in Arms,"wilich at once placid the audience on good terms with themselves and they immediately called for more, a call to which the choir were sot slow to respoad. "Ihe same ocourred in all the orlon. glees " N.gat and Day," "Toe Tyr )1," " The Pilgrim," "Italian Palad,"ar..l"S oldiers chorus " from "Faust." When everything is exceptionally good it is a most d.ffi task to single out any particular g'e3 as the beet, bot "Toe Tyrol" and "Italian Salad " appearedlto call forth the most appreciation, the singing of the former causin: a perfect furore. 'ale choir re- spcn led to ea :h reca'l, singing "Men of Harlech," and "Annab Lee." Of coarse the choir was the centre al- traction,the other items in the programmemezely serving an side lights. Mid MIS Williams Peaa, however,proved that she possessed* powerful soprano vioe,anl her sing- ing of "Tee Swallows," and "Dearest Heart," was much aperyciated. Eos Dsr sane wirh harp acoompar. iment "Penni! ion";n Welsh and Eaglish,the catchy mu tic, and quaintness; of the verse, Tate tak ng the fancy of the audience. Miss Bessie Jones proved herself a capable performer on the harp, and Miss Mabel Tansley showed by her manipulation of the violin that she is an arciate of no mean ability. Mr, David Junes, who is the possessor of a powerful bass voice eau: in splendid style "Blow, blow,' and "Queen of the Earth" receiving a recall for the litter. Mr. Tom Salathlel a tea-r, gave a good interpretation of the song "GoI company " and for "aliens, where art thou." he receive al eacsre,responding with "My pretty Jane." Just before the close of the programme, an official 6tippd forward and said they were much obliged to the audience for supporting Cher friends in the manner they had done, but it would be impossible for the choir to coins t 3 Earlestown again, unless the p ice of- the front seats, and other pa:ts of the hall, was doutiel. On February 13th, 1899, the choir would appesr ir the Parr Hall, Warringtha, and he b mought the audience for their own sakes, as soon as tine plate was open, to go to Warrington and byik anie seats or there wiuld Is none to have. He gave them early notice, bedause there were only two men in Warrinron whO knew that the choir was eragaeed for toe dat earned. Th 4 s aging of the "Land of my fathers '' eel " Go I save tho Queen," brought the concert to a close.</p>
        <p>I•••••...••••••••</p>
        <p>rice</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY. The servicem connection with the above society '1 on Sunday Wfti lairly-well .attended. Mr: T. Haw- ' thorn presided and in a brief address referred to the ! responsibility attaching to hearers of the Gospel, Mr. Nicholson Smitn,of Nianchester,was the speaker awl delivered a very interesting and practical ad- I dress based on the parable of the prodigal son. Mr. wollacott sang as a solo " Behold Me standing at the door." Tne Rev. John Yonge and Mr. Waetuall I - took part in the service. MEETINGS OF' SPIRITUALISTS Miss E. A. Smith occupied the platform at the Academy-street Temperance Hall on Sunday after- anon and evening. In the afternoon the subject of the aild:ess was "Virny Spiritualism is neecie 1." and in the evening "Was Jesus God?" Both ad tresses w4re attew ively listened to. At the close of the afternoon service eleven clairvoyant delineations were given, and in the evening sixteen. On Aon- day evening the meeting was held in the Druids' Hall, which was well tilled. A few good tesss were given in psychometry, principally to strangers.</p>
        <p>BIBLES. PRAYRItso /LIMN BOOKS, AIDUMS, Purses. to.—Jo( S. GREZ.V, Stationer Sce,, 34 Bridge- street, Wurmigton. WYCLIFFE CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR, SOCIETY. On Monday eveulaz a Cogcert was given by the above society in WycLi&amp; Hall, the proceeds being tor the bazaar fund in aid af the Rigby memorial. There was a eplendid programme given hebre a crowded end interested audience. The concert was opened by the Rev. John Yonge's address. Miss Kershaw sang Dream of Paradise,' with 'cello and vi.iliti obligato. Mr. W. J. Fard gave two clarionet solos in his us ial ma,terlv s..yle. A trio in which Me:srs. Ford. Pear,od and Parker, took part eswe I much amusement. Mr. ti. Wallington gave two Isongs entitled Tan Englishman" and "To e Seep Man" the latter causing much merriment among the audience. Mr. W. Parker gave a song which was well received. Mr. G. Ford rendered a very comical recitation entitled " Dr. Quack." Miss lleaa's recitation" In liowton's Yard " was very well delivered. Miss Bauchier's violin solos (us- played that yaung lady's musical powers very well. A liumorou, duet was given by Miss Kershaw and Mr. It Wallington, botn of whom iinsd their parts admirably. The cancrt was closed by a drama " The Pack Judge," which took the audience by storm and made the room re-echo with latizhtet. The performers were Messrs. W. J. F-ird. as judge, Mr. W. H. Wallicigtoa, lawyer, Mr. G. Po-d. P.C. , 131,,ze:a 100,000 1.. Mr. W.Parker, Llewellyn Woodee- Poi, Mr. A. Gleave, as Mike Dolan, and Mes Morris as Mrs. Peculiar.</p>
