      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MEETING IN SUPPORC OF MR. MALKIN'S CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>A. meeting in supP;rl.--ot:-.M-r:-T1-1-0-M. 35 Broomhall Mal- kin, the Liberal candidate. for Be weep Ward was held in Catherine-street, Bewsey-road, on Saturday aftEraoon last. Councillor Shaw Green presided, and there were also present on tO9 lurry Mr. Malkin, Councillor Tinnion Messrs. Lang, Healy, and Sambrook. The CHAIRMAN said that there was a general impres- sion in the ward slit Mr, Malkin ought t ) be one of the thre.‘ representatives in the Town Council, and that ime prersion they wanted to mske a fact !Inc. Tuesday. Mr. MALKIN said he had iive.l and wv,rk'et in B!wsey Ward for the past 29 y-ari, a 1,1 he, therEf ire, cd.iined to know its requiremenrs. He ha I been round the ward ears and years ago nefore I 3 thought of entering the C milli, and he could see things which ought to be im- proved. In his electim addr:ss he had told them that he would e ideavour to obtain cleaner and brighter gas, which, as they knew, was wanted. Ile would advocaie the making tire river Mersey navigable for the co:nmerce and trade of Warrickgen. If elected, he would try to compel the Manche tir Sdip Canal Company to fulfil their agrvments with the Warrington Corporation in keeping the river navigable,so • hat trade alight be suc- cessfully carried on. He would alr:o se :hat contracts were given to local tradesmen, and that the men em- ployed w els pail at tr ids union rates, and have proper hnurs. (Hear, hear.) He ha I served on the Board of Guardians for the past four years,and had obtained for several officials of the Workhouse, higher wages, better terms and hours. He took an active interest in all suL- j-cts which had come before the Board of Guardians,and tie always gave his views upon them. He advocated trade soc'e lee, and contended mat any man showing his certificate of membership at the Union should be given a proper supper and bed, and allowed to go on his j ourney seeking work the following morning without doing a task. (Hear, hear, ani4 applause.) Councillor TINNION s rid teat if any ward in the town should be represented by a working man it should be Bewsey Ward, and they had c mfidence in Mr. Maikie as their representative on the Board of Guardians He sail that something ouch; to he done at once with re- gard to the Mersey as spoken of by Mr. Malkin, Boats went ashore two or three times a week. Councillors Shaw Green and Pierpoint bad done all they could in the matter, and they wart ,d someone to hack them on. If Mr Malkin were elected he would do h s ! utmost to help them and for the good of the ward. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>The CHMRMAN said that during the past two years many important events had taken place in the town. Sanitary matters and paving in the Bewsey section had been grealy improved. As ne was coming to the meet- ing things were pointed out to him which wanted do'ng but he could tel thorn the reason, the land did not b, - loog to th. Corporation. Very important things would have ti tra done in the near future. They were havi ,g n.w police buildings, wrii3h were an abiolute necessity. Serious E.t eat improvements would hive to be effeoted. Look at the present desolate state of Bridg,r-street The Co orr.il were careful in the matt!rs, and wanted to get fair And equitable pric ,s, so as not to burden the ratepayers of the town. They wantscl a new bridge at Bridge-foot, as a great deal of traffic to Manchester and Liverpool hid to o over the bridge, which was blocked at timei with the enormous traf. They needed a tech- nical scion./ and an abattoir ani a small-pox Inspiral and the fever hospital should be enlarged. Also another reservoir was required and tramways and eletric light- ing. They would again have to go to Parliament to have these improvements passed. A new school was needed fo! Bewsey, but Mr. Smethurst reported that they could nos see their way to the ere.ltion of one at presmt.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. LANG of the Trades Council also spoke in favour of Mr. Malkin's candidature, and the meeting shortly afterwards terminated. A TIE IN BEVVSEY WARD, It can hardly be said that the Municipal elections in Warrington have aroused as much interest • this year as on former ocasions. This may be accounted for in several ways. In the first place the mere fact that only five of the wards were being contested minimised the interest consider- ably. The fortunate candidates who were allowed it" walk over" were: —Councillor F. Monks ia Whitecross Ward, Councillor Burton in St. Austin's Wind, Councillor Wilkinson in Orford Ward, unl Councillor Carmen in Howley Ward. It will be seen that three Liberals were returned unopposed, and one Conservative. Another element contribut- ing to the quietness of the elections was the fact that only two meetings were held. The majority of the candi- dates contented themselves with publishing their addresses and placards bearing upon their claims for the honourable position which they sought to occupy. Then again no definite question has been placed before the electors for an expression of opinion thereon as has been the case in former 'elections. Tuesday was the day fixed for the poll- ing, and fortunately fine weather prevailed, and little difficulty was experienced in getting electors to the poll. Both sides were well supplied with vehicles and workers, although the Liberals suf- fered somewhat in this connection owing to the , polling day being on a Tuesday. During the day the Mayor (Alderman Wright) accompanied by the Town Clerk and Alderman Blackhurss vi-ited the several polling stations, travelling from place to place in a cab. Oa a visit bin:: odd to the polling statioos, little excitement was noti able in any of the wand. At an early hour in the mo ing, Mr. Horton and Mr. Heaton F , were to be se in irate nixing in front of Kenirick-3treec ' Infant School, where the polling booth in Town Hall .Wad was situated. Tee pelting proceeded very Slowly during tile morning. There was a good display of mural literatu ein the Ward. Mr. Horton has issued a large poster in white letters on a red g ound with a blue border asking the electors to '• Vote for dorton." A cir- cular, whieh was well distributed, read as follows :- 4‘ Vote f or James Horton, because he resides in the water nd knows its want;, Because he will look after the best interests of the ratepayers. Because he will join the youneer memhers of the Council in preventing cliqese or party politics standing in the way eaf the best interests of the town. Because Ohe Gas Committee (Robert Heaton, vice-chairma3) ' has spent ineney out of revenue and caleed ceasuiners to pay a top price for gas. Because, if elected, he has time to attend to his duties and has no selfish interest; to serve. Because he will suecort the ' introduction of Corperation tramways, electric lighting, and all improvements that will be of benefit to the elsctors. Consider well these reasons and vote f-r James Horton." One who signed himself" en old True 1Blue" appealed f r votes for Mr. Heaton on the follow- ' leg grounds :—" Working men of Warringtoa, beware of the Radical independent candidates, who have taken I donut the react flag temporarily, and are using a neutral I colour as calculsted to entrap unwary Conservatives , into voting for them. Vo:o straight for the COUSerVa• ' titre candidates, and return them all at the head of the po 1 by large majorities on Tuesday,November let, 1858. —Yours truly. an Old True Blue." Proceeding t) Bewseysward, small crowds were to he van co'lected rowel the committee rooms in Gannett- ✓reet and Hoyis--itrest where tie two 13 !ling etttiene were situated. Boot candidates were hard at wo k end there wan every evid ince of a keen etruzgle taking place Mr. Malkin was splendidly assisted by a nurnher of ironworker.. Amongst the appeals tc the electors were the foil owing :—.• Don't f rget Malkin's services." "I'm vetine for Malkin." " Play up, Bewsey, score well for Malkin," " Mr. Rogers save, 'Vote for the bet man." ' e The best man is Malkin." For Mr. Regers. one who aignd himsslf "A Catherinr-itreet Forgeman," appealed to the voters by giviee a cemplete deaial to certain st.te peals which had boon made with regard to Mr. Etaa'r,, which ere characterized as '1 a pack of f dee- herds and lies." The placard went on to state that Mr. , Rogers bed proved himeell to be the workingman's true friend : that he had always advocated the st pada rd rate of wages in all Corporation contract tethat he had never Vlsed ova any party question, and that he had acted indripondently, In St. Jahn's ward, p.rhaps the grrateet interest was evinced. The polling etat ices was at toe Old Club Room, 3 .hio-street,and the Liberal and Conservative Committee rooms were directly opposite. Mr. William Farness was to he seen hard at work and he was assisted be a num- b rof workers—`er too small a number for such a large , distria '. Prominent on the other aide were Mr. Baird land Courcillor John Reherrs. Mere morel literature want; bf 3 seen in this ward nate any other. Stretched across the street near the polling station were displayed elle mottoes... Furnoss for St. John's Ward. '• Vote for Turn, as, even has given I Foe cheaper gas, and will get it ch"" still." " A donation of £lO will be paid to the tn., !anti-nary if nv onrionent can prove that the pro- uti.es m de are not heirs; carried ollt.—W. Furr""*" Other requests on the walla were, " Vote for Furness and lower rats." " Vote for Furness, your tried representative, who fulfils his promises." The Co eservatives for some mysterious reason issued a nlacerd hearing the following words :—" Mulicirial Eleetion, 1898 Corrupt and Illegal Pract.ces Act, 1884. £2O reward. It having come to our knowledge that some persons are going about attempting to influence voter; he bribery, the above reward is offered to ally- on, who will furnish such substantial information as will lead to thP convictio lof any Person or rersons giving, 'r oceiving or promising anything in the form of a brib at these elec.ions. (Signed) Warrington Coe- serva ive Assneihtio ." A number of wild promises wer made by Mr Baird in the following terms :—"Vote fur Baird ant the biz leaf," "Vote for Baird and batter streets'." " Blird and chelner gm," " Baird and open crates for the peels," " Baird wet the abolition of all nei•ancea." . . .</p>
        <p>The vating at Fairfield ward during the earlv part of the morning was yea. iy. but it slackenel somewhat</p>
        <p>about 11 o'clock. Towards dinner titan?, however, when the neighbouring works disgorged their employs, the pilling re:, sive t an impetus. The neighbouring hoard- ings d so!aved appe Is to the voter's in assertive if not always convincing language. Some of the bills were as follows :—" Reserve your vote for Duncan, your tried repr: se stative." " Cooper and open spaoes fir the public ; immediate pavement of the new streets ; better and cheaper gas ; and efficient cleaning and lighting of the Areas. Vote for Coop r the practical man, for 14 year 3 a member of the Rural Sanitary Authority." "Vote for the most practical and tried man,David S. Duncan." At times there was plenty ta interest one. A querulous laoking old man totlered up, evidently brim full of curiosity and began to sure y the scene. The eagle eye of oae candidate alight id on him, when the following dialogue took place : Candidate : (eagerly) Well, are you looking for me ? Old man : Ni sir. Candidate : ! Have you a veto P Old man: No sir. The Candi- dee's interest evaporated, and he retired, something evidently requiring his personal attention. After six o'clock the polling c.inm ,nceil in earnest, the bu'k of the voters being free to exercise their privilege in leisurely fashion. The voting at Latchford Ward was on a par with other wards for the same time of the day. There were , the usual small bills. Mr. Ellison was described as the " popular " candidate and the mm who looked after tilt workingm ire's interest." An appeal was made for the , return of Mr. Sampson, which it was stated would result in the immediate openin., of Victoria Park,and which it was alleged had been negleoted by Mr. Ellison for two years. Mr. Sampson was described as an advocate of a fair day's wigs for a lair day's work, and as the best ,o mdidate, the most straightforward man. An emu:Aug discussion was overheard during the morning. A Small knot of voters were talking about a " pint of bear and a vote." it seemed to be rather a proble nto them what one should do in case of an offer of the former for the latter, until a man, who in dress looked like a horse thaler, with cast of cour.t•nance inseparable from one who is brsught lnt,c,otsct with horses, conclusively proved to his own satisfaction that the. b )st thing to do would be to" tak pint and vote. as he wanted." Voting livened up later in the day at both the polling stations.</p>
      </div>
