        <head>VIE POLLING ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>VIE POLLING ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>A..leq ate di lane no aca na a been made by the Town Cle•lr tor tie electio 1. The polling s ations were situa- ted in the following places :—Cown Ha I Ward, Infant Shoo, Kendrick-street ; Bevy Ward, 98, Hoyle-street; and St. Aun's Smoot. Danuetc.street ; St. John's Ward, the Old Club-r rom, John-street ; Fairfield Ward, Fair- field School, off Menehester-road ; and Latchford Ward, St. Jamts's Sohool, Old-road, and Christ Ohurch School, Wash-lane. The following is a list of the presiding officers and poll chorke .—Town Hall Ward : Presiding officers, Messrs. W. 11. Woodcock and A. J. Willett ; poll clerks, eV sirs. E. J. Smith arid it. Starsey. lie weey Ward : Presiding officer, Messrs. T. S. Steele and C. H. Una wort ; poll a rim, Messes. W. 'r. Flood and Lawrence Hot on. St. John's Ward : Presiding officers, Messrs. W. S. Howard, J. Suscliff r. an I T. H. Grange'; poll clerks. Messrs. H. Iloughten, G. F. Freeman, and J. W. Ho wart 1. Fairfield Ward : Presiding officers, Messrs. J. W. Whittle and R. Crompton ; poll clerks, Messrs. H. Lawrence and S. Howarth. Latchford Ward : Presiding offim s, Meesri. jrhe Harrison, T. Cooper and soantey Wileou ; poll clerks, Messrs. F. Tim. periey, A. Give and W. Boyle. roe presiding aldermen wire :—Tow a Hill Ward, Mr. Rooercs ; B3WSPV Ward, Mr. Faimough ; Fahfield Ward, Mr. Blackhurse:, St. John's Ward, Mr. Sutton ; and Lotehferd Ward, Mr. Bolton. Toe Cnief Constable superi,itee lad the olice arrangements which were excellent. THE SCENE Ar THE TOWN tIA.I.•L. Promptly at eight 'clicke the ballit bite; were re- moved from thi polling station' tot ate 'Cowl a ill where the wo:k of colria-I.r the votes was hula, iiately pro- ceeded with in the Council Chamber. A large crowd immediately began to assemble in f:ont of the Town Hall. A poss 3 of pod was statione 1 there but the crowd gave little trouble and the armlet good humour prevailed. They waited patiently-until after nine o'clock when the results in four wards were made known. The exception was in Bewsey ward, and it was well after ten o'clock before the result in this ward was declared. A large crowd waited owaide the Torn Hall to see the last of what they thought would turn out to be an exciting incident. It was rumoured that one of the candidates was " in " by one, and that a re- count was to be made. As time wore on, however, and tio Town Clerk was seen to go to his office to consult his books, it was correctly surmised that there was some question requiring settlenent that could not be put aside by a mere recount. The real fact was that the first count had shown Mr. Malkin to be elec:ed by 319 to 318, but this was excluding a paper in Mr. Rogers' favour which had been temporarily rejected on objet- tion. This paper bad apparently been marked by some blunt instrument, such as a stick, and although the cross was not as distinct as wield base hem had it ' been made by a proper instrument, it was—or appeared so to the returning officer—clear as to whom the voter intended to support. Alderman James Faired nigh there- foe decided decided to allow tiro vote, which made a tie, but he refuse I to give a casting vote in favour at either candi- date, The result was that there was a dead heat, and efforts at a compromise having failed a new election will have to take place. Alderman F airclough announced the figur.3 to the expectant crowd, the supporters of both sides (lee ‘rag Vociferously. Tne results are appended :</p>
