        <p>PRO ;EEDINGS T THE LIBEL CLU3.</p>
        <p>—.— Beyond cneerine again ant *emu for cue euccessful candidates no speechee were cloistered until half-put ten. Councillor F.R.ROBINITS then arrive 1 and stated that as many of theta were iu blissful ignorance at to thy je,ute of the election in B .iresey Ward, be align: sly that the I recount had r !suited in a tie, and thiDsputy-afayn had refused to give his casting vote. t'he electioi w suit, therefore,would have to be fought again. ("nut's fair." "We will give him a tie.") Mr. MALICIN,OII entering the blillard•roona was hear.ily cheered. He sail it was hard lines to hive first woe by ; one vote, and as the result of a recount to find that it was a tie. Toe, had fought a very hard fight, and he heartily tnanked all who had assisted him. They had co: been defeated, and if ttie co nmittee azain accepted him as a c andidat.i. he was prepared to fight over again. (App!ausi, and a \U.:a " And win etsily.") Re must say that his friends had worked with earnestness and zealousness, but they had been dliappoin:ed in slme of those who had beers taken t ) the poll. fle dined say, however, that in the next fight he would be able to con- vsrt them to his side,and the next time he hop.id to win. (Loud applause.) Councillor DUNCAN, wh ) Feu celled howl bra speech, had an enthusiastic ovation. 113 said he was a better worker than a spiaker, but he desired t ) heartily tbalk them all tor the manses in which they kid helped him that day. Nnw he had got held of the ward he meant to stick to it fait. (Applause, and three diens for " Duncan.") If there Was a fight thee at the end of the nun twelve mtnths, ha would not heritate to put hi. shouliPr to the wheel. (Itinewed applause.) lie was nisv thoroeelay tired out and did not wish to say more than express his h .artieit thanks to the workers who had assisted him. (Applause). .</p>
