        <p>At Widnes, ro t k in - the Wat,r-o) and Victoria Wards. 'Val) retiri ig me'ntiets of the oA‘er wards were re-Aec7.ed without opposition, v.z.:—Farn.. worth, Councillor W. Norris lltltn, Councillor W. Christie ; Simms Cross, Councidm G. J. Neil ; and West Bank, Councillor E. F. G)ssage. In Victoria Ward, Councillor J. Carroll did not seek re-election, and the candidates therm were Mr. E Calvert and Mr. W. Palin. The predominan: question at siue was the proposed consecration of the Church of England portion of the cemetery, Mr. Calvert' s opposition to Mr. Patin t&amp;ng a Protest against his action no tip, consecration question. In Waterloo War Councillor fl. B Shores, the retiring member was opposed by Mr. Two L4WS. who declared for consecration, whnrsas Mr. Snores was in favour of the religious cenenooy 0.7 con3ecra-,ion, but again t giv- ing up the legal control of Widnes property ti the diocese of Liverpool. The result of toe voting was _ _ : Waterloo Ward.</p>
