        <head>CLOSE OF TIIE LOAN EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>THE CLOSE OF TIIE LOAN EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>' The close of the exhibition on Saturday was marked by a record attendance throughout the day. In the afternoon the children of the Pedgat trial Schools ware allowe 1 to visit the exhibition free of charge on the invitation Of Councillor A. Woods (chairman of the Arts Exhibition Commit- tee). In the afternoon ria organ recital was given. The artistes were Mr. P. Ranaall (organ), Sergeant Riches (clarinet), and Mr. G. H. Gough (vocalist). The programme was a goad one incluuing overture " Zampe ;" Toreado some, " Carmen ;" song, " Out on the deep ;" and Mendelssohn's War march of the priests." A ventriloquial sketch contribu -e 1 by Mr. A. Grounds was keenly appreciated by the youngsters. The Exhibition and Parr Hall in the evening were crowded to theirutmost capacity.Mr.Alfred R. Sutton's Orchestra was present and contributed the first item on the programme, March, '4 Corneliue," under the conductorship of Hr. Sutton, for which they were heartily applauded. Mrs. Pearson Ara unable to be present, but Madame Nellie Pickton made an excellent substitute, and was encored for her second song, "The Children's Home," giving in response "Love's Old Sweet Song." Mr. J. M. Rate's rendering of " The Sailor's grave" secured a recall and "The Last Watch" was given. Mr. T. N. Johnson sang "Tee storm fiend " and received a good reception. Mr. R. H. Pearson gave violin solos with his usual ability, the more successful being " Faust " and "Tarantelle." The selections by the Urchestre Were admirably executed aril were deservedly applauded. The accompanist was Mr. W. H. Payton Mus. Bee., wno performed his duty with characteristic skill. At the conclusion of the concert Mr. JOHN CROSFIELD raid he was is:ary to say that was the last evening. Trig cornmitee had been asked repeatedly if they could not !tort, it open for another week. They would hay been very glal to do EO DUI it was impossible as they only co: the loin oi th s pic- tures for that month, and also the Gymna•iu n was re - quired for classts next month. Therefore thee were compelled te close the exhibition that night. Be must congratu'ate them and the whole Of Warrington on the IDOst successful exhibition that had ever been hell in the to wit. They had had an attendance of 25,700 people, and Nit day the attend- ance had reached 3,000. This was exceedingly satisfac- tory to them as a committee ani it reflected credit upm the whole town, thet the people should patronise art to such an extent. His parpose we more partioularlydo Move a vote of thanks on their behele and on ben .If of other people in Warriogton to his friend, Mr. Alfred Woods. It was terough him mere particd,arly th it the exhibition had beer such a success, because wehmi him they cold not have got together such a splendid collection of pictures ; neither could tney have ha] such excellent music b,t afternoon aed evening. They were also indebted to th Mayor, Town Clerk, Borough Treasurer and oth irs for helping to m ikg the exhibition a success. They did not corn ne ice with the object of making any profit, and he thought at the finish there would be some 10S3. Bit he dia not care one fig for that. If it cost them £lOO or £2OO. it would be worth the money, and he would rejoice if it was with- in their power to repeat the exhibition and coact:lns every year to come. He was sorry that was impossible as they could not expect gentlemen to lend their pictures year after year. But they could nave Saturday I night cencerts in that Rail during the winter seasons and for years to come, and from the way in which the Mayor , had sleek ni to him about it, he thought and hoped it , would be accomplish,,d. He wished to make re erence ' to the criticism or cenierniiation of the Art Cenrnittee I for opening the exhibition last Sunday atternoen. He I did not care for condemnation of that sort. It was only I narrow-minded men Who collet say it was no benefit fol. people when they could receive instruction from slice an exhibition as that on a Sunday af :ernoan. It was :nisch better in his opinion, and he w.s sure it was in theirs, that people should be there than spend. jug their after-mane and evenings in pub:ic houses. (Bear, hear). Ha begged to move that their beat thanks ' be given to his friend Mr. Alfred Woods for his exertions lin the exhibition. (Applause). I Councillor BENNETT said tie had been a•kel to second , I the proposition. He did not think they woeld have had the exhibition without the help of their friend Mr. Woods. The result had been remarkably successful. 'They must not think of the mere monetary loss. Tn. immense amount of pleasure rec load Lad been sufficient return. It seemed to him, that in a town like War- rington devot-d to large industrial pursuits, they should not forget the higher things of life. Be hoped another result would be the raising of a spirit of emulation f erim viewing the works of local artists like Mr. Henry Woods and Mr. Luke Fades. The vote of thanks was carried with 'acclarna:io i. Councillor WODDS, in reply, said lie felt lik a member ef Parliament who has just been returned. It was very gratifying to him and Mr. Croefiell and other members of the committee that their efforts had been such a huge success. They had provided in Parr Hall one of the best orchestras travelling in the country, the Probyn tellies' Orchestra. The only loss they would hays was the cost of the installation of the electric light plant. Another large item was the insurances on the pictures. He thought if they got out of the exhibition by only spending £2OO of the ratepayers' money they would do very well. (Hear, hear.) They had had, not even ex- cepting the Royal Academy of London, the fineit exhibi- tion which had ever been brought together. That was c encensus of opinion from experts ; and not even I Liverpool could get up such an exhibition and give a I concert all fir ed. To give them an idea of the system I with which they had worked they had taken charge of 17,000 umbrellas. That had brought them in £26 clear : profit, and they had not lost one umbrsela. (Ap- plause.) Oaa thine only' had been lost and 1 that was a bracelet. Tney would all admit I that the last day had been as bright as ever. Be must say what a deep debt of gratitude they owed to Mr. I Holbrook Gasket!. • (Applause.) Be received a letter tram his brothe•-in-law, Mr. Like Fades, who sail they could not have had such a exhibition if it had not been for Mr. Gaskell. They had only had one refusal of any note and, thank God, they had b ieri able to do without it He thanked them heartily for the vote of thanks. What he had done, he had done for his native town. Appal se.)</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MARKETS. At he Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday new wheat (white) fetched 43 31 to 43 41 (701 b. to the bushel) ; red wheat, 33 91 to 3s 101 ; new oats, 3s 31 to 33 51. Flour, bipt seconds, per 2401 b, 21s to 22s 61. Bran, 100 to Hid per 20Ib. In the wovision markets, price 4 were as follows :—Fresh nutter, Is to Is 21 per lb ; fresh eggs, 7 for Is ; fowls, 43 64 to 5s per couple ; ducks, 54 to 5s 61 ; English beef, 5d to 101 : muttm, 61 to 9 d; veal, 61 to 101; perk, 61 to Bd.</p>
        <p>NRW BYRLAWEI FOR WARRINGTON. The following new byelaw applicable to the Borough of Warrington come into force on and after 14th November 189 S. 1. No person shall frequent and Use any stroet within the Borough either on behalf of himself or of any other person for the pur- pose of bookmaking, or betting, or wagering, or agreeing' to bet or wager win any person. 2. A person driving or having charge of any waggon, cart, or other carriage drawn by a horse or other animal, in any street within the Borough shall dur- ing the period between one hour after sunset and one hour before sunrise carry attached to such waggon, cart, or other carriage a lamp or lamps, which shall be so constructed and placed as to ex- hibit a light in the direction in which he is pro- ceeding, and so lighted and kept lighted as to afford adequate means of signalling the approach and position of such waggon, ca,rt.or other carriage. Provided that where one lamp only is so carried, it shall be attached to the off or right side of the waggon, cart, or other carriage ; and provided also that where such waggon, cart, or other Carriage is carrying timber, such person shall also carry a lamp or lamps, so constructed as to exhibit a light or lights visible to parsons overtaking such waggon cart, or other carriage. 3. Every person who shall Offend against any of the foregoing Byelaws shall be liable for every such offence to a penalty of five pounds.</p>
