        <p>On Thursday at the Warrington Occisional before Colonel Ridgway, William Glynn, a labourer of Turton-atreet, Golborns, was brought up on remand charged with causing tee death of Michael M'Atee. assistant barman, who resided at 15, Grundy-street. Golborne, by butting him in the stomach with his head, in the L-gh arms [dote', Golborne, at about 7-10 p.m. en the 22nd Octlber. Mr. H. J. Longton, Warrington, represonted tbe the prisoner, and Inspector Scott prosecuted on behalf Of the police.</p>
        <p>The •first witness called was Patrick Higgins, labourer, 15, Bradley-treet, Go!borne, who said he vraS in the Legh Arms Hotel on the 22ad o,tober. lee sof prisoner come from the back door along the !obey. Something pPssed between prisoner and Al'Atee, aud M'Atee litre: is hand if to strike prisoner. Prisoner butted M'Are. with his bead in the st arnacO, and be fell towards the front door. Witness did not think there was anything wrene all wont out at the back. He subsequenly saw M'Atee lying down in the billiard room, with acr iwd round him He could not say weather he was dead or not.—Mr. Longton : He wag brother-in-law of the dsceased. He did not think there was any it-feeling between prisoner and M'Atee. The deceased had laid a tray down before striking the' prisoner. He could not say whether they struggled together and " fluttered " past the doorway, as stated at the inquest. He did not think it was a serious affair as no threats were used. When be left the house about a quarter of an hour after the affair, he saw prisoner standing outside. James Steele, a drawer, living at 2, Mitchell-street, Golborne,said he was in the Legh Arms at 7 p.m. on the 22nd October. M'Atee ordere i prisoner out of the house. lie refused to go, and squared up to him. Witness got hold of prisoner and took him out. He afterwards came back, and saw prisoner and M'Atee struggling in thlt lobby. He separated them. When next he saw the dee' ceased he was in the back yard dead.—By Mr. Longton: M'Atee ordered prisoner to clear out in an excited man- ner. When he took Glynn out he did not hear him use any threat towards M'Atee. M'Atee did not say any. thing afterwards. He did aot think M'Atee was trying to nut prisoner out at the back door. William Parr, drawer, 3, Brook-street, Golborne, gave corroborative evidence, and added that prisoner said be would have the beer or he must fight. He then saw them scuffling in the lobby near the billiard room door. Wit- ness caught M'Atee in his arms,and with assistance car- ried him into the billiard roam. M'Atee luTiked as pale as death, and he the reht lie was dead. M'Atee waN sae aomisbdead:nrihdiaeunnestiatheri tehyi peen rsrki .issoni collier,ese urcvialhns2et nndwdd drink neettknowe—eki Bl.nisyt mrmelyeal, tart; Longton : r ?WA tee afterwards said he was in great pain 8.9.G1 brie I wrerigaei done for, fie hail been " topped" in the stomach. Witness. with John McMullen, went for Dr. Part. On coming back witness said to prisoner, who was in tbe Spinner's Arms "Dees tha know what tha's don.," and he replied " No." Wineose said " Theau's kilt Mick M'Atee." Prisoner re,olied "I only hit him twice."--21 Mr. Lengton : Ile thought there wag no real bad feel- ing between the two men previous to that day or on that day except when the beer was refused. John McMullen, painter, 79, Legh-street, Golbornet said he saw Glynn tighten his trousers belt and say "I'd do him one," and went round to the baek. The nest that witness saw was the struggling* M'Atee had Glynn'e head under his left arm, and prisoner's face wall nearly black, he being choking with the equeezing.-139 Mr. Longton : Witness haz irded the opinion that Glynrs would have been dead if they had not released bins from M'Atea's squeezing. It was a hard struggle tO e r tfinh edid t rnungtg lhe car o mdme ceenacseedd , s ayb uatn yr; hayi neeg told witness whet he had deposed teat dtt hoeaePyraaentaayametbeeto odtnhylvi ogarnehfati t M'Atee said. Witness did not regard Glynn's expression, •` I'll do him one," as a Berson. threat.</p>
        <p>Dr. Part. of Glhorn-i. said he was called to the Legb Arms Hotel, at 7-45 p.m. on the 22nd October. He found d al. lie made a post mortem examination no the body to,i fod ming day, and found there were slight rupture; c.f the hear: and two wounds in the liver, 0" of them being about an inch bag and half an inch deePt Death was due to. syncope consequent upon the shock or the injuries rel,nvad.—rly Mr. Lon.zt m : M'Atee wag a arm man, bigger than the prisoner. The injuries to tho liver caul I not have hien c aused by M'Atee having squenad prisoner's head into his aide. He did not thin the injuries from whi.7to NV:tree died could have bees causal by the squening of corm f areign substance against himself. If the blow came with sufficient force the injuri.a which the man died from could have been caused by the prisoner's head corning in contact wide M'Atee'a side.</p>
        <p>P.C. Wyll, ,t ttinnea at Golborne, said that ia cons&amp; rpeoco ot intoma-io . Ire3e3ivqd ho found prisoner on. Brook Side footpath at 7-45 p.m. on the 22ad October. He took him ta Gi',AtY)rne Policq Station, and he there el-Larval him with CitliinZ th , death of M'atee. He rB. Pli the I can ouly die ouce; he hit me once,and ; Itiittl'h'ilmilr'l. twice." Prisoner was the worse for drink ' when apprehended. Primer, will re4r•ro..l hi 4 eefenc 3at tr. sumstion , of Mr. Longroa, ole3dad guilty, and was committed tO Liverpool Ass.ze4 ors a chsrge of manslaughter.</p>
